+    { id: 30, slug: 'blankets-and-wine-2026-season', category: 'Events', title: 'Blankets & Wine Kampala: BNXN headlines a line-up built in Uganda', excerpt: 'The Kampala edition lands on 13 December at Millennium Grounds, Lugogo with BNXN at the top of the bill, joined by King Saha, Tai Dai, Aziz Azion, Tojo, Sean Offixial and Kyle Simbwa.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '6 min read',
+      img: 'images/articles/blankets-and-wine-kampala-poster.jpg', imgCaption: 'Blankets &amp; Wine Kampala save-the-date poster — supplied to 7H Music Blog', imgPos: 'center 40%',
+      author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR },
+      listen: { type: 'artist', id: '3zaDigUwjHvjOkSn0NDf9x', title: 'BNXN — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ng/artist/bnxn/1347142581',
+      body: '<p class="lead">Blankets &amp; Wine has named the bill for its Kampala edition: <strong>BNXN</strong> tops a seven-act line-up at <strong>Millennium Grounds, Lugogo</strong> on <strong>13 December 2026</strong>, with King Saha, Tai Dai, Aziz Azion, Tojo, Sean Offixial and Kyle Simbwa also confirmed.</p><p>The line-up went out on the festival’s Kampala channels on 15 October, with early-bird passes advertised at UGX 100,000. The save-the-date poster had already fixed the date and the venue; what it did not carry was a single name. That gap is now closed, and the bill reads less like whoever is trending this month and more like a cross-section of the last two years of Ugandan music — with one Nigerian headliner on top.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">The day, at a glance</h3><div class="factbox"><p class="factbox-title">Blankets &amp; Wine Kampala 2026</p><ul><li><span class="k">Date</span><span class="v">13 December 2026 · gates 12pm–12am</span></li><li><span class="k">Venue</span><span class="v">Millennium Grounds, Lugogo, Kampala</span></li><li><span class="k">Headliner</span><span class="v">BNXN</span></li><li><span class="k">Ugandan bill</span><span class="v">King Saha · Tai Dai · Aziz Azion · Tojo · Sean Offixial · Kyle Simbwa</span></li><li><span class="k">Music policy</span><span class="v">Etania · Selector Jay · DJ Ssese · DJ Dash · Fem DJ · City Girl · Josh MC · Viana Indi · Timothy Code · Zonke Africa</span></li><li><span class="k">Early bird</span><span class="v">UGX 100,000</span></li><li><span class="k">Tickets</span><span class="v"><a href="https://mookh.com/blanketswine" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-out>mookh.com/blanketswine</a></span></li><li><span class="k">Production</span><span class="v">Chama Nation</span></li></ul></div><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">Why BNXN</h3><p>BNXN — Daniel Etiese Benson, still Buju to half the internet — is the biggest name on the poster by distance. The Lagos-raised singer has roughly six million monthly listeners on Spotify, two albums in <em>Sincerely, Benson</em> (2023) and <em>CAPTAIN</em> (2025), and a 2026 that has not slowed: the five-track <em>The Game Needs Us</em> with Sarz in May, “Eja Meja” with Asake, and the six-song <em>Still In Charge</em> on 1 October. <a href="#article/bnxn-from-buju-to-kampala">The full profile is here</a>.</p><p>For a Kampala crowd the interesting part is not the numbers but the register: BNXN is a melody singer first, the kind of act who can hold a field at dusk without a hype man. A single regional headliner changes the shape of a night, and this one arrives in the middle of a release run rather than at the end of one.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">The Ugandan half of the bill</h3><p><strong>King Saha</strong> is the elder statesman here and, on current form, its surest bet: two sold-out Lugogo Cricket Oval nights in 2025, a full Kololo Airstrip in August 2026, and “Mpulira” out on 15 October. He headlined this festival’s 2019 Uganda edition; seven years later he returns with the deepest catalogue on the poster. <a href="#article/king-saha-from-leone-island-to-blankets-and-wine">Read the profile</a>.</p><p><strong>Tai Dai</strong> brings the new wave in its sharpest form — the five-track <em>WHET</em> EP, released in July under her own Tai Dai Official imprint, after years of being the guest on everybody else’s record. <a href="#article/tai-dai-whet-new-wave-spotlight">Read the spotlight</a>.</p><p><strong>Aziz Azion</strong> is the bridge to the 2010s: the guitar-led R&amp;B catalogue that began with “Nkumira Omukwano” in 2008 and has never really left rotation. <a href="#article/aziz-azion-guitar-emperor-at-blankets-and-wine">Read the profile</a>.</p><p><strong>Tojo</strong> and <strong>Sean Offixial</strong> are close friends and regular collaborators — the pair the younger half of the crowd knows from the same cluster of records — “In Da Bar”, “Big Spender”, “We Yu De”, “Wild Out” — a small circle that kept swapping verses until one of the songs travelled. <a href="#article/tojo-we-yu-de-to-blankets-and-wine">Tojo</a> · <a href="#article/sean-offixial-in-da-bar-to-blankets-and-wine">Sean Offixial</a>.</p><p><strong>Kyle Simbwa</strong> completes it: the Kasangati vocalist and producer whose <em>East African Summer</em> landed in August and who was named Apple Music’s Up Next artist for East Africa a month later. <a href="#article/kyle-simbwa-apple-music-up-next">Read the spotlight</a>.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">The music policy</h3><p>The live bill is only half the day. The DJ and host policy for the Kampala edition, as confirmed to 7H Music Group: <strong>Etania (Life of the Party)</strong>, <strong>Selector Jay</strong>, <strong>DJ Ssese</strong>, <strong>DJ Dash</strong>, <strong>Fem DJ</strong>, <strong>City Girl</strong>, <strong>Josh MC</strong>, <strong>Viana Indi</strong>, <strong>Timothy Code</strong> and <strong>Zonke Africa</strong>.</p><p>It is a broad cross-section of the city’s booths rather than one crew. Etania headlines the selector half: the DJ, MC and television personality sold out Lugogo Hockey Grounds in April with <em>Becoming Life of the Party</em>, the first major solo concert by a Ugandan woman DJ. Selector Jay brings more than twenty years of open-format work across Radio City, NTV and Guvnor. DJ Ssese reps Ssese Nation. DJ Dash, a Don Julio ambassador, is one of the hosts behind Useless Mondays. Fem DJ is the festival’s long-serving official DJ and the name behind the Fem Effect nights. City Girl, an NRG Radio presenter, is one of the busiest women on the club circuit.</p><p>The host line is just as deliberate. Josh MC, Viana Indi — NRG Radio’s evening host, formerly of NBS — and Timothy Code, the rapper and songwriter who has become one of the city’s most in-demand MCs, are there to hold a noon-to-midnight day together between live blocks. That is less compering than crowd architecture.</p><p>Zonke Africa complete the picture with a collective format built for long sets: Afro-house, amapiano and Afro-tech, with 2Nyuke and Mwami Kiyimba among the rotating cast. Set times and stage splits have not been published — but the policy already says the booth is not being treated as filler between bands.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">What the line-up is saying</h3><p>Read the seven names together and a thesis appears: a veteran (Saha), a two-decade R&amp;B career (Aziz), a new-wave rapper (Tai Dai), an Up Next vocalist (Simbwa), a crossover Afrobeats pair (Tojo and Offixial) and one continental star to close it. That is a more deliberate map of the scene than most Kampala festival bills manage, and it puts the old and the new in the same headline block rather than on separate nights.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">Tickets and the shape of the day</h3><p>Early bird is UGX 100,000 through <a href="https://mookh.com/blanketswine" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-out>mookh.com/blanketswine</a>. The festival runs from noon to midnight, which in practice means an afternoon of blankets-and-picnic sets before the headline block after dark; the poster names Chama Nation as producer. Bills move and tiers sell out — confirm the line-up and your ticket with the organiser before you travel.</p><p class="note">Sources: Blankets &amp; Wine Kampala’s line-up announcement of 15 October 2026 and the festival’s Kampala event page; artist catalogue details from Spotify, Apple Music and Deezer. Ticket availability has not been independently checked.</p>' },
+    // ---- Blankets & Wine Kampala line-up + artist files (17 October 2026) ----
+    { id: 65, slug: 'bnxn-from-buju-to-kampala', category: 'Interview', title: 'BNXN: from Buju to the Captain — and now a Kampala headline', excerpt: 'Six million monthly listeners, two albums, an EP with Sarz and a six-song project out this month: the Nigerian singer arrives at Blankets & Wine with the deepest catalogue on the bill.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '7 min read',
+      img: 'https://i.scdn.co/image/ab6761610000517454047830689ea245d14a390f', imgCaption: 'BNXN (photo: Spotify artist profile)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR },
+      listen: { type: 'artist', id: '3zaDigUwjHvjOkSn0NDf9x', title: 'BNXN — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ng/artist/bnxn/1347142581',
+      body: '<p class="lead">The name at the top of the Blankets &amp; Wine bill is not a Ugandan one, and that is the point. BNXN — Daniel Etiese Benson, born in Lagos in 1997, raised between Gbagada and Ogun State, family roots in Akwa Ibom — has spent the years since 2018 turning melody into a continental career.</p><p>He started as Buju, sang in church and school choirs, and broke through in 2018 with “Catch a Vibe” before “Spiritual” with Zlatan made him a name in 2019. A Headies Rookie of the Year nomination followed, then the sort of problem most artists never have: a stage name too close to a legend’s. In February 2022 Buju became BNXN — pronounced “Benson” — and by the end of that year he had the Headies Next Rated award and an EP, <em>Bad Since ’97</em>, that aged better than most of its peers.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">The album run</h3><p><em>Sincerely, Benson</em> arrived in October 2023: fifteen songs, Headie One and Popcaan among the guests, and a version of Afrobeats that leaned on R&amp;B songwriting rather than percussion. The collaborative record with Ruger, <em>RnB</em>, came in April 2024 — seven songs from two artists who had spent years being compared. Then <em>CAPTAIN</em> in July 2025: sixteen tracks, with Rema, Seyi Vibez and FOLA among the guests, and the record that moved him from featured voice to headline act.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">2026 has been the busiest year yet</h3><p><em>The Game Needs Us</em>, the five-track EP with producer Sarz, landed in May with “Back Outside”, “Already” and “Rum &amp; Soda” — a continuation of the pair’s work on “Gwagwalada” and “Pidgin &amp; English”. “Eja Meja” with Asake followed, and on 1 October the six-song <em>Still In Charge</em> closed the loop, with “If You Like”, “16” and “Shamaya” among them — all inside half an hour.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">What to expect at Lugogo</h3><p>BNXN is not a shouter. His sets lean on the catalogue’s soft edges — “Finesse”, “Very Soon”, “Pidgin &amp; English” — with the newer records slotted in where the tempo lifts. On a festival field at dusk that works: the songs are already known, the choruses are low enough for a crowd to carry, and the band does not need to fill space between them.</p><p>The booking also says something about how Blankets &amp; Wine is programming. The Kampala edition could have been filled entirely from the city; instead one continental name sits over six Ugandan acts, and the rest of the bill is built to make that name feel like part of the same night rather than a separate concert.</p><div class="factbox"><p class="factbox-title">BNXN at a glance</p><ul><li><span class="k">From</span><span class="v">Lagos, Nigeria</span></li><li><span class="k">Albums</span><span class="v"><em>Sincerely, Benson</em> (2023) · <em>CAPTAIN</em> (2025)</span></li><li><span class="k">2026</span><span class="v"><em>The Game Needs Us</em> with Sarz · <em>Still In Charge</em></span></li><li><span class="k">Award</span><span class="v">Headies Next Rated, 2022</span></li><li><span class="k">Kampala date</span><span class="v">13 December 2026, Blankets &amp; Wine</span></li></ul></div><p class="note">Sources: Spotify artist page and release metadata; Apple Music; Headies and press reporting. Monthly-listener figures move daily and are given as an approximate.</p>' },
+    { id: 66, slug: 'king-saha-from-leone-island-to-blankets-and-wine', category: 'Interview', title: 'King Saha: from Leone Island’s back row to the top of the bill', excerpt: 'Fifteen years after “Signal”, the Entebbe singer has two Lugogo sell-outs, a full Kololo night and a new single this month behind him. On 13 December he returns to Blankets & Wine.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '7 min read',
+      img: 'https://i.scdn.co/image/ab676161000051747a610e24a5808a82d5e50e6d', imgCaption: 'King Saha (photo: Spotify artist profile)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR },
+      listen: { type: 'artist', id: '3JQTIErs7TXtmo3HIv3yJa', title: 'King Saha — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/us/artist/king-saha/805432891',
+      body: '<p class="lead">King Saha has been on a Ugandan festival bill every year since most of this line-up was in school. On 13 December he returns to Blankets &amp; Wine — the festival he headlined in 2019 — and the case for him is no longer nostalgia.</p><p>He was born Ssemanda Manisul in Entebbe in 1989, released his first record, “Signal”, in 2011, and learned the trade from the back of a stage: a backup singer at Jose Chameleone’s Leone Island before he founded his own Kings Love Entertainment. “Mulirwana” in 2014 made him a household name, and the concert that followed at Freedom City on Entebbe Road was the first proof that a young singer from the lake could pull a crowd on his own name.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">The catalogue is the argument</h3><p>What separates Saha from the one-hit class is the length of the run: “Biri Biri” (2018), “On My Way”, “Zakayo”, “Gundeze”, “Kamwanyi”, “Very Well”, “Ebiseera Ebyo”, “Pretty Pretty”, “Nakyakala”, “Sala Pulesa”. These are songs that have been sung back at him across three phases of the industry, and they are why a Saha set can run forty minutes without a lull — as the Kololo crowd this August would tell you.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">Two Lugogo nights, then Kololo</h3><p>2025 was the year the argument ended. Saha sold out Lugogo Cricket Oval twice in the same year, the second night in November, and followed it in August 2026 by filling Kololo Airstrip — a show that was as much a statement about the central region’s live audience as it was a concert. He is booked for London in November. On Spotify his monthly listeners sit above 150,000, a figure that understates him locally, because his audience is a live audience first.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">Still releasing, quietly</h3><p>The 2026 file has been steady rather than loud: “Wewe” and “Batuleke” in June, “History” in July, “Eyali Owange” in August, “Buntu Butono II” on 30 September, and “Mpulira” — a love song, released on 15 October — two months before the festival. None of them needed a campaign; the catalogue does the work.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">Why he is on this bill</h3><p>Because Blankets &amp; Wine is a picnic before it is a rave, and Saha is the rare Ugandan act who can play both halves: the band-led, singalong afternoon and the headline block after dark. He did it at the 2019 Uganda edition alongside Shekhinah and Sammy Kasule. Seven years on he returns with the biggest domestic catalogue on the poster — and, on the evidence of the last eighteen months, the strongest live case too.</p><div class="factbox"><p class="factbox-title">King Saha at a glance</p><ul><li><span class="k">From</span><span class="v">Entebbe, Uganda</span></li><li><span class="k">Debut</span><span class="v">“Signal” (2011)</span></li><li><span class="k">Label</span><span class="v">Kings Love Entertainment</span></li><li><span class="k">Breakthrough</span><span class="v">“Mulirwana” (2014)</span></li><li><span class="k">Live</span><span class="v">Two sold-out Lugogo nights (2025) · Kololo Airstrip (2026)</span></li><li><span class="k">Latest</span><span class="v">“Mpulira”, 15 October 2026</span></li><li><span class="k">Kampala date</span><span class="v">13 December 2026, Blankets &amp; Wine</span></li></ul></div><p class="note">Sources: Spotify artist page and release metadata; Apple Music; Wikipedia; concert reporting from Eagle Online, Sqoop and Pulse Uganda. Listener figures are approximate.</p>' },
+    { id: 67, slug: 'aziz-azion-guitar-emperor-at-blankets-and-wine', category: 'Interview', title: 'Aziz Azion: the guitar emperor’s long game', excerpt: 'From a kadongo kamu band in 1998 to “Nkumira Omukwano”, Oxygen and a two-decade catalogue: the R&B guitarist joins the Blankets & Wine bill on 13 December.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '6 min read',
+      img: 'https://i.scdn.co/image/ab67616100005174203be93ee7c62907f8e70d12', imgCaption: 'Aziz Azion (photo: Spotify artist profile)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR },
+      listen: { type: 'artist', id: '5A4WNUoNE2OHg8hbn0wzDi', title: 'Aziz Azion — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/aziz-azion/980858222',
+      body: '<p class="lead">There is a version of Ugandan R&amp;B that begins with a guitar riff, and Aziz Azion has been playing it for twenty years. On 13 December the “guitar emperor” joins the Blankets &amp; Wine bill.</p><p>Aziz Mukisa was born in Fort Portal and raised in Kampala, and his route into the industry was the band circuit rather than the studio: he learned guitar in 1998 in a kadongo kamu group, spent time with Paulo Kafeero’s Kulabako Guitar Singers, passed through Kato Lubwama’s Diamond Productions, and ended up in Jeckaki Band, where he backed Jose Chameleone on tour. By the time he stepped forward as a singer he had already played on more records than most artists release.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">The breakthrough</h3><p>“Nkumira Omukwano” in 2008, produced by Benon Mugumbya, changed the size of the room. It was a Luganda love song with an R&amp;B spine and a guitar line people still hum, and it was followed by “Yegwe”, “Pain Killer”, “Beera Nange” and “Nakupenda” with GNL Zamba. The <em>Oxygen</em> album arrived in 2011, and the Pearl of Africa Music award for Best R&amp;B act followed the same year.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">A label, a band, a live reputation</h3><p>He built his own imprint, Shotyme Music, and kept the band at the centre of the operation. The clearest recent evidence is the 2023 Strings of Love concert: three sessions in one night, the Maestro band behind him, a set drawn from the whole catalogue — and Fresh Kid delivering GNL’s verse on “Nakupenda” to an audience that knew every word.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">Still moving</h3><p>The last three years have not been a victory lap. “Mataala” with Alien Skin in 2023 put him in a new conversation; “Oli Omu” got a Zouk Version in 2025; “Nyongera” and “Slow Motion” kept the melody-first template intact into 2026. Roughly 39,000 monthly listeners on Spotify is a modest number for a two-decade career, and a reminder that his audience listens on radio and at weddings as much as on streaming.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">Why he is on this bill</h3><p>Because a festival bill that only books the last eighteen months is a playlist, not a festival. Aziz connects the 2010s R&amp;B era to the new-wave line-up around him — and he is the act most likely to turn a picnic crowd into a chorus.</p><div class="factbox"><p class="factbox-title">Aziz Azion at a glance</p><ul><li><span class="k">From</span><span class="v">Fort Portal, raised in Kampala</span></li><li><span class="k">First instrument</span><span class="v">Guitar, 1998</span></li><li><span class="k">Breakthrough</span><span class="v">“Nkumira Omukwano” (2008)</span></li><li><span class="k">Album</span><span class="v"><em>Oxygen</em> (2011)</span></li><li><span class="k">Award</span><span class="v">PAM Best R&amp;B Artiste (2011)</span></li><li><span class="k">Label</span><span class="v">Shotyme Music</span></li><li><span class="k">Kampala date</span><span class="v">13 December 2026, Blankets &amp; Wine</span></li></ul></div><p class="note">Sources: Spotify artist page and release metadata; Apple Music; Wikipedia; The Observer’s review of Strings of Love; Flash Uganda Media. Listener figures are approximate.</p>' },
+    { id: 68, slug: 'tojo-we-yu-de-to-blankets-and-wine', category: 'Interview', title: 'Tojo: the “We Yu De” moment and what came after', excerpt: 'One December record turned into a crossover: Tojo 256 arrives at Blankets & Wine with “Big Spender”, “In Da Bar” and a spring single behind him.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '5 min read',
+      img: 'https://is1-ssl.mzstatic.com/image/thumb/Music211/v4/eb/e2/8c/ebe28c49-1b02-8161-8a52-bb09990857ed/artwork.jpg/600x600bb.jpg', imgCaption: '“We Yu De” — Tojo, CxJ Powell &amp; YUKO (official artwork)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR },
+      listen: { type: 'track', id: '0gYpScuZtIeoRLx2YTBlmK', title: 'We Yu De — Tojo, CxJ Powell & YUKO' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/tojo/1884330340',
+      body: '<p class="lead">“We Yu De” came out on 21 December 2025 and did what most Ugandan singles do not: it crossed over without changing its accent. Ten months later Tojo is on the Blankets &amp; Wine bill.</p><p>Tojo 256 records through One Groove; the credits on “We Yu De” list Mugema Paul among its writers. He came up in the same Kampala circle as CxJ Powell and Sean Offixial; he and Offixial are close friends and constant collaborators rather than rivals. The record that changed his year is a question rather than a statement: “We Yu De” translates roughly as “where are you?”, and with CxJ Powell and YUKO on the hook it became one of the scene’s bigger crossover records of the season — north of 200,000 Spotify plays and still in rotation on the strength of the melody rather than a campaign.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">The run behind it</h3><p>It was not a first attempt. “In Da Bar” — the record he and Sean Offixial made together — landed in April 2025; “Big Spender” followed with the two of them sharing the billing; then “We Yu De” in December closed the year. The pattern is a small, tight circle swapping verses until one of the records travels — and in 2026 the circle has kept moving: “Fall In Love” in May, “ACTIVE” with Offixial and CxJ Powell, and a guest spot on Shannon’s “Baddie” remix alongside Fik Fameica, AaronX and DJ Lito.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">What he does live</h3><p>Short songs, sung hooks, no dead air. The three-minute format that made “We Yu De” work on a phone screen is also the format that works at four in the afternoon on a festival field, which is roughly where the bill puts him: in the block where the crowd is standing rather than sitting.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">Why he is on this bill</h3><p>Because a festival needs the record the audience already knows before the headliner arrives, and “We Yu De” is that record in Kampala this year. It also gives the song a second life in front of a crowd that learned it in December — which is how a single becomes a catalogue.</p><div class="factbox"><p class="factbox-title">Tojo at a glance</p><ul><li><span class="k">From</span><span class="v">Kampala, Uganda</span></li><li><span class="k">Label</span><span class="v">One Groove</span></li><li><span class="k">Breakthrough</span><span class="v">“We Yu De”, 21 December 2025 (with CxJ Powell &amp; YUKO)</span></li><li><span class="k">Also on</span><span class="v">“In Da Bar” and “Big Spender” with Sean Offixial</span></li><li><span class="k">2026</span><span class="v">“Fall In Love” · “ACTIVE”</span></li><li><span class="k">Kampala date</span><span class="v">13 December 2026, Blankets &amp; Wine</span></li></ul></div><p class="note">Sources: Spotify and Apple Music release metadata; Deezer credits; Shazam; MBU’s February 2026 video round-up. Play counts are approximate and move daily.</p>' },
+    { id: 69, slug: 'sean-offixial-in-da-bar-to-blankets-and-wine', category: 'Interview', title: 'Sean Offixial: from “In Da Bar” to the Blankets & Wine stage', excerpt: 'The OMAB Music artist has spent two years turning verses into records — In Da Bar, Location, Marry Me, Wild Out — and now has a festival bill to match.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '5 min read',
+      img: 'https://i.scdn.co/image/ab676161000051741f23c5dc6cd5af447e192a3c', imgCaption: 'Sean Offixial (photo: Spotify artist profile)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR },
+      listen: { type: 'artist', id: '4ixj9ewwQZN7uZQMbtAJ6h', title: 'Sean Offixial — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/sean-offixial/1804189997',
+      body: '<p class="lead">Sean Offixial has spent two years turning guest verses into records of his own — and doing it beside the same small circle. The Blankets &amp; Wine bill is the first time a Kampala festival crowd gets him with his name on the stage card.</p><p>Born Lyadda Christopher Sean, he records through OMAB Music, and the catalogue reads like a shared notebook: “In Da Bar” in April 2025 — the record that made his name, cut with his close friend and frequent collaborator Tojo — then a Norst Wave remix that kept it alive through the year, “Location” with Mr.Tee, “Marry Me” with CxJ Powell, and “Big Spender” alongside Tojo again. Roughly 75,000 monthly listeners on Spotify is a lot for an artist with a handful of singles; it is also what happens when one song becomes a bar, a caption and a dance at once.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">2026: widening the circle</h3><p>This year the collaborations have gone sideways rather than safe: “ACTIVE” with Tojo and CxJ Powell, “WILD OUT” with CxJ Powell and Ledra in July, and “Tomaala” with a lyric visualiser in May. The pattern is the same one that produced “In Da Bar”: a small group of Kampala writers and singers trading verses until one of them sticks. Tojo and Offixial are friends first and collaborators second — they write together, share a circle of producers, and neither man’s success is treated in the camp as a separate story.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">What the booking means</h3><p>For an artist whose biggest records are collaborations, a festival slot is a different test. A crowd that knows “In Da Bar” from a club will give him the first thirty seconds; the rest of the set has to earn the next twenty minutes. That is the step between being on everybody’s song and being the reason people came — the step Tojo took with “We Yu De” and Tai Dai took with <em>WHET</em>, and one the two friends are taking side by side rather than in competition.</p><h3 class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold">Why he is on this bill</h3><p>Because he is part of the writing room that produced half the records on it. “In Da Bar”, “Big Spender” and “ACTIVE” are the connective tissue between Tojo’s set and the rest of the afternoon, and putting both names on the poster is the festival admitting that.</p><div class="factbox"><p class="factbox-title">Sean Offixial at a glance</p><ul><li><span class="k">From</span><span class="v">Kampala, Uganda</span></li><li><span class="k">Label</span><span class="v">OMAB Music</span></li><li><span class="k">Breakthrough</span><span class="v">“In Da Bar”, April 2025</span></li><li><span class="k">Also</span><span class="v">“Location” · “Marry Me” · “Big Spender” · “WILD OUT”</span></li><li><span class="k">2026</span><span class="v">“Tomaala” · “ACTIVE” · “WILD OUT”</span></li><li><span class="k">Kampala date</span><span class="v">13 December 2026, Blankets &amp; Wine</span></li></ul></div><p class="note">Sources: Spotify and Apple Music release metadata; Deezer and Shazam credits; artist’s own announcements. Listener figures are approximate.</p>' },
+    // ---- @smartist.u artist management toolkit (6 slides supplied 17 October 2026) ----
+    { id: 61, slug: 'artist-management-toolkit-125-templates', category: 'Industry Insights', title: 'The artist management toolkit: 125 templates promised, 89 listed, five kits explained', excerpt: 'A six-slide carousel from @smartist.u sells a 125-template artist management toolkit. The contents pages add up to 89 named items — and they are a useful syllabus for anyone managing an artist in Kampala.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '8 min read', img: 'images/articles/smartist-toolkit-cover.jpg', imgPos: 'center 50%', imgCaption: 'The "comment 125" slide from the @smartist.u toolkit carousel', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '4DA5LBW4NWzVLcVSOrBLsI', title: 'Mike 1ne — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/us/artist/mike-1ne/1840503931',
+      body: '<p class=\"lead\">A six-slide carousel from <strong>@smartist.u</strong> is selling a 125-template artist management toolkit. The contents pages are the useful part.</p>\n<p>The offer is simple: comment \"125\" and the files arrive. We have not seen the templates themselves, so this is not a review of them. What we do have is a syllabus — five kits covering startup, money, touring, recording and marketing — and it is a good map of what managing an artist actually involves.</p>\n<div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">The toolkit, as listed</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Account</span><span class=\"v\">@smartist.u</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Site</span><span class=\"v\">smartistu.com</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Kits</span><span class=\"v\">5 — Startup · Money · Touring · Recording · Marketing</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Promised</span><span class=\"v\">125 templates</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Listed</span><span class=\"v\">89 named items, counting the Startup Kit\'s \"+10 more\" as ten</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Delivery</span><span class=\"v\">Comment \"125\" on the post</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Seen by us</span><span class=\"v\">The six contents/CTA slides only</span></li></ul></div>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Five kits, five jobs</h3>\n<p>The Startup Kit covers the relationship and the company: the management agreement, an on-boarding checklist, a contact directory, a business plan, a task audit, competition analysis and a trends questionnaire. The Money Kit is the financial filing cabinet. The Touring Kit is the road. The Recording Kit is the release and the rights around it. The Marketing Kit is the audience — release plans, radio, playlists and press.</p>\n<p>Read as a whole, the five kits describe a real job. Most artist management in Kampala is done informally, by a friend with a phone and good intentions. The toolkit\'s value is that it names the documents that friend is missing.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Startup: the relationship, in writing</h3>\n<p>The first item is the one that matters most: an <strong>artist management agreement</strong>. Commission, term, duties, who pays for what, and how either side exits. The second is an <strong>on-boarding checklist</strong> — the assets, logins, catalogue, calendar and contacts a manager needs in the first month.</p>\n<p>The rest of the kit is the part people skip: a contact directory (labels, publishers, promoters, radio, DSPs), a music business plan, a task audit, competition analysis and an industry trends questionnaire. None of it is creative work. All of it is what separates a manager from a fan with a car.</p><figure class=\"portrait\"><img src=\"images/articles/smartist-startup-kit.jpg\" alt=\"The Startup Kit contents page\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The Startup Kit contents page. <em>Artwork: @smartist.u</em></figcaption></figure>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Money: the kit that decides whether you last</h3>\n<p>Sixteen items — profit and loss, daily bookkeeping, invoices, cashflow, royalties, revenue streams, a service record, personal and business budgets. It is the least glamorous page in the carousel and the one that decides whether the relationship survives its first bad quarter. <a href=\"#article/artist-management-money-kit\">We have taken the Money Kit apart separately</a>.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Recording: the release is a file, not an upload</h3>\n<p>Eighteen items cover the release and the rights around it: album budget, master metadata, ISRC and UPC codes, label copy, distribution, co-writer splits, publishing administration, producer and sync agreements, and guides to labels, publishers and music supervisors. This is where a record becomes an asset with a paper trail. <a href=\"#article/artist-management-recording-kit\">The full Recording Kit is here</a>.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Touring: a logistics company that happens to play music</h3>\n<p>Nineteen items, from the booking advance sheet and promoter agreement to the run sheet, stage plot, input list, tour budget, personnel doc and merch register. A tour is not a series of shows; it is a small logistics business with a band in it. <a href=\"#article/artist-management-touring-kit\">The Touring Kit, item by item</a>.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Marketing: the part everyone mistakes for the whole job</h3>\n<p>Eighteen items: album and single release plans, a release timeline, a marketing forecast, radio and playlist target sheets, a one sheet, a social calendar, audience reports and guides to media, publicists and radio programmers. It is the kit most artists think management is. It is the fourth most important of the five.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The numbers on the slides</h3>\n<p>The five contents pages name <strong>89 items</strong> between them, counting the Startup Kit\'s \"+10 more\" as ten. The call-to-action slide promises <strong>125 templates</strong>. Where the other 36 live is not shown on the slides — presumably in the files themselves, or in kits the carousel does not list.</p>\n<p>That is not a scandal; contents pages are highlights. But it is worth knowing what you are actually being shown. The slides also do not state a price, a file format, or what happens after the comment. Those are the questions to ask before you send your details.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Two things to know before you comment \"125\"</h3>\n<p><strong>1. It is a Canadian toolkit.</strong> One Money Kit item is \"Grant funding for Canadians\"; the Recording Kit includes a CANCON and MAPL guide, which is Canadian content certification for radio. Those pages will not transfer to Uganda. The structure will; the specific programmes will not.</p>\n<p><strong>2. A template is not advice.</strong> A management agreement, a split sheet and a promoter agreement are legal documents. A template gives you the shape; a lawyer gives you the version that holds up in your jurisdiction. Budget for that review before you sign anything with an artist.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The five documents to keep updated</h3>\n<p>If 125 templates is too many to start with, start with five: the management agreement, the master metadata sheet, the co-writer split sheet, the monthly profit and loss, and the run sheet. Those five cover the relationship, the catalogue, the rights, the money and the show. Add the rest as the operation grows.</p>\n<p>The number on the slide is marketing. The number that matters is the number of documents you will actually maintain.</p>\n<p class=\"note\">Source: the six slides supplied on 17 October 2026 from @smartist.u / smartistu.com; item counts are ours, from the lists as printed. We have not seen or tested the templates. This is not legal, tax or financial advice. <em>Credit: @smartist.u.</em></p>' },
+    { id: 62, slug: 'artist-management-money-kit', category: 'Industry Insights', title: 'The money kit: royalties, cashflow and the records an artist manager cannot improvise', excerpt: 'Sixteen templates for the financial side of management — from a daily bookkeeping sheet to a service record that protects your commission. What each one is for, and where the Canadian toolkit needs a Ugandan rewrite.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: 'images/articles/smartist-money-kit.jpg', imgPos: 'center 50%', imgCaption: 'The Money Kit contents page (artwork: @smartist.u)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '0tVbW89Gmk8xqxAuc06c3k', title: 'Echoled — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/echoled/1716201210',
+      body: '<p class=\"lead\">The Money Kit is the least glamorous page in the carousel and the one that decides whether the management relationship survives its first bad quarter.</p>\n<p>Sixteen items, grouped roughly into business records, artist money, personal money and start-up costs. None of them requires accounting software; all of them require the discipline to fill them in on a bad week as well as a good one.</p>\n<div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">The Money Kit, as listed</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Business records</span><span class=\"v\">Annual profit &amp; loss · daily bookkeeping · invoice template · monthly revenue · cashflow tracking · new business opening balance · monthly expenses · startup expenses</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Artist money</span><span class=\"v\">Artist finances · royalties 101 · revenue streams checklist · service record · grant funding for Canadians</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Personal money</span><span class=\"v\">Personal budget · personal net worth sheet · personal expenses</span></li></ul></div>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The three numbers a manager must know</h3>\n<p>Every management relationship comes down to three questions: what came in, what went out, and what is owed. <strong>Daily bookkeeping</strong> answers the first two at the level of the individual transaction. <strong>Monthly revenue</strong> and the <strong>annual profit and loss</strong> turn those transactions into a picture. <strong>Cashflow</strong> tells you whether the picture is affordable this month.</p>\n<p>If you keep only one page, keep the cashflow. A profitable year can still bounce a supplier payment in a slow month, and a manager who cannot see that coming is managing by surprise.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Royalties are not one number</h3>\n<p>The kit includes a <strong>Royalties 101</strong> guide and a <strong>revenue streams checklist</strong> because royalties arrive from several different places: mechanical royalties from reproductions, performance royalties from plays and broadcasts, neighbouring rights for performers and master owners, streaming income, and sync fees when a song is used with picture. In Uganda, collection is fragmented and often slow; the collection society (UPRS) is one route, not the whole map.</p>\n<p>The practical move is to keep your own statement. Every release, every writer split, every registration number, every payment received. When a society or distributor asks a question three years later, that file is the answer.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The service record protects your commission</h3>\n<p>A <strong>service record template</strong> is the document most managers do not keep and most disputes turn on. It logs what you did, when, and for which artist: the booking you chased, the release you project-managed, the radio run, the brand meeting. If your agreement pays a percentage, the service record is how you justify it — and how the artist sees what they are paying for.</p>\n<p>It also protects the artist. A manager who can show the work is easier to trust than one who asks for 20% and disappears.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Cashflow is not profit</h3>\n<p>Deposits arrive before shows; balances arrive after. Royalties arrive quarterly, if at all. A tour can be profitable on paper and still run out of money in the middle of the month. The <strong>tour budget</strong> belongs in this kit too, even though the carousel files it under Touring: per diems, transport, accommodation, venue deposits and the merch float all hit the same account.</p>\n<p>Keep a simple rule: every commitment over a set amount gets written down before it is made, and every payment gets a receipt. The receipt is not bureaucracy; it is the only proof you will have when the memory of the night has faded.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The Canadian line</h3>\n<p>\"Grant funding for Canadians\" is the item that tells you where the toolkit was built. There is no Ugandan equivalent hiding in the file. The principle still applies: grant funding is a revenue stream, and it needs its own calendar, eligibility file and reporting. If you are going to chase it, build the local version — the funder, the deadline, the documents, the report — and keep it in the same folder as the invoices.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Personal money is in the same file</h3>\n<p>The kit includes a personal budget, a personal net worth sheet and a personal expenses template. That is not padding. Managers who mix their own money with the artist\'s money lose track of both, and the artist usually finds out at the worst possible moment.</p>\n<p>Separate accounts are the minimum. A personal net worth sheet is the longer game: it tells you whether the management business is actually building anything for you, or just keeping you busy.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Build the file in a week</h3>\n<p><strong>Day 1:</strong> open a separate business account or wallet. <strong>Day 2:</strong> list every revenue stream, including the ones that have paid nothing yet. <strong>Day 3:</strong> build the invoice and service record templates. <strong>Day 4:</strong> start daily bookkeeping with last month\'s transactions. <strong>Day 5:</strong> build the cashflow sheet for the next 90 days. <strong>Day 6:</strong> reconcile the artist\'s royalties and registrations. <strong>Day 7:</strong> review it with the artist.</p>\n<p>Seven days will not make you an accountant. It will make you the manager who knows where the money is.</p>\n<p class=\"note\">Source: the Money Kit slide supplied on 17 October 2026 from @smartist.u / smartistu.com. Not financial or tax advice — confirm treatment with a qualified accountant and, for Uganda, with URA. <em>Credit: @smartist.u.</em></p>' },
+    { id: 63, slug: 'artist-management-recording-kit', category: 'Industry Insights', title: 'The release file: metadata, splits and the paperwork between the studio and the playlist', excerpt: 'The Recording Kit lists 18 documents — ISRC and UPC codes, label copy, split sheets, publishing and sync agreements. Here is what each one protects, and why the marketing plan belongs in the same folder.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: 'images/articles/smartist-recording-kit.jpg', imgPos: 'center 50%', imgCaption: 'The Recording Kit contents page (artwork: @smartist.u)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '4cd1RhZnbFFkmPgY4rmj7R', title: 'Quency — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/us/artist/quency/1587395509',
+      body: '<p class=\"lead\">The Recording Kit is where the art becomes an asset: 18 items from the album budget to the music supervisor guide.</p>\n<p>Most of the work happens before the release button. The kit is a reminder that a record needs a budget, a code, a credit line, a split sheet and a distribution plan long before it needs a playlist pitch.</p>\n<div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">The Recording Kit, as listed</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Money</span><span class=\"v\">Album budget · shipping cost matrix</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Metadata</span><span class=\"v\">Song master metadata sheet · ISRC &amp; UPC code guide · label copy template · lyric sheet template</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Release</span><span class=\"v\">New release checklist · distribution guide · the artist cycle (diagram/guide)</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Rights</span><span class=\"v\">Co-writer split sheet · publishing admin agreement · sync licensing agreements · producer agreements</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Guides</span><span class=\"v\">CANCON &amp; MAPL · Luminate (SoundScan) · record labels &amp; A&amp;R 101 · publishers 101 · music supervisors 101</span></li></ul></div>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Metadata is the record\'s passport</h3>\n<p>An <strong>ISRC</strong> identifies the recording; a <strong>UPC</strong> identifies the product. Get them wrong, or fail to embed them, and the royalties can land in the wrong account or nowhere at all. The <strong>master metadata sheet</strong> is the single source of truth: title, artist, featured artists, writers, producers, ISRC, UPC, release date, label, copyright line and territory.</p>\n<p>The <strong>label copy template</strong> and <strong>lyric sheet</strong> sit in the same folder because they answer the same question: what exactly is this record, and who made it? Streaming platforms, collection societies and sync buyers all ask versions of that question.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Splits before the session, not after</h3>\n<p>A <strong>co-writer split sheet</strong> is a one-page document that says who wrote what and who owns which percentage. Fill it in on the day the song is written, sign it, and keep a copy. The alternative is the sentence every independent artist has heard: <em>we will sort the split later</em>. <a href=\"#article/buzzmusicia-unhinged-advice-independent-artists\">We wrote about that sentence here</a>; this is the document that prevents it.</p>\n<p>The same logic applies to the <strong>producer agreement</strong>. A beat is not a favour, and a percentage is not a vibe. Put the ownership, the advance, the royalty and the credit in writing.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The release checklist and the artist cycle</h3>\n<p>The <strong>new release checklist</strong> is the operational spine: master delivered, metadata approved, artwork, ISRC/UPC, distribution, pre-save, release date, marketing assets, playlist targets, radio servicing, content calendar. The <strong>artist cycle</strong> diagram is the longer version — the rhythm of write, record, release, promote, tour, repeat.</p>\n<p>The <strong>distribution guide</strong> and <strong>shipping cost matrix</strong> are the unglamorous logistics. If you are making physical copies, the shipping matrix is the difference between a souvenir and a loss.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The rights stack</h3>\n<p>Four agreements do most of the work: a <strong>publishing administration agreement</strong> (who collects your composition income), a <strong>producer agreement</strong> (who owns the master and who gets paid), a <strong>sync licensing agreement</strong> (what happens when a film or advert wants the song) and the <strong>co-writer split sheet</strong>. None of them should be signed without a lawyer, and none of them should be left until the song is a hit.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">CANCON is Canadian; the principle is not</h3>\n<p>The kit includes a <strong>CANCON and MAPL</strong> guide — Canada\'s content certification for radio, which asks whether the music, artist, production and lyrics are Canadian. It has no legal force in Uganda. The principle is still useful: broadcasters everywhere have content rules and reporting requirements, and a manager who understands the local version can plan a radio run instead of hoping.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The guides</h3>\n<p><strong>Luminate (SoundScan)</strong> is the American sales and streaming data service; it matters if you are pitching to a US label or distributor. <strong>Record labels and A&amp;R 101</strong>, <strong>publishers 101</strong> and <strong>music supervisors 101</strong> are exactly what they sound like: a map of the three rooms a manager will eventually be in. Read them before the meeting, not after.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The Marketing Kit is the second half</h3>\n<p>The carousel separates recording from marketing, but the release is one project. The Marketing Kit supplies the other half: an album release timeline, single and album release plans, a marketing forecast, streaming playlist target sheets, a one sheet, a social calendar, radio formats and guides to media, publicists and radio programmers.</p><figure class=\"portrait\"><img src=\"images/articles/smartist-marketing-kit.jpg\" alt=\"The Marketing Kit contents page\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The Marketing Kit contents page. <em>Artwork: @smartist.u</em></figcaption></figure>\n<p>One caveat: the two release-plan examples are labelled Bieber and Rihanna. Those are major-label campaigns with major-label budgets. Read them for structure — the sequence of announcement, single, video, press, radio, playlist — not for scale. A Kampala release plan is the same sequence with smaller numbers and fewer people.</p>\n<p>The recording is finished when the file is delivered. The release is finished when the metadata, the splits, the rights and the campaign all line up.</p>\n<p class=\"note\">Source: the Recording Kit and Marketing Kit slides supplied on 17 October 2026 from @smartist.u / smartistu.com. Not legal advice; have agreements reviewed by a qualified lawyer. <em>Credit: @smartist.u.</em></p>' },
+    { id: 64, slug: 'artist-management-touring-kit', category: 'Industry Insights', title: 'The touring file: 19 documents between the booking and the merch table', excerpt: 'Advance sheets, promoter agreements, run sheets, stage plots, tour budgets and a merch register. The Touring Kit is a logistics manual for the road — and a checklist for a first regional tour.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: 'images/articles/smartist-touring-kit.jpg', imgPos: 'center 50%', imgCaption: 'The Touring Kit contents page (artwork: @smartist.u)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '3sjmAVaeka80SCvK69bedW', title: 'Joshua Baraka — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/joshua-baraka/1526660844',
+      body: '<p class=\"lead\">Nineteen documents for the road, from the booking advance to the merch register.</p>\n<p>The Touring Kit is the most operational page in the carousel. It assumes the tour is happening and asks whether you have the paperwork to survive it: the deal, the schedule, the stage, the money and the people.</p>\n<div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">The Touring Kit, as listed</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Booking</span><span class=\"v\">Booking advance sheet · show booking email template · tour booking guide · booking agents 101 · talent buyers 101 · promoter agreement template</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Planning</span><span class=\"v\">DIY tour management guide · tour itinerary · tour budget · tour reference notes · asset inventory template</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Show day</span><span class=\"v\">Run sheet · stage plots &amp; input list · set list creating guide · personnel doc</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Merch</span><span class=\"v\">Merchandise revenue tracker · merch sales register</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">People</span><span class=\"v\">Tour managers 101 · email sign-up sheet</span></li></ul></div>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The advance sheet is the deal in one page</h3>\n<p>The <strong>booking advance sheet</strong> is the document that prevents the post-show argument. Fee, deposit, balance, expenses, accommodation, meals, transport, curfew, capacity, stage size, sound and lights, and who pays for what. Get it confirmed in writing before the date is announced.</p>\n<p>The <strong>show booking email template</strong> is the same idea at the start of the conversation: a short, specific ask that makes it easy for a promoter to say yes or no. The <strong>promoter agreement</strong> is the formal version. Cancellation, force majeure, payment schedule and marketing obligations all belong in it.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The run sheet and the stage plot</h3>\n<p>The <strong>run sheet</strong> is the minute-by-minute plan: load-in, soundcheck, doors, sets, curfew, gate close. The <strong>stage plot</strong> is the diagram of who stands where and what they need; the <strong>input list</strong> is the channel list for the sound engineer. Together they are the two documents a venue will actually use.</p>\n<p>The <strong>set list creating guide</strong> and the <strong>personnel doc</strong> are about the show itself: what gets played and who is on the road. The personnel doc should name every person travelling, their role, their passport details and their emergency contact. That is not admin for its own sake; it is what you need when something goes wrong at a border.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The money on the road</h3>\n<p>The <strong>tour budget</strong> is the plan; the <strong>merch revenue tracker</strong> and <strong>merch sales register</strong> are the reality. Merch is often the difference between a tour that breaks even and one that does not, especially for independent artists. Count the stock before you leave, record every sale, reconcile the cash at the end of each night, and keep the float separate from the takings.</p>\n<p>The <strong>asset inventory template</strong> is the other half of the money file: every instrument, laptop, camera and piece of gear, with serial numbers and a replacement value. If it travels, it can disappear.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The people file</h3>\n<p>The kit includes guides to <strong>booking agents</strong>, <strong>talent buyers</strong> and <strong>tour managers</strong>, plus an <strong>email sign-up sheet</strong>. The first three describe the roles a manager will negotiate with; the last one builds the audience you will sell to next time. A tour that collects no contacts is a tour you have to repeat from zero.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The regional tour</h3>\n<p>For a Kampala-based artist, the realistic first tour is regional: Nairobi, Kigali, Dar es Salaam, Addis Ababa, Lagos. The paperwork changes at each border. Check the work-permit and withholding-tax rules for every country before you announce the date, and build the cost of the permit into the budget. The East African Community makes movement easier for citizens; it does not automatically make a paid performance visa-free.</p>\n<p>The <strong>itinerary</strong> and <strong>tour reference notes</strong> are how you keep that straight: dates, venues, contacts, travel times, border crossings, per diems and the name of the person meeting you at each stop.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The DIY tour is a real tour</h3>\n<p>The kit calls it a <strong>DIY tour management guide</strong>, and that is the right frame. Most independent tours are DIY: the artist drives, the manager advances, the merch table is a friend with a bag. The documents do not make it corporate. They make it repeatable — and they make the next promoter more likely to answer the email.</p>\n<p>A tour is a logistics company that happens to play music. The kit is the company\'s filing cabinet.</p>\n<p class=\"note\">Source: the Touring Kit slide supplied on 17 October 2026 from @smartist.u / smartistu.com. Visa, permit and tax requirements vary by country; confirm them with the relevant immigration authority before announcing a date. <em>Credit: @smartist.u.</em></p>' },
