+    { id: 58, slug: 'yomex-paragon-rules-successful-event-brand', category: 'Industry Insights', title: 'Rules for a successful event brand: the playbook, and the Kampala version', excerpt: 'A 15-slide carousel from @yomex_paragon lays out eight rules for building an event brand. We read each one against the Kampala calendar — and added the rule the carousel left out.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '9 min read', img: 'images/articles/yomex-rules-cover.jpg', imgPos: 'center 45%', imgCaption: 'Cover of the @yomex_paragon carousel "Rules For A Successful Event Brand"', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '3JpbQORpcLAUy1WyKF1ETc', title: 'Karole Kasita — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/karole-kasita/1448305930',
+      body: '<p class=\"lead\">The best event advice rarely arrives as a press release. It arrives as a carousel: fifteen slides, eight numbered rules, and one line that does most of the work — <em>ideas don\'t build events. Execution does.</em></p>\n<p>The carousel is from <strong>@yomex_paragon</strong>, titled <em>Rules For A Successful Event Brand.</em> Fifteen slides reached us: the cover, rules <strong>1, 2, 3 and 5 to 9</strong>, and six ad slides inserted between them. Rule 4 was not in the set. We are not going to invent it. What follows is each rule as the slide puts it, what it asks of a promoter in Kampala, and where the advice stops.</p>\n<div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">The carousel, as supplied</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Account</span><span class=\"v\">@yomex_paragon</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Title</span><span class=\"v\">Rules For A Successful Event Brand.</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Line</span><span class=\"v\">\"Ideas don\'t build events. Execution does.\"</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Slides</span><span class=\"v\">15 — a cover, 8 numbered rules, 6 ad slides</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Missing</span><span class=\"v\">Rule 4 (not supplied)</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Ads</span><span class=\"v\">Six slides branded @shay.mizz</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Scope</span><span class=\"v\">Naming, marketing, production, sponsorship, post-show operations</span></li></ul></div>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">1. Pick the name. Pick the date. Start marketing.</h3>\n<p>The slide is blunt about urgency: don\'t overthink it, give yourself one to two months from idea to market, get it out there. Naming and dating are the two decisions everything else hangs from, and the two that are hardest to change later.</p>\n<p>Kampala\'s last quarter is the test. The <a href=\"#article/rolex-festival-2026-10th-edition\">Rolex Festival</a> is at Lugogo on 29 November, Jose Chameleone\'s \"Unlimited\" follows at the same ground on 4 December, <a href=\"#article/blankets-and-wine-2026-season\">Blankets &amp; Wine</a> is on 13 December, and the December club calendar fills in around them. A new event brand that lands in that pile without a reason to exist is not competing for attention; it is donating it. The crowded calendar is not an argument against a December date. It is an argument for choosing the week, the room and the reason deliberately.</p>\n<figure class=\"portrait\"><img src=\"images/articles/yomex-rule-1.jpg\" alt=\"Slide 1 of the carousel: pick the name, pick the date, start marketing\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>Slide 1: pick the name, pick the date, start marketing. <em>Artwork: @yomex_paragon</em></figcaption></figure>\n<p>One to two months is workable for a first room of 150 to 300 people. For anything with a stage, a headline act and a real gate, eight to twelve weeks is the more honest runway — and the marketing should start on the day the date is fixed, not the week the flyer is ready.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">2. Make marketing personal.</h3>\n<p>The slide says: <em>leverage everyone around you. Plead. Bribe. Treat. Your friends and their friends can be your first customers.</em> Read \"plead, bribe, treat\" as slide shorthand, not a procurement policy. The principle is that a first event is sold person by person. A boosted post reaches strangers; a direct message reaches someone who might actually leave the house.</p>\n<p>In Kampala that means the group chats, the office WhatsApp, the DJ who already has your crowd, the MC who can talk about the night on radio, the boda stage near the venue, the church and mosque networks, the gym. It means invitations written like invitations, not broadcast flyers. And it means giving early buyers a reason to bring one more person — a two-for-one, a table deal, a friend\'s ticket — because the second ticket is cheaper to sell than the first.</p>\n<p>The slide\'s \"friends and their friends\" line is the whole funnel in six words. Track who brought whom. The person who brought three people is your marketing department for the next edition.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">3. Don\'t get offended.</h3>\n<p>The slide asks: <em>they didn\'t come to the first? Or the second? That is an opportunity you can leverage. Keep inviting them.</em> A no is rarely about you. It is about money that week, distance, a competing event, a babysitter, a shift. The mistake is to treat the no as a verdict and stop asking.</p>\n<p>Keep a list. Invite again. Make the third ask easier than the first: a cheaper tier, a lift, a seat, a friend. The people who skip the first edition and come to the third are not late converts; they are the audience you built by not taking it personally. In practice this is a spreadsheet and a WhatsApp broadcast, not a vibe.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The missing fourth rule</h3>\n<p>Slide 4 was not in the set, so the playbook has a hole where a rule should be. If we were writing it, it would be this: <strong>price the event before you print it.</strong></p>\n<p>Naming and marketing are the visible half. The invisible half is a budget: venue, sound, lights, stage and roof, security, MC and DJs, artist fees, marketing, permits, staff, water, toilets, contingency. Then the break-even: how many tickets at what price, and what happens if you sell only 60% of them.</p>\n<p>That is the rule that stops a beautiful first edition from becoming a debt. The carousel\'s rule 6 — don\'t maximise profit yet — only makes sense if you already know what you are choosing not to make.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">5. Never go cheap on production.</h3>\n<p>The slide is short: <em>especially your first event. Go all out. It will be worth it.</em> Production is the part of the night the audience cannot forgive. A weak flyer is forgotten; a mic that cuts out is a story. The first edition sets the reputation the second edition has to sell.</p>\n<p>Going all out does not mean renting the most expensive rig in Kampala. It means hiring a sound engineer who has done a show this size, a console with a backup plan, a generator that has been tested under load, a stage that can take rain, security that knows the gate plan, and enough toilets and water. It means a site visit and a soundcheck, not a WhatsApp quote.</p>\n<figure class=\"portrait\"><img src=\"images/articles/yomex-rule-5.jpg\" alt=\"Slide 5 of the carousel: never go cheap on production\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>Slide 5: never go cheap on production. <em>Artwork: @yomex_paragon</em></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Three quotes, three references, and one question: show me a show you did that looked like the one I am trying to do. The supplier who answers that question properly is worth more than the one who is cheapest.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">6. Don\'t maximise profit yet.</h3>\n<p>The slide says: <em>invest in the experience. Build the brand. Build the community. Plant the seed first.</em> First editions are customer acquisition. The money you do not take out is spent on the things people talk about: a short queue, a clean gate, good sound, water, a host who keeps the night moving, a room that feels full even when it is not.</p>\n<p>But \"don\'t maximise profit\" is not \"don\'t budget\". Decide the investment before the show, cap what you are willing to lose, and spend it on the things that compound. The Rolex Festival did not become a ten-year brand by pretending its first edition was a payout. It became one by turning up every year.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">7. Don\'t wait for sponsors to come to you, go to all of them.</h3>\n<p>The slide calls the waiting game what it is: <em>\"sponsors will come after our great event\" is a myth. A great event doesn\'t automatically attract sponsors. Go knocking on every door and build valuable relationships.</em> This is the rule that separates promoters who last from promoters who have one good night.</p>\n<p>Sponsorship is a sales cycle, not a reward. Brands plan budgets in quarters and years; a great event in November does not create money in December. The ask needs a one-page deck: who is in the room, how many, what the brand gets — naming, activation space, content, PA mentions, a table — what the last edition delivered, and the date. Start three to six months out.</p>\n<p>Go to the brands that already sell to your audience: beverages, telecoms agents, boda and delivery apps, restaurants, clothing labels, banks. A local brand with a marketing manager you can actually meet will move faster than a multinational waiting on regional approval. And after the show, send the report. The sponsor who gets photos, numbers and a thank-you is the sponsor who renews.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">8. After a successful show, the work begins.</h3>\n<p>The slide lists the unglamorous part in four short lines: <em>build the systems. Accounting. Structure. Processes.</em> Then it lands the point: the event is over, the business is just beginning. The week after is where most event brands lose the next edition.</p>\n<p>Count the gate, pay the crew, file the receipts, back up the content, thank the people who came, write down what broke, and fix a date while the room still remembers you. <a href=\"#article/event-brand-after-the-show-kampala\">We have written the full post-show checklist separately</a>, because this is the slide that deserves its own piece.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">9. Ego is the enemy.</h3>\n<p>The highest-numbered slide in the set is the quietest: <em>stay grounded. Keep learning. Stay teachable. The bigger you get, the easier it is to get distracted.</em> A sold-out night is the most dangerous data point in the business. It feels like proof, but it is one night, one room, one crowd.</p>\n<p>The promoters who keep going are the ones who debrief honestly while everyone else is still celebrating: what worked, what almost failed, who saved the night, what will not be repeated. The warning about distraction is precise. The bigger the brand, the more offers arrive — venues, sponsors, side projects, someone else\'s event. The job is to keep doing the boring thing that made the first edition work.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">What the carousel leaves out</h3>\n<p>Eight rules is a good marketing playbook. It is not an operating manual. The gaps are the things that do not fit on a designed slide:</p>\n<ul class=\"winners\"><li><span class=\"k\">Permits</span><span class=\"v\">Check with the venue, KCCA and the relevant authorities before you announce. Requirements and timelines vary by venue and event type.</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Contracts</span><span class=\"v\">Artist deposits, cancellation, rider, payment schedule — in writing.</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Safety</span><span class=\"v\">Security plan, medical plan, insurance, and a named person responsible for each.</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Access</span><span class=\"v\">Step-free entry, seating, toilets and a quiet route for people who need them.</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Weather</span><span class=\"v\">A covered plan for an outdoor event in a rainy season.</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Data</span><span class=\"v\">Consent for the attendee list you are about to build — and a place to keep it.</span></li></ul>\n<p>None of that makes a good carousel. All of it decides whether there is a second edition.</p>\n<p>The line on the cover is right: <em>ideas don\'t build events. Execution does.</em> The eight rules are a decent test of whether you are building an event brand or just throwing a party. The missing fourth rule — and the operating manual around it — is where the work actually lives.</p>\n<p class=\"note\">Source: the @yomex_paragon carousel <em>Rules For A Successful Event Brand</em>, 15 slides supplied on 17 October 2026; rules quoted as written on the slides. The six ad slides in the carousel are branded @shay.mizz and are covered separately in <a href=\"#article/instagram-carousel-promotion-ads\">our read of the six ad slides</a>. <em>Credit: @yomex_paragon.</em></p>' },
+    { id: 59, slug: 'event-brand-after-the-show-kampala', category: 'Industry Insights', title: 'After the last song: the systems a Kampala event brand needs before the next show', excerpt: 'The eighth rule in @yomex_paragon\'s carousel is the one most promoters skip: accounting, structure and process. Here is what that looks like in the week after a Kampala show.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '5 min read', img: 'images/articles/yomex-after-the-show.jpg', imgPos: 'center 40%', imgCaption: 'The after-show crowd — from slide 8 of the @yomex_paragon carousel', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '3sjmAVaeka80SCvK69bedW', title: 'Joshua Baraka — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/joshua-baraka/1526660844',
+      body: '<p class=\"lead\">The most useful slide in the carousel is the least glamorous. <em>After a successful show, the work begins.</em> Accounting. Structure. Processes. The part nobody photographs.</p>\n<p>Most event brands in Kampala are built by people who became operators by accident. A DJ, an artist, a host, a promoter with a good room — and suddenly there are suppliers, staff, tickets, a gate and a bank account. The show is the visible skill. The week after is the business.</p>\n<div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">The post-show file</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Gate</span><span class=\"v\">Ticket stock, comps, online and cash sales, float</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Suppliers</span><span class=\"v\">Invoices, balances, deposits and delivery notes</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Crew</span><span class=\"v\">Fees, transport, meals, agreed amounts</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Money</span><span class=\"v\">One account or wallet, statements, receipts</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Content</span><span class=\"v\">Photos, video, permissions, sponsor deliverables</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Data</span><span class=\"v\">Attendee list and consent</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Debrief</span><span class=\"v\">Run sheet against what actually happened</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Next</span><span class=\"v\">Date, early bird, thank-you</span></li></ul></div>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Count the gate before you count the win</h3>\n<p>The first number is not attendance. It is reconciliation: how many tickets were issued, how many were scanned or torn, how many were comped, how much cash was in the float, and what the mobile money statement says. The gap between the room and the receipts is where most first-time promoters get their first unpleasant surprise.</p>\n<p>It is rarely a dramatic theft. It is comps nobody wrote down, a guest list that grew at the door, two people selling from the same book, a float that got used to buy ice. Use one ticketing method where you can. If you are taking cash, use numbered tickets and two people counting, and reconcile the same week, not the same month.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Pay the people who made it happen</h3>\n<p>Crew, sound, security, the MC, the DJs, the venue balance, the cleaners. Agree the amount in writing before the day, and pay on the day you said. The event business in Kampala is small and word travels at the speed of a group chat. The sound engineer who was paid late will remember it longer than the crowd remembers the show.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Put the money in one place</h3>\n<p>An event brand needs a bank account or a mobile money wallet that is only for the event. Every ticket, every supplier, every deposit goes through it. Keep the receipts. Download the statements. A simple spreadsheet with date, item, amount, paid to and receipt number is enough to start.</p>\n<p>This is also the file you will need for tax. Nothing here is tax advice, and the rules depend on how the business is registered — but the artists who get caught out are rarely the ones with clever accountants. They are the ones with no records at all. <a href=\"#article/buzzmusicia-tax-write-offs-independent-artists-uganda\">Our piece on the write-offs independent artists ask about</a> covers the categories; this is the paper trail behind them.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Write down how the night ran</h3>\n<p>Take the run sheet you planned and mark what actually happened: load-in, soundcheck, doors, each set, the curfew, the gate close. Note the supplier who arrived late, the cable that failed, the queue that did not move. Give each supplier a one-line score. This is the document you will read before the next edition, and it is worth more than the photos.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Capture the proof</h3>\n<p>Photos and video are not decoration; they are the sales material for the next event and the report a sponsor expects. Get a photographer or videographer who knows the room, brief them on the shots you need — gate, crowd, stage, sponsor branding, the host — and get consent where people are identifiable. Publish while the night is still in people\'s feeds.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Thank people properly — and ask for the next one</h3>\n<p>The attendee list is the most valuable thing you built. Thank them the next day. Send one good photo. Ask one question: what would make the next one better? Then give them the next date before the feeling fades. An early-bird ticket to the second edition, sold in the week after the first, is the cheapest marketing you will ever buy.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The ego trap</h3>\n<p>The ninth rule in the carousel is the one that protects all the others: ego is the enemy. A sold-out room can hide a broken budget, a supplier who will not work with you again, and a team that is exhausted. Celebrate the night, then read the numbers. The debrief is not a criticism of the show; it is the instruction manual for the next one.</p>\n<figure class=\"portrait\"><img src=\"images/articles/yomex-rule-9.jpg\" alt=\"Slide 9 of the carousel: ego is the enemy\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>Slide 9: ego is the enemy. <em>Artwork: @yomex_paragon</em></figcaption></figure>\n<p>A successful show is a receipt, not a trophy. The work after it — counting, paying, filing, thanking, writing it down — is what turns one good night into an event brand.</p>\n<p class=\"note\">Source: @yomex_paragon, <em>Rules For A Successful Event Brand</em>, slide 8 (quoted) and slide 9. The full playbook is covered in <a href=\"#article/yomex-paragon-rules-successful-event-brand\">our walkthrough of all eight rules</a>. Not tax or legal advice. <em>Credit: @yomex_paragon.</em></p>' },
+    { id: 60, slug: 'instagram-carousel-promotion-ads', category: 'Industry Insights', title: 'Six ad slides, five domains: how to read the promotion pitches inside an Instagram carousel', excerpt: 'The event advice in @yomex_paragon\'s carousel is free. Six of its fifteen slides are paid pitches for playlist and influencer services. What they claim, and what to ask before you pay.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: 'images/articles/yomex-promotion-ads-hero.jpg', imgPos: 'center 45%', imgCaption: 'One of the six ad slides supplied with the carousel (artwork: @shay.mizz)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '4cd1RhZnbFFkmPgY4rmj7R', title: 'Quency — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/us/artist/quency/1587395509',
+      body: '<p class=\"lead\">Six of the fifteen slides in the carousel are not advice. They are advertisements — branded <strong>@shay.mizz</strong>, pointing at five music-promotion domains.</p>\n<p>That is not a scandal. Carousels are a distribution channel, and the ads are how the account gets paid. But the rules and the pitches should be read separately. One is editorial advice; the other is a sales deck with a scroll-stopping design.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"images/articles/yomex-ad-slides.jpg\" alt=\"The six advertising slides supplied with the carousel, showing five promotion domains\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The six ad slides, as supplied: five domains and one teaser. <em>Artwork: @shay.mizz</em></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">The six ad slides</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Musosoup</span><span class=\"v\">musosoup.com — \"Submit your music once and pitch to multiple blogs, curators, TikTokers, and more with a money back guarantee\"</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">YouGrowPromo</span><span class=\"v\">yougrowpromo.com — \"Support for Spotify, TikTok, YouTube, and Instagram. With campaign manager and money back guarantee\"</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Teaser</span><span class=\"v\">\"Music labels use these secret sites to make their artists go viral\" — no domain named</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Spaceloud</span><span class=\"v\">spaceloud.com — \"Influencer promotion platform with thousands of social creators and analytics for campaign tracking\"</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Orion</span><span class=\"v\">orionpromotion.com — \"Expert playlist pitching, social and influencer campaigns, and real time analytics\"</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Moonstrive</span><span class=\"v\">moonstrivemedia.com — \"Organic Spotify and YouTube playlist promotion from SEO backed, high traffic playlists. No bots\"</span></li></ul></div>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">What the slides actually claim</h3>\n<p>Strip the design and the six slides are selling four things: a pitch to many outlets at once, campaign management across platforms, influencer or creator promotion, and playlist placement with analytics.</p>\n<p>The words doing the heavy lifting are the guarantees. \"Money back guarantee\" appears twice. \"No bots\" appears once. \"Organic\" appears once. \"Secret sites\" appears once, without a domain — it is a hook for the five that follow, not a claim you can check.</p>\n<p>None of those phrases means anything until the vendor defines it. A guarantee against what, measured how, refunded when? \"Organic\" according to whom? \"No bots\" for how long after the campaign ends?</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Why a money-back guarantee needs a contract</h3>\n<p>A guarantee is only as good as its terms. Before paying, ask for the refund policy in writing: what counts as delivery, what counts as failure, how long the campaign runs, who decides, and how long the refund takes. If the answer is a screenshot of a WhatsApp message, that is not a guarantee; it is a hope.</p>\n<p>The same applies to \"campaign manager\". Is that a person or a dashboard? How often will they report? What happens if a playlist is removed mid-campaign?</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Playlists are not all organic</h3>\n<p>Anyone can make a Spotify playlist. Editorial playlists — the ones with Spotify\'s own branding — cannot be bought, and no service can guarantee a place on one. When a vendor says \"high traffic\" or \"SEO backed\", ask for the playlist links and look at them: who follows it, what else is on it, whether the tracks have anything to do with each other, and whether the plays arrive in a suspiciously even pattern.</p>\n<p>Bot-driven streams are against the platforms\' rules, and the risk lands on the artist\'s release, not the vendor\'s invoice. A campaign that gets a song flagged is worse than no campaign. Ask how the vendor detects and excludes artificial traffic, and get the answer in writing.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The \"secret sites\" slide is a hook</h3>\n<p>There are no secret sites. The next five slides name the domains in public, and the same services are advertised publicly. The teaser exists to stop the scroll and make the list feel like insider knowledge. It is good copy. It is not evidence.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">What to ask before you pay</h3>\n<ul class=\"winners\"><li><span class=\"k\">Scope</span><span class=\"v\">What exactly is delivered, in what window, for what price?</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Links</span><span class=\"v\">Which playlists, creators or outlets — by name, not by category?</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Reporting</span><span class=\"v\">What will I see, how often, and is it raw data or a summary?</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Definitions</span><span class=\"v\">What do \"organic\", \"no bots\" and \"guaranteed\" mean in this contract?</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Refunds</span><span class=\"v\">What triggers one, who decides, and how long does it take?</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Contract</span><span class=\"v\">Cancellation, ownership of content, and liability if a platform removes the campaign</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Evidence</span><span class=\"v\">Two case studies from the last year, with the artist\'s permission</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Compliance</span><span class=\"v\">Does the campaign follow platform rules, and will you put that in writing?</span></li></ul>\n<p>Then start with one campaign, at the smallest size that gives you a real answer. Measure saves, follows and repeat listeners, not just streams. A spike that disappears in a week is not growth.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">What this means for the carousel\'s advice</h3>\n<p>The event rules stand on their own. The ads are a separate transaction, and the fact that an account gives good advice does not vet the services it sells. Treat a promotion pitch like any other supplier: brief it, contract it, and ask for the report.</p>\n<p>That is not cynicism. It is the same standard the carousel asks of promoters — execution over ideas, systems after the show, and no waiting for someone else to do the work.</p>\n<p class=\"note\">Source: the six ad slides supplied with the @yomex_paragon carousel, branded @shay.mizz; claims are quoted as written on the slides. 7H has not used or tested these services and receives no fee from any of them. This is not an endorsement. <em>Credit: @yomex_paragon; @shay.mizz.</em></p>' },
