+    { id: 57, slug: 'afrima-10th-edition-entries-open-2027', category: 'News', title: 'AFRIMA opens entries for its 10th edition — 40 categories, and a January deadline', excerpt: 'The All Africa Music Awards portal is open until 10 January 2027, with the ceremony in Lagos next May. What Ugandan independents need to know, and what East Africa did at the last edition.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: 'images/articles/afrima-10th-edition-entries.jpg', imgPos: 'center 50%', imgCaption: '10th AFRIMA call for entries (poster: AFRIMA / African Union)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '3sjmAVaeka80SCvK69bedW', title: 'Joshua Baraka — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/joshua-baraka/1526660844',
+      body: '<p class=\"lead\">The portal is open. AFRIMA, the All Africa Music Awards, is taking entries for its <strong>10th edition</strong> from <strong>11 October 2026</strong> until <strong>10 January 2027</strong> — three months, 40 categories, and no entry fee mentioned anywhere in the call.</p>\n<p>For Ugandan independents this is the widest continental door available: no nomination, no label backing, no invitation. You register on the AFRIMA website, fill the form accurately, paste working links to your work, and you are in the same screening process as the acts you hear on radio.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"images/articles/afrima-9th-edition-finale.jpg\" alt=\"Winners on stage at the 9th AFRIMA in Lagos\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>Winners on stage at the 9th AFRIMA in Lagos in January 2026. The 10th edition finale is in Lagos on 23 May 2027. <em>Photo: Legit.ng</em></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">10th AFRIMA — the entry file</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Portal opened</span><span class=\"v\">11 October 2026</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Closes</span><span class=\"v\">10 January 2027</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Where to submit</span><span class=\"v\"><a href=\"https://afrima.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">afrima.org</a> — website only, no email entries</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Categories</span><span class=\"v\">40 — regional and continental, across genres</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Eligible work</span><span class=\"v\">Produced or released 10 Aug 2025 → 10 Jan 2027</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Who can enter</span><span class=\"v\">Artistes, songwriters, producers, video directors, DJs, dancers &amp; choreographers, labels, executives, unrecorded artistes</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Ceremony</span><span class=\"v\">Lagos, Nigeria · 18–23 May 2027 (main show 23 May)</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Form languages</span><span class=\"v\">English, French, Swahili, Arabic, Portuguese (+ Spanish)</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Trophy</span><span class=\"v\">23.9-carat gold AFRIMA trophy</span></li></ul></div>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">What the tenth edition actually is</h3>\n<p>AFRIMA was founded in 2013 by its International Executive Committee in partnership with the <strong>African Union</strong>, and it has become the continent\'s longest-running music awards platform, broadcast into more than 84 countries. The 10th edition\'s calendar was unveiled on 20 September 2026 at a creative industry roundtable in Beverly Hills, Los Angeles; the entry window opened three weeks later.</p>\n<p>The numbers behind the call are the argument for entering. The 9th edition drew a record <strong>10,717 submissions</strong>, which a 13-member international jury reduced to 343 nominees across 40 categories. Those are long odds — but they are odds you can only take by submitting. The 9th edition ceremony ran in Lagos in January 2026; the tenth moves to May 2027, with the build-up spread across London, Windhoek, Cotonou, Nairobi, Accra, Paris, Warri and Port Harcourt under the banner #Road2AFRIMA10Xperience.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">What happened to East Africa last time</h3>\n<p>Tanzania\'s <strong>Juma Jux</strong> won Best Male Artiste in Eastern Africa at the 9th edition, beating a shortlist that included Kenya\'s Bien, Diamond Platnumz, Rwanda\'s Bruce Melodie and Element Eleéh, Burundi\'s Sat-B, Ethiopia\'s Yared Negu — and Uganda\'s <strong><a href=\"#article/joshua-baraka-tiwa-savage-commando\">Joshua Baraka</a></strong>, the only Ugandan in that category. Madagascar\'s Denise took Best Female Artiste in Eastern Africa. Uganda\'s <strong>Winnie Nwagi</strong> was also on the Eastern Africa female shortlist.</p>\n<p>Read that again: one Ugandan male nominee, one Ugandan female nominee. For a scene releasing at the rate Kampala does, that is not a talent problem — it is a submission problem. The jury can only shortlist what the portal receives.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Six things to get right before you submit</h3>\n<p><strong>1. Pick the right category.</strong> Forty categories across five regions and the genre awards means most artists have more than one possible home, and submitting in the wrong one is the most common self-inflicted exit. AFRIMA\'s own Eastern Africa juror, Kenya\'s Lucy Ilado, put it plainly when the portal opened: take time to understand the categories and submit where the work best fits.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Check your dates.</strong> The eligibility window is 10 August 2025 to 10 January 2027. A record released in July 2025 is out; anything from this year\'s run — including <a href=\"#article/review-kyle-simbwa-east-african-summer\">Kyle Simbwa\'s <em>East African Summer</em></a>, <a href=\"#article/review-mike-mungu-love-isnt-enough\">Mike Mungu\'s <em>LOVE ISNT ENOUGH</em></a> or <a href=\"#article/echoled-kush-after-hour\">Echoled\'s <em>Kush After Hour</em></a> — is squarely inside it.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Use links that work.</strong> Audio and video go in as downloadable or streamable links — YouTube and SoundCloud are the examples the organisers give. Test every link in a private browser window, logged out, on a phone. A dead link is an automatic exit.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Do not email it.</strong> Entries sent to any AFRIMA email address, or through any platform other than the website, will not be accepted. This is stated in every version of the guidelines.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Read the consent paragraph.</strong> The entry form includes a broad grant allowing AFRIMA to use your audio, video, image, likeness and lyrics for promotional and broadcast purposes, including on its channels and by media partners, without royalties or compensation. That is standard for awards submissions, but it is a grant you should read and understand before you tick the box — particularly if the record is signed to someone else or has a feature on it.</p>\n<p><strong>6. Submit early, not on 10 January.</strong> Three months sounds like a long window until the upload fails at 11pm on the deadline. The organisers\' own calendar has collation and pre-screening running 11–14 January, adjudication 15–22 January and nominees announced globally on 29 January — there is no grace period built into that schedule.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The Ugandan case for entering</h3>\n<p>This blog spends most of its time on records that will never appear on a continental stage, and that is fine — the scene does not exist to feed awards. But the cost of a submission here is an afternoon and a set of working links, and the asymmetry is real: a nominee slot puts a Ugandan independent in the same broadcast as the continent\'s biggest names, in front of a jury that has already shown it will shortlist East African work.</p>\n<p>The 9th edition proved the door opens. The tenth is open now. If you have a record from the last fourteen months that you believe in, the only way it loses is by not being in the pile. <a href=\"https://afrima.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Entries close 10 January 2027 at afrima.org</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"note\">Source: AFRIMA press release, 7 October 2026; AFRIMA call-for-entry guidelines; African Union; Pulse, Arise News, TheCable, BusinessDay and The Lagos Review coverage of the 10th edition call; winners lists for the 9th edition via Music In Africa, Channels TV and Punch. Entry criteria and the consent terms are as published by AFRIMA — check the current guidelines on afrima.org before submitting. <em>Credit: AFRIMA; African Union; Music In Africa.</em></p>' },
