+    { id: 54, slug: 'buzzmusicia-15-things-music-industry-uganda', category: 'Industry Insights', title: '15 things nobody tells you about working in music — the Kampala version', excerpt: 'An independent-music carousel asked the question this month. We only saw its cover, so here is our own answer: the fifteen things nobody tells a Ugandan independent artist, from metadata to the manager myth.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '8 min read', img: 'images/articles/buzzmusicia-15-things.jpg', imgPos: 'center 40%', imgCaption: 'Cover of @buzzmusicia\'s 6 October 2026 carousel', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '0tVbW89Gmk8xqxAuc06c3k', title: 'Echoled — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/echoled/1716201210',
+      body: '<p class=\"lead\">A carousel of career advice can be worth more than a press release. On 6 October, the independent-music account <strong>@buzzmusicia</strong> posted one titled <em>\"15 things nobody tells you about working in the music industry\"</em>, filed under its <strong>OFF THE RECORD</strong> banner. It drew 4,627 likes, 80 comments and 575 shares.</p>\n<p>We cannot tell you what is on slides two to sixteen — the screenshot we were sent shows only the cover, and the account\'s page did not load for us when we tried. What follows is not a summary of their list. It is the Ugandan version of the same conversation: the things nobody tells an independent artist in Kampala, written from what we have watched happen this year.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"images/articles/kampala-downtown.jpg\" alt=\"Downtown Kampala from above, with traffic and buildings\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>Downtown Kampala. <em>Photo: not not phil / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 2.0 (cropped)</em></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">The post we are responding to</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Account</span><span class=\"v\">@buzzmusicia (independent-music account, Los Angeles)</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Series</span><span class=\"v\">OFF THE RECORD</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Title</span><span class=\"v\">\"15 things nobody tells you about working in the music industry\"</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Posted</span><span class=\"v\">6 October 2026 · 1/16 slides</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Engagement</span><span class=\"v\">4,627 likes · 80 comments · 575 shares · 1,728 saves</span></li></ul></div>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">1. Nobody is coming to discover you</h3>\n<p>The deal that changes everything is mostly a story artists tell each other. In this market, the working model is a release every few weeks, a small budget, and a habit of telling people the song exists. The artists on our pages who have travelled furthest did not wait for a label to find them; they put out records consistently enough that the label conversation became optional.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">2. Your catalogue is the asset, not the single</h3>\n<p>A single is an announcement. A catalogue is an income stream. <a href=\"#article/echoled-kush-after-hour\">Echoled</a> went into his second EP with years of singles behind him — \"One Night Stand\", \"Broken Hearts\", \"Kintu\", \"Home Alone\" — so that <em>Kush After Hour</em> arrived with an audience already trained to press play. One song cannot carry a career; fifteen can carry a booking.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">3. Metadata is the job</h3>\n<p>Wrong credits, missing ISRCs and unregistered splits do not hurt on release day. They hurt two years later, when a sync request arrives and nobody can prove who owns what. The unglamorous spreadsheet is the difference between getting paid and explaining.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">4. Streaming pays per thousand, not per song</h3>\n<p>At Uganda\'s streaming penetration — around 1.1%, against a regional average of 5–10% — a hit on Spotify is not a salary. The revenue that changes an artist\'s year is live work and brand deals. Streaming is the shop window; the till is elsewhere.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">5. Radio still moves the room</h3>\n<p>Industry reporting keeps finding the same thing: artists who promote on radio get roughly twice the live-show opportunities of those who do not. A playlist add is a nice line in a bio. A radio add is a crowd on Saturday.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">6. The split conversation has to happen before the studio</h3>\n<p>Every collaboration on this site is credited. That is not a formatting preference. A feature without a written split becomes an argument the moment the song earns anything — and in Uganda, that argument can end up in court, as it has for bigger artists than most of the people reading this.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">7. The collaborators are the marketing</h3>\n<p>Look at how the scene actually travels. <a href=\"#article/tai-dai-whet-new-wave-spotlight\">Tai Dai</a> was on everybody else\'s record for three years before <em>WHET</em> turned the catalogue around to face her. <a href=\"#article/review-mike-mungu-love-isnt-enough\">Mike Mungu</a>\'s album carries five guests across four tracks. Being easy to work with is a distribution strategy.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">8. Nobody owes you a share</h3>\n<p>Shares are a favour, and they are also measurable now. Saves and sends matter more than likes, because they are the two signals that push a song into a stranger\'s feed. Ask for the save. Ask for the send. The like is a courtesy.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">9. The video is a second release, not a formality</h3>\n<p><a href=\"#article/peypah-know-you-visualiser\">Peypah\'s \"Know You\"</a> came out in May. The visualiser landed on 15 October, four and a half months later, and the song got a second life out of it. Treat the visual as a re-release with its own push, not as an afterthought shot in a corridor.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">10. Deadlines are the whole job</h3>\n<p>Delivery dates for DSPs, artwork specs, lyric sheets, the distributor\'s cut-off — miss one and the release moves a week. A week is a lifetime in a scene that puts out this much music. The people who look \"lucky\" are usually just early.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">11. Your phone is the label office</h3>\n<p>Inbox, contracts, splits, invoices, the distributor dashboard, the group chat with the videographer. Most independent careers here are run from one device. Back it up, and keep the passwords somewhere that is not that device.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">12. The business side is a skill you can learn</h3>\n<p>Copyright, neighbouring rights, mechanicals, advances against royalties — none of it is secret, and all of it is searchable. The artists who get taken advantage of are rarely the least talented. They are the ones who did not read the page.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">13. A manager is not a saviour</h3>\n<p>Management works when there is something to manage: a release calendar, a booking pipeline, a brand deck. Hiring a manager to \"find opportunities\" before any of that exists usually means paying someone to do the admin you have not started.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">14. Your city is not your ceiling</h3>\n<p>Kampala records are turning up on playlists in Accra and Nairobi. \"Know You\" reached Spotify\'s <em>Hot Hits Ghana 2026</em>; Kyle Simbwa\'s <em>East African Summer</em> was built for rooms outside Uganda. Make the song for home and the metadata for everywhere.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">15. The people who last are the ones who kept releasing</h3>\n<p>Not the ones with the biggest first week. Not the ones with the best cover art. The ones still putting records out three years later, with their splits written down and their catalogue intact. That is the entire trick, and it is mostly admin.</p>\n<p class=\"note\">Read as a response to the cover of @buzzmusicia\'s 6 October 2026 carousel. We have not reproduced or summarised its individual slides; the points above are 7H Music Blog\'s own, written for the Ugandan independent scene. <em>Credit: @buzzmusicia (prompting post, cover only); Atlas industry report via Pulse Uganda; Apple Music; Spotify.</em></p>' },
+    { id: 55, slug: 'buzzmusicia-unhinged-advice-independent-artists', category: 'Industry Insights', title: '\"We\'ll sort the split later\": 15 lines independent artists hear, and what they mean', excerpt: 'A viral carousel collected the unhinged things people say to independent artists. We only saw the cover, so here are the Ugandan equivalents — and the translation for each one.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '7 min read', img: 'images/articles/buzzmusicia-unhinged-things.jpg', imgPos: 'center 45%', imgCaption: 'Cover of @buzzmusicia\'s 28 September 2026 carousel', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '4cd1RhZnbFFkmPgY4rmj7R', title: 'Quency — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/us/artist/quency/1587395509',
+      body: '<p class=\"lead\">Some sentences stay with an artist for years. On 28 September, <strong>@buzzmusicia</strong> collected them under an <strong>OFF THE RECORD</strong> cover: <em>\"15 unhinged things people in the music industry have said to independent artists.\"</em> The post was marked <strong>(pt. 1)</strong>, ran to 16 slides, and drew 3,222 likes with 202 comments — a lot of people recognising something.</p>\n<p>Only the cover reached us, so this is not a report on their fifteen. It is the Ugandan version: the lines an independent artist in Kampala is likely to hear, and what each one actually means.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"images/articles/contract-signing.jpg\" alt=\"Two hands signing a printed contract with a pen\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>Signing on the dotted line. The clause nobody reads is usually the one that decides who owns the master. <em>Photo: Signature Pro / Unsplash</em></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">The post we are responding to</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Account</span><span class=\"v\">@buzzmusicia (independent-music account, Los Angeles)</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Series</span><span class=\"v\">OFF THE RECORD</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Title</span><span class=\"v\">\"15 unhinged things people in the music industry have said to independent artists\" (pt. 1)</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Posted</span><span class=\"v\">28 September 2026 · 1/16 slides</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Engagement</span><span class=\"v\">3,222 likes · 202 comments · 360 shares · 754 saves</span></li></ul></div>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">\"Let\'s do this one for exposure.\"</h3>\n<p>Translation: the person asking has a budget and it is not for you. Exposure is a real currency in exactly one situation — when the platform reaches an audience you cannot reach yourself, and you can name that audience in advance. A brand with money is not that situation. A festival slot with a paying gate can be.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">\"Send me the full song and I\'ll see what I can do.\"</h3>\n<p>Sometimes true, often a free download. The professional version of this is specific: which playlist, which radio show, which date, and what they need from you to place it. If the sentence has no noun in it, it is not an offer yet.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">\"We\'ll sort the split later.\"</h3>\n<p>Later never arrives with documents. Every feature on a record is a negotiation waiting to happen, and the only version that ends well is the one that starts with a message — or a one-page agreement — before the session. This is the sentence that has ended careers, not just friendships.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">\"You don\'t need a contract, we\'re family.\"</h3>\n<p>Families are the hardest people to sue and the easiest to be exploited by. A contract is not a sign of distrust; it is what lets the relationship survive the first disagreement about money. If someone resists a written split, that is the information.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">\"The advance is against your royalties — so it\'s basically free money.\"</h3>\n<p>It is not free. It is a loan repaid from your own income, usually with the label keeping ownership of the recording for the term. Ask what the recoupment looks like, how long the term runs, and what happens if the record earns nothing. Then ask again, in writing.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">\"We\'re taking it to the DSPs next week.\"</h3>\n<p>Distribution is not a favour and it is not a deal. Any independent artist can be on every major platform for a small annual fee. Anyone presenting distribution as the valuable part of a contract is selling you the thing that costs the least.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">\"Nobody\'s going to stream a Luganda record.\"</h3>\n<p>The scene\'s biggest records disagree, repeatedly. The language is not the constraint; the marketing is. If a record does not travel, the useful question is what happened to it after release week, not what language it was sung in.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">\"You\'re not ready for a headline slot.\"</h3>\n<p>Sometimes accurate, and worth hearing. The tell is whether the person saying it can tell you what \"ready\" would look like: which rooms, which numbers, which support slots. Without that, it is a way of keeping you on the undercard.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">\"We can\'t pay for the feature, but we\'ll tag you.\"</h3>\n<p>A tag is not a fee. If the collaboration is with a bigger artist and the reach genuinely moves your numbers, take the call — but know that you are trading a fee for marketing, and that the trade should be your decision, not a surprise.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">\"Send me your EPK, your press shots, your radio edits and a video — by Friday.\"</h3>\n<p>Legitimate, though rarely as urgent as it sounds. What is not legitimate is doing all of that work and then hearing nothing. Ask what the opportunity is before you spend the money on the shoot. The answer tells you whether it is worth the Friday.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">\"You should be grateful — I\'m giving you a platform.\"</h3>\n<p>A platform that needs you to be grateful is usually a platform that needs you more than it admits. Gratitude is for people who did something specific and useful. It is not an invoice.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">\"Just make what\'s trending.\"</h3>\n<p>The records that travel furthest in this scene tend to be the specific ones: <a href=\"#article/review-kyle-simbwa-east-african-summer\">Kyle Simbwa</a>\'s <em>East African Summer</em>, <a href=\"#article/echoled-kush-after-hour\">Echoled</a>\'s late-night EP, <a href=\"#article/review-mike-mungu-love-isnt-enough\">Mike Mungu</a>\'s album of duets. Trend-chasing produces the third-best version of somebody else\'s song.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">\"We\'ll take your masters as security.\"</h3>\n<p>Ownership of the recording is the asset. Whatever the paperwork says about recoupment, read the clause that names who owns the master and for how long. If you do not understand it, pay a lawyer for one hour — it is cheaper than the alternative, and considerably cheaper than the ten years.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">\"This is how it\'s always been done.\"</h3>\n<p>Usually said about a deal structure designed before streaming existed. The industry has changed faster in the last decade than in the previous three. \"Always\" is a reason to ask more questions, not fewer.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">\"Trust me.\"</h3>\n<p>The least useful sentence in the business. Trust is what a signed page, a paid invoice and a delivered release look like after the fact. With the rest of the list, it is a red flag with a smile.</p>\n<p class=\"note\">Read as a response to the cover of @buzzmusicia\'s 28 September 2026 carousel. We have not reproduced or summarised its individual slides; the quotes above are the lines we hear in the Ugandan scene, presented as 7H Music Blog\'s own commentary. <em>Credit: @buzzmusicia (prompting post, cover only); Atlas industry report via Pulse Uganda; 7H Music Blog reporting.</em></p>' },
+    { id: 56, slug: 'buzzmusicia-tax-write-offs-independent-artists-uganda', category: 'Industry Insights', title: '18 tax write-offs independent artists ask about — and the two questions that matter more', excerpt: 'A carousel on the deductions artists miss was saved 3,236 times. We only saw its cover, so here is the working Ugandan version: the categories, the evidence they need, and why none of it is tax advice.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '8 min read', img: 'images/articles/buzzmusicia-tax-writeoffs-hero.jpg', imgPos: 'center 40%', imgCaption: 'Cover of @buzzmusicia\'s 23 September 2026 carousel', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '4DA5LBW4NWzVLcVSOrBLsI', title: 'Mike 1ne — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/us/artist/mike-1ne/1840503931',
+      body: '<p class=\"lead\">\"18 tax write-offs independent artists could be missing before 2026 ends.\" That was the cover of a 23 September carousel from <strong>@buzzmusicia</strong>, filed under <strong>UNDER THE RADAR</strong>. It ran 20 slides and 1,358 likes, with 3,236 saves — the highest save-to-like ratio of the three posts we were sent, which tells you exactly what kind of post it is: people are keeping it for later.</p>\n<p>We only have the cover, so this is not their eighteen. It is the Ugandan working version of the same list — the categories independent artists here ask about most, what each one means, and the two questions that matter more than the list itself.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"images/articles/receipts-bookkeeping.jpg\" alt=\"A hand holding receipts over a calculator and banknotes\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>Receipts, a calculator and a ledger: the paper trail that turns a claim into a deduction. <em>Photo: Pexels</em></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">The post we are responding to</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Account</span><span class=\"v\">@buzzmusicia (independent-music account, Los Angeles)</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Series</span><span class=\"v\">UNDER THE RADAR</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Title</span><span class=\"v\">\"18 tax write-offs independent artists could be missing before 2026 ends\"</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Posted</span><span class=\"v\">23 September 2026 · 1/20 slides</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Engagement</span><span class=\"v\">1,358 likes · 25 comments · 127 shares · 3,236 saves</span></li></ul></div>\n<p class=\"note\"><strong>Read this first.</strong> The list below is not tax advice, and it is written for a US post — the original carousel is American, and every line item lives or dies on the law where you are. In Uganda, the relevant regimes are the Income Tax Act and the Uganda Revenue Authority\'s rules for small businesses and professional income; the categories below are the ones artists most often ask about, not a claim that any specific expense is deductible here. Confirm with URA or an accountant before you file.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">1. The studio, not just the session</h3>\n<p>Session fees are the obvious one. The less obvious ones are the things that make the session possible: a laptop that only holds the DAW, an interface, a microphone, hard drives, the monthly cost of the software. If it is used for the music, keep the receipt and keep it separate from the household.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">2. Travel that is actually work</h3>\n<p>Fuel to a video shoot, a bus to Jinja for a festival, a flight for a showcase — the question is not \"was it fun\", it is \"was it for the business\". Keep the date, the purpose and who was there. A photo of the gig is better evidence than a memory.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">3. The phone you release from</h3>\n<p>If the phone is the label office, part of the bill is a business cost. The same goes for the data bundle that uploads the masters. Split the personal from the professional and write the split down.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">4. Artwork and photography</h3>\n<p>Cover art, press shots, the videographer, the editor, the designer who made the logo. Creative work is a cost of the release, not a gift, and the invoice is the record of it.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">5. Distribution and platform fees</h3>\n<p>The distributor\'s annual fee, the website, the domain, the email tool. Small numbers, but they add up — and they are the easiest to forget because they are paid automatically.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">6. Promotion, including the ads</h3>\n<p>Paid posts, playlist pitching services, the radio plugger, the PR. Keep every invoice and check what was actually delivered. A category that is easy to claim is also easy to abuse.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">7. Registration and rights fees</h3>\n<p>Copyright registration, ISRC and ISWC codes, collection-society membership, the fee for the collecting society\'s registration. These are the costs of being paid later, and they are deductible now.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">8. Professional fees</h3>\n<p>The accountant, the lawyer who read the contract, the person who prepared the split sheet. Advice is a business expense, and paying for it once is usually cheaper than the mistake.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">9. Home studio space</h3>\n<p>If a room is used exclusively for the music, part of the rent and the utilities belong to the business. The word \"exclusively\" is doing a lot of work in that sentence — a bedroom with a mic in the corner is harder to justify than a room that is only a studio.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">10. Bank and transaction costs</h3>\n<p>Fees on receiving foreign royalties, the charges on a business account, the cost of converting. Small, boring, and real.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">11. Insurance and protection</h3>\n<p>Equipment cover, and — for anyone touring — the policies that come with the booking. If it protects the business, it belongs with the business.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">12. The education</h3>\n<p>The course on mixing, the workshop on royalties, the conference ticket. Learning the business is a cost of the business.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">13. Merch and physical costs</h3>\n<p>The print run, the stock, the packaging, the shipping. If you sell it, the cost of getting it to the buyer is part of the sale.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">14. The collaboration costs</h3>\n<p>Session players, the featured artist\'s fee, the producer\'s advance. Every one of these should have an invoice and a written split behind it.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">15. The video, end to end</h3>\n<p>Location, transport, the crew\'s food, the equipment hire, the colourist. A video is a production with a dozen small costs that nobody photographs.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">16. Subscriptions that actually earn</h3>\n<p>The DAW, the plugin bundle, the sample library, the stock footage account, the cloud backup. Pay annually where it is cheaper, and cancel what you did not open this year.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">17. Bad debts and refunds</h3>\n<p>The show that was cancelled, the fee that never arrived, the deposit that had to be returned. Document them. A loss you cannot evidence is a loss you cannot claim.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">18. The boring one: records</h3>\n<p>Bookkeeping is not a write-off, it is the thing that makes every other write-off possible. A folder per year, a receipt per expense, and a monthly hour with the numbers. That is the whole system, and it is the step most artists skip.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The two questions that matter</h3>\n<p><strong>Is it a business or a hobby?</strong> In most tax systems, the answer changes what you can claim. The evidence is behaviour: invoices, a separate account, a release calendar, a profit motive. If the music is a business, run it like one on paper as well as on stage.</p>\n<p><strong>Can you prove it?</strong> Every category above collapses without a receipt, a date and a purpose. The 3,236 saves on that carousel are people planning to sort it out later; the only version of this that helps is the one where the folder already exists.</p>\n<p class=\"note\">Read as a response to the cover of @buzzmusicia\'s 23 September 2026 carousel. We have not reproduced or summarised its individual slides, and nothing here is tax advice for Uganda or anywhere else — confirm every category with URA or a qualified accountant before filing. <em>Credit: @buzzmusicia (prompting post, cover only); Uganda Revenue Authority guidance; 7H Music Blog reporting.</em></p>' },
+    // ---- October 17 features: Rolex preview, Kyle Simbwa and Mike Mungu reviews, Peypah visualiser ----
+    { id: 50, slug: 'rolex-festival-2026-10th-edition', category: 'Events', title: 'Rolex Festival at 10: Cricket Oval, 29 November, and a decade of the chapati', excerpt: 'The tenth edition lands at Lugogo Cricket Oval on Sunday, 29 November, with ordinary tickets at UGX 20,000, VIP at 50,000 — and no performer yet announced.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: 'images/events/rolex-festival-2026-poster.jpg', imgPos: 'center', imgCaption: 'The Rolex Festival tenth-anniversary poster (organiser artwork)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '3JpbQORpcLAUy1WyKF1ETc', title: 'Karole Kasita — top tracks (2024 edition)' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/karole-kasita/1448305930',
+      body: '<p class=\"lead\">The Rolex Festival returns for its tenth edition on <strong>Sunday, 29 November 2026</strong> at <strong>Lugogo Cricket Oval</strong> in Kampala — ten years after it began as a celebration of Uganda\'s most famous street food and grew into one of the country\'s biggest annual gatherings.</p>\n<p>The details come from the organisers\' own channels rather than a ticketing listing. The official @RolexFestival account has confirmed the date, the venue and the prices: <strong>UGX 20,000</strong> for ordinary entry and <strong>UGX 50,000</strong> for VIP, sold through the MoMo App or by dialling <strong>*16520#</strong>. The campaign runs under <strong>#RolexFestivalAt10</strong>. What it has not done is name a single performer.</p>\n<div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">Rolex Festival 2026 — the confirmed details</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">When</span><span class=\"v\">Sunday, 29 November 2026</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Where</span><span class=\"v\">Lugogo Cricket Oval, Kampala</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Edition</span><span class=\"v\">10th — #RolexFestivalAt10</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Tickets</span><span class=\"v\">Ordinary UGX 20,000 · VIP UGX 50,000</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">How to buy</span><span class=\"v\">MoMo App or *16520#</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Line-up</span><span class=\"v\">Not yet announced</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Organiser</span><span class=\"v\">Rolex Initiative</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Updates</span><span class=\"v\"><a href=\"https://x.com/rolexfestival\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">@RolexFestival</a></span></li></ul></div>\n\n<figure><img src=\"images/articles/rolex-ugandan-street-food.jpg\" alt=\"A rolex, an omelette and vegetables on a chapati, lifted from a pan\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>A rolex being made in Kampala: egg and vegetables on a chapati, lifted straight off the pan. <em>Photo: Ssemmanda will / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0 (cropped)</em></figcaption></figure><h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Ten years of the rolex</h3>\n<p>A rolex is not a watch. In Uganda it is a chapati rolled around an omelette, usually with tomato, onion and cabbage, made to order at a roadside stand and eaten on the move. It is cheap, fast, everywhere, and — depending on who is arguing — either the country\'s greatest contribution to street food or its most reliable breakfast. The name is a pun on \"rolled eggs\".</p>\n<p>The Rolex Initiative has run the festival since 2016, building it around that food. Enid Mirembe — the organisation\'s founder and CEO, and Miss Tourism Uganda 2015/16 — has framed it as a platform for Ugandan food, culture and music rather than a conventional music festival. The event has moved between the Uganda Museum, Lugogo Cricket Oval and Zoe Grounds as it has grown, and its partner list over the years has included Coca-Cola, the Uganda Tourism Board, the Ministry of Tourism, Total, Africell, Galaxy FM and KCCA.</p>\n<p>The food has its own record-book history: Uganda has set Guinness World Records for both the largest and the fastest rolex, and CNN has included the rolex among Africa\'s best street foods. The festival is where that local pride gets a stage, a crowd and a very long queue.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">What the day looks like</h3>\n<p>The 2024 edition is the best guide to the format. It ran at Zoe Grounds on 18 August, opened at 09:00 and closed at 20:00, charged UGX 20,000 at the gate and put Ray G, Karole Kasita and Fik Fameica on the bill. That is a daytime-to-evening event built around food stalls, cooking demonstrations and a broad Ugandan music line-up rather than a single headline set.</p>\n<p>Expect the same shape in 2026: a food village, rolex makers working several skillets at once, DJs through the afternoon and live acts as the light drops. The food is the headliner before the music is. Gate times for this edition have not been published.</p>\n<p>That 2024 bill is also why the Spotify player below plays Karole Kasita: a sample of the festival\'s booking taste, not a name on the 2026 poster.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The line-up is still empty</h3>\n<p>As of 17 October, neither @RolexFestival nor the Rolex Initiative site has announced a performer for the tenth edition. That is not unusual this far out, but it does mean that any poster circulating with names on it should be treated as unofficial until the organisers post it themselves. This article will be updated when the bill lands.</p>\n<p>Past editions point to a multi-act Ugandan bill rather than one dominant headliner: dancehall, Afro-pop and band music, with the food running all day underneath. If that pattern holds, the tenth edition will be less about one set and more about the full afternoon.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The date, the tickets and the week around it</h3>\n<p>The 29 November date puts the festival at the front of Kampala\'s end-of-year run. <a href=\"#article/kampala-live-season-chameleone-levixone-mesach\">Rachael Magoola\'s 60th anniversary show</a> is on 27 November; Jose Chameleone\'s \"Unlimited\" concert follows at the same Cricket Oval on 4 December. The Rolex Festival sits between them, on the Sunday, with a daytime format that makes it the family-friendly option in a very crowded fortnight.</p>\n<p>Buy through the MoMo route the organisers have published, and check the amount on the confirmation screen before approving. Ticket availability has not been independently checked here, and prices can change. A separate mid-year Rolex event appears on some listing sites for 2026; the 29 November date is the one the organisers are promoting as the tenth-anniversary edition.</p>\n<p class=\"note\">Source: official @RolexFestival posts on 5 and 15 October 2026; Rolex Initiative; NBS Television; listings for the 2024 edition. No performer had been announced at the time of publication. Ticket availability has not been independently checked at checkout.</p>' },
+    { id: 51, slug: 'review-kyle-simbwa-east-african-summer', category: 'Album Review', title: 'Kyle Simbwa\'s East African Summer is a debut album that fits in 18 minutes', excerpt: 'Seven tracks, 18 minutes and no filler: the Apple Music Up Next artist turns Kasangati into a summer record, with \"Cruise Ctrl\" as the outlier and \"East African Girl\" as the one already in circulation.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '5 min read', img: 'https://i.scdn.co/image/ab67616d0000b2734fc7ed29f817fc3987081a81', imgPos: 'center center', imgCaption: 'East African Summer — official cover art', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '5YA97S9AzbC0pbTaeX3X8W', title: 'East African Summer — Kyle Simbwa' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/album/east-african-summer/6797513622',
+      body: '<p class=\"lead\">Kyle Simbwa\'s <em>East African Summer</em> is a debut album that behaves like an EP: seven tracks, 18 minutes, and not one of them wasted.</p>\n<p>It arrived on 27 August 2026 through rain. labs, under exclusive licence from Palagroove, and it is filed under Afro-Pop. Simbwa made it with producer Naj — Eric Najuna Tumuheirwe — and works across the writing, production and engineering himself, the hands-on approach that has defined his records since <em>Person</em> in 2025. A month after release, Apple Music named him its Up Next artist for East Africa.</p>\n<div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">East African Summer at a glance</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Released</span><span class=\"v\">27 August 2026</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Length</span><span class=\"v\">7 tracks · 18 minutes</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Genre</span><span class=\"v\">Afro-Pop</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Made with</span><span class=\"v\">Naj (Eric Najuna Tumuheirwe)</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Label</span><span class=\"v\">℗ 2026 rain. labs, under exclusive licence from Palagroove</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Guests</span><span class=\"v\">Tungi (\"Sugar Honey\") · MAUIMØON (\"Cruise Ctrl\")</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Milestone</span><span class=\"v\">Apple Music Up Next East Africa, 25 September 2026</span></li></ul></div>\n<figure><img src=\"images/articles/kyle-simbwa-up-next.jpg\" alt=\"Kyle Simbwa — Apple Music Up Next East Africa portrait\"><figcaption>Kyle Simbwa — Apple Music Up Next East Africa (photo: Apple Music).</figcaption></figure>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The record, in order</h3>\n<p>It opens with \"Weekend\" (2:38) and the line Apple\'s editors chose to lead with: \"Woke up all feeling like the president.\" \"Fi Dem\" (2:23) follows, then \"Fi Dem (Reprise)\" (2:34) — a percussive procession of the same idea rather than a straightforward repeat. \"East African Girl\" (2:52) is the title in miniature. \"Sugar Honey\" (3:37) brings in Tungi and the hook \"Sugar honey sweet / Baby you should be with me\". \"Christina\" (2:35) keeps the record moving, and \"Cruise Ctrl\" (2:14) closes it with MAUIMØON.</p>\n<p>The shape is deliberate: two versions of one thought at the top, three tracks in the middle, and a guest-assisted exit. There is no interlude, no skit, no track that exists to fill a minute. Eighteen minutes is not a compromise; it is the argument.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The one that could only be his: \"Cruise Ctrl\"</h3>\n<p>The closer is the shortest song on the album and the only one that shares its last word with another artist. MAUIMØON is a fitting partner for that job: the Ugandan singer-producer was the first artist Apple Music spotlighted through Up Next East Africa, in 2023, and Simbwa is the programme\'s 2026 pick. The baton passes in two minutes and 14 seconds.</p>\n<p>It also makes <em>East African Summer</em> a record that ends in conversation rather than summary — unusual for a debut, and a useful clue to where Simbwa might go next. MAUIMØON turns up on <a href=\"#article/tai-dai-whet-new-wave-spotlight\">Tai Dai\'s <em>WHET</em></a> this year too, on \"Where U Go\", which makes the two records cousins of the same season.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The one doing the rounds: \"East African Girl\"</h3>\n<p>If a song from this album has already reached you, it is probably this one. \"East African Girl\" was out as a single before the album, and it carries the project\'s whole idea in its title: the specific, local subject made universal by a melody that does not need translation. At 2:52 it is also the most direct route into the record — the track to play for someone who has not heard a Kyle Simbwa song before.</p>\n<p>That is an editorial call, not a chart report. No streaming or radio numbers are claimed here. But in a year in which Ugandan pop has been travelling further on playlists and radio across the continent, \"East African Girl\" is the song built for those rooms: a title that travels, a groove that works in Kampala, and a chorus that does not overstay.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Why the brevity matters</h3>\n<p>Apple\'s editorial describes <em>East African Summer</em> as Simbwa\'s debut album, which is the platform\'s framing. At seven tracks and 18 minutes, plenty of listeners will call it an EP instead. Both descriptions are defensible. What matters more is that it is the fullest single document of his sound so far — more complete than <em>Person</em>, more confident than the singles that preceded it, and short enough to play twice before deciding.</p>\n<p>He came into 2026 with \"Do You Wrong\", the January duet with <a href=\"#article/remz-down-new-wave-spotlight\">Remz</a>, and leaves it as an Apple Up Next artist with a debut album that does not waste a second. The next record will have more room. This one used what it had.</p>\n<p class=\"note\">Start with \"East African Girl\", then play \"Cruise Ctrl\" for the other side of the record. <em>Credit: Apple Music; Spotify; Shazam; Capital FM; The Tribe UG; KBC.</em></p>' },
+    { id: 52, slug: 'review-mike-mungu-love-isnt-enough', category: 'Album Review', title: 'Mike Mungu\'s LOVE ISNT ENOUGH is a seven-song study in company', excerpt: 'The Kampala Afro-fusion singer\'s new album is 19 minutes of features and exits — with \"Confused\" as its most crowded room and \"Do You Remember\" as the track already out front.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '5 min read', img: 'https://i.scdn.co/image/ab67616d0000b2735fe02a94324dfe9e06e20fc3', imgPos: 'center center', imgCaption: 'LOVE ISNT ENOUGH — official cover art', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '4twrawLG16nxdplLiE6cBf', title: 'LOVE ISNT ENOUGH — Mike Mungu' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/album/love-isnt-enough/6814441235',
+      body: '<p class=\"lead\">Mike Mungu\'s <em>LOVE ISNT ENOUGH</em> is 19 minutes long and rarely alone: four of its seven songs carry a guest, and the record is better for the company.</p>\n<p>It arrived on 2 October 2026 through rain. labs, under exclusive licence from Ten Music Holdings and Palagroove, and Apple files it as Afro-fusion. The guests are Angell Mutoni, Auxxi, MURU, Kamanzii and Senté — five collaborators across four tracks, with MURU and Kamanzii sharing one. For an artist who has spent the year moving between other people\'s records and his own, the arrangement makes sense.</p>\n<div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">LOVE ISNT ENOUGH at a glance</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Released</span><span class=\"v\">2 October 2026</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Length</span><span class=\"v\">7 songs · 19 minutes</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Genre</span><span class=\"v\">Afro-fusion</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Label</span><span class=\"v\">℗ 2026 rain. labs, under exclusive licence from Ten Music Holdings / Palagroove</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Guests</span><span class=\"v\">Angell Mutoni · Auxxi · MURU · Kamanzii · Senté</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Solo tracks</span><span class=\"v\">\"Ma Sade\" · \"Tonight\" · \"Do You Remember\"</span></li></ul></div>\n<figure><img src=\"images/articles/mike-mungu.jpg\" alt=\"Mike Mungu portrait\"><figcaption>Mike Mungu (photo supplied to 7H Music Blog).</figcaption></figure>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The shape of it</h3>\n<p>\"Ma Sade\" (2:39) opens alone. \"Mood\" (2:52) brings Angell Mutoni; \"Involved\" (2:41) brings Auxxi; \"Tonight\" (2:22) is the shortest track and one of only three without a guest. \"Confused\" (2:46) doubles up with MURU and Kamanzii, \"Somebody\" (3:25) brings Senté, and \"Do You Remember\" (3:00) closes the album on Mungu\'s own.</p>\n<p>That tracklist is the record\'s real architecture. The solo songs are the spine — an opening statement, a breath in the middle, a final word — and the features are the rooms off it. Nobody is here to fill a verse. Angell Mutoni, Auxxi, MURU, Kamanzii and Senté each brings a different voice into the frame.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The one that could only exist here: \"Confused\"</h3>\n<p>It is the only song on the album with two guests, and the only one that has to make room for three voices in under three minutes. MURU and Kamanzii join Mungu at the record\'s midpoint, turning \"Confused\" into the album\'s most crowded room — and its most interesting casting decision. The title promises mixed signals; the guest list gives it three perspectives to work with.</p>\n<p>It is also the track that best explains why the album works as an album rather than a playlist of singles. Put \"Confused\" between \"Tonight\" and \"Somebody\" and the sequence does something: solitude, company, a bigger conversation, then a single voice again.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The one doing the rounds: \"Do You Remember\"</h3>\n<p>If one song from this album is already out in front, the likeliest candidate is the closer. \"Do You Remember\" is the longest track here at three minutes, the final word on the record, and the song Apple\'s artist page currently lists above every other cut from <em>LOVE ISNT ENOUGH</em>. That is not a chart position — it is a signal of which track the platform sees people reaching for.</p>\n<p>It is also the right place to start if you have not heard Mungu before. The album\'s title promises a thesis about love and sufficiency; the closer is where that thesis gets its last hearing, with no guest to share the microphone.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The company he keeps</h3>\n<p>Mungu has spent 2026 as a guest as much as a host: \"Kingdom\" with <a href=\"#article/yuko-neo-soul-spotlight\">YUKO</a>, \"Kibuga Kyange\" alongside Dero, The-Hommie and Lany X, and \"Tornado\" on <em>Science</em>. <em>LOVE ISNT ENOUGH</em> returns the favour. <a href=\"#article/sente-afro-fusion-storyteller\">Senté</a>, whose spotlight already flagged \"Somebody\" as one of his appearances this year, is the most familiar name on the list; Angell Mutoni brings the Kigali connection; Auxxi, MURU and Kamanzii widen the circle further.</p>\n<p>What emerges is a record about intimacy that is rarely alone in the room. For an artist whose early work was built around a single voice, that is the growth: Mungu now sounds comfortable enough to let other people into the frame, and confident enough to close the album by himself.</p>\n<p class=\"note\">Start with \"Do You Remember\", then go back to \"Confused\" for the album\'s fullest room. <em>Credit: Apple Music; Spotify; Boomplay; Audiomack; Shazam.</em></p>' },
+    { id: 53, slug: 'peypah-know-you-visualiser', category: 'News', title: 'Peypah and JoJo Hundred give \"Know You\" a visualiser — and a second life', excerpt: 'The 2:51 Afro-fusion duet, released in May, finally gets a visual — shot by Salim Muhumuza and edited by Shogzz — and a playlist life that has already reached Ghana.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '5 min read', img: 'https://i.scdn.co/image/ab67616d0000b273bbd0fbce907b28b890340305', imgPos: 'center 35%', imgCaption: '\"Know You\" cover art (Peypah)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'track', id: '19pZXkskgig4bKFNBzbsA6', title: 'Know You — Peypah & JoJo Hundred' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/gh/album/know-you-single/6773898964',
+      body: '<p class=\"lead\">Peypah\'s \"Know You\" has been out since May. This week it got a visualiser — and with it a second life.</p>\n<p>The clip landed on the PEYPAH YouTube channel on <strong>15 October 2026</strong>, four and a half months after the single. It runs 2:52; the song runs 2:51. The credits are short and specific: shot by Salim Muhumuza (@SalimMuhumuzadp), edited by Shogzz (@Shogzz10), with Layceeytrix credited on production.</p>\n<div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">\"Know You\" — the file</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Artists</span><span class=\"v\">Peypah &amp; JoJo Hundred</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Single</span><span class=\"v\">29 May 2026 · 2:51</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Visualiser</span><span class=\"v\">15 October 2026 · 2:52</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Watch</span><span class=\"v\"><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_X6iUGLHplo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">YouTube</a></span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Credits</span><span class=\"v\">Salim Muhumuza (shot) · Shogzz (edit) · Layceeytrix (production)</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Label</span><span class=\"v\">℗ 2026 PEYPAH</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Genre</span><span class=\"v\">Afro-fusion</span></li></ul></div>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The song</h3>\n<p>\"Know You\" is a duet built on a simple request: \"I just want to know you, babe / tell me a little bit of something.\" The ask repeats until it becomes the hook, and the record\'s charm is that it never resolves into a confession. It stays in the asking.</p>\n<p>The writing is specific about the material — Gucci and Chanel, nails done \"for the attention\" — without letting the brands do the emotional work. The point is the performance of being worth knowing, and the song is smart enough to notice it. JoJo Hundred\'s feature gives the record its second centre of gravity: the Croydon-raised rapper\'s drill-tinted delivery against Peypah\'s smoother vocal makes the duet feel like two people negotiating rather than harmonising.</p>\n<p>At 2:51 it does what the best Afrobeats-adjacent duets do: it ends while you are still deciding whose verse you prefer.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Who is JoJo Hundred?</h3>\n<p>He is not a name dropped in from nowhere. JoJo Hundred came up through the late-2010s London scene, broke out with \"B.W.B\" in September 2019 — a record that has passed a million Spotify plays — and has spent 2026 releasing steadily: \"Fdo\", \"Punctuality\" and \"Luwombo\" (31 August). \"Know You\" already sits in his Apple Music top three, behind \"Luwombo\" and \"Foden\".</p>\n<p>His sound sits where drill, Afrobeats and R&amp;B meet, which is why the pairing works. Peypah\'s Afro-fusion gives him a warmer bed than his solo records; he gives the song an edge it would not have had alone.</p>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Peypah\'s year</h3>\n<p>The single is part of a busy 2026 for Peypah. \"One Love (2026 FIFA World Cup Anthem)\" arrived on 5 June, and the project <em>Afrobeasts</em> is also out this year, alongside earlier singles such as \"Lucy\", \"Oh My\", \"Healing\", \"Emisiwa\" and \"Vayibuu\". \"Know You\" has travelled further than most: it has turned up on Spotify\'s <em>Hot Hits Ghana 2026</em> and other regional playlists, the kind of placement that moves a Kampala record into a West African feed.</p>\n<p>That is what the visualiser is for. It is a visualiser rather than a full narrative video: a second push, the kind independent artists use to keep a song in feeds long after release week. Four and a half months after the single, the timing is deliberate. The song is already out there. The clip is the reminder.</p>\n<p class=\"note\">The visualiser is on <a href=\"https://youtu.be/_X6iUGLHplo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">YouTube</a>; the single is in the Spotify player below. <em>Credit: YouTube; Apple Music; Spotify; Shazam.</em></p>' },
