+    { id: 49, slug: 'songs-of-the-week-october-17-2026', category: 'Top Hot Releases', title: 'Songs of the Week: Mr Fallback\'s "Jennifer" leads the first five', excerpt: 'A new weekly file from the 7H desk: the five records we had on repeat this week — led by "Jennifer", with AYMA, ECEE & YUKO, Quency and Remz behind it.', date: 'Oct 17, 2026', readTime: '5 min read', img: 'images/covers/jennifer.jpg', imgCaption: 'Jennifer — Mr Fallback, No. 1 this week (official artwork)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'playlist', id: '1p7M4hsphFLrezmw9arXGs', title: '7H Music Group : The Sound — Songs of the Week' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/us/album/jennifer-single/6822713688',
+      body: '<p class="lead">Five records, one shared playlist, and a new home for the releases moving Kampala: this is the first Songs of the Week.</p>'
+        + '<p><strong>Top Hot Releases</strong> is the blog\'s new category for the weekly picks — the records the 7H desk actually kept replaying, filed every week from Uganda and the wider East African scene. It is an editorial listening list, not a streaming chart: no play counts are ranked here, nobody pays for a place, and the order is simply the order we would put the songs in a playlist. The bar is that a record has to feel current — either brand new, or a recent release that has only now found its audience.</p>'
+        + '<p>This week\'s five run from a 7H single that is barely a day old to a September sleeper that has been in the headphones since release. Here is the file.</p>'
+        + '<ul class="winners">'
+        + '<li><span class="k"><img class="art" src="images/covers/jennifer.jpg" alt="Jennifer cover art" loading="lazy">1 · "Jennifer"<br>Mr Fallback</span><span class="v">Out October 16, two weeks after <em>Kiwatto</em>, and the week\'s clearest statement: a dedication record that still moves. It carries the <strong>2Puff Records</strong> imprint over the in-house 7H sound — a new chapter in how his records travel. <a href="#article/mr-fallback-jennifer">Read the full story</a>.</span></li>'
+        + '<li><span class="k"><img class="art" src="https://is1-ssl.mzstatic.com/image/thumb/Music221/v4/da/b4/e9/dab4e9df-e203-92da-84e9-85daf7383703/712132199933_cover.jpg/600x600bb.jpg" alt="ASS TITUDE cover art" loading="lazy">2 · "ASS TITUDE"<br>AYMA</span><span class="v">His first solo single since June\'s "MAPENZI", following the "GONE" link-up with Soundlykbb that has settled at the top of Soundlykbb\'s Spotify top-tracks list. Released through Party Wright Records under exclusive licence to Himaya Music. <a href="#article/ayma-gone-ass-titude">More on AYMA</a>.</span></li>'
+        + '<li><span class="k"><img class="art" src="https://is1-ssl.mzstatic.com/image/thumb/Music211/v4/88/fa/52/88fa5229-2753-d145-a863-2f44b29bd80b/0.jpg/600x600bb.jpg" alt="Focus cover art" loading="lazy">3 · "Focus"<br>ECEE &amp; YUKO</span><span class="v">September 4\'s north–south collaboration: ECEE\'s Kitgum-to-Kampala rap switch-up meeting YUKO\'s Bruges-raised neo-soul, with both artists writing their own credits and releasing independently. <a href="#article/ecee-kitgum-focus-spotlight">Meet ECEE</a> · <a href="#article/yuko-neo-soul-spotlight">meet YUKO</a>.</span></li>'
+        + '<li><span class="k"><img class="art" src="https://is1-ssl.mzstatic.com/image/thumb/Music211/v4/5f/b1/e8/5fb1e8a5-6ef5-d4a0-b77c-fa0cc33f83c8/artwork.jpg/600x600bb.jpg" alt="Pg cover art" loading="lazy">4 · "Pg"<br>Quency</span><span class="v">The cut currently sitting at the top of Quency\'s Spotify page — trap-leaning Kampala rap released on his own Uncle Q Records, from the same underground run that produced "Jus Sayin\'" with Pryce Teeba. <a href="#article/quency-jus-sayin-freestyle-spotlight">Read the spotlight</a>.</span></li>'
+        + '<li><span class="k"><img class="art" src="https://is1-ssl.mzstatic.com/image/thumb/Music221/v4/f4/cd/4b/f4cd4bdc-1463-81ba-92f7-32beb39de3fd/7333595899807.jpg/600x600bb.jpg" alt="Down cover art" loading="lazy">5 · "Down"<br>Remz</span><span class="v">Two minutes and ten seconds of low, steady vocal over a soft Afro-pop bed — still the clearest introduction to Remz, and still the record at the top of her Spotify artist page after its September 25 release. <a href="#article/remz-down-new-wave-spotlight">Meet Remz</a>.</span></li>'
+        + '</ul>'
+        + '<p>The file also lives on Spotify: <strong>7H Music Group : The Sound</strong> stacks each week\'s five beside the Fresh Finds spotlights and the 7H roster, and it is where the list keeps running between posts. <a href="https://open.spotify.com/playlist/1p7M4hsphFLrezmw9arXGs" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-out>Follow the playlist</a>.</p>'
+        + '<h3 ' + H3 + '>Why these five</h3>'
+        + '<p>The through-line is independence. Mr Fallback\'s record is a partnership between two independent imprints rather than a major-label play; AYMA is building a catalogue a few thousand monthly listeners at a time; ECEE and YUKO each run their own imprint and write their own songs; Quency releases through Uncle Q Records; Remz has no big machine behind her and does not need one. None of that is a marketing angle — it is simply how this scene is putting records out in 2026, and it is why a weekly file feels more useful than a monthly one.</p>'
+        + '<p>The five are also a fair map of where the sound is. Afro-pop and Afrobeats lead, but "Focus" pulls rap and neo-soul into the same frame, "Pg" keeps the underground\'s punchline economy intact, and "Down" is the kind of short, unhurried R&amp;B-adjacent record that has been quietly defining the new wave. <a href="#article/uganda-music-economy-atlas-report-2026">The industry numbers</a> say most Ugandan artists are independent; the week\'s playlist says that is not a weakness.</p>'
+        + '<h3 ' + H3 + '>Bubbling under</h3>'
+        + '<p>Four records just missed the five and are worth the next slot in your queue: <a href="#article/joka-ready-new-era">Joka\'s "READY"</a> (October 2), <a href="#article/yuko-neo-soul-spotlight">YUKO &amp; Mike Mungu\'s "Kingdom"</a>, <a href="#article/ayma-gone-ass-titude">AYMA &amp; Soundlykbb\'s "GONE"</a> and <a href="#article/echoled-kush-after-hour">Echoled\'s <em>Kush After Hour</em> EP</a>. They are linked with both platforms at the end of this page.</p>'
+        + '<div class="factbox"><p class="factbox-title">How Songs of the Week works</p><ul>'
+        + '<li><span class="k">Cadence</span><span class="v">Weekly — a new file every week</span></li>'
+        + '<li><span class="k">Picks</span><span class="v">Five ranked records, plus four "bubbling under"</span></li>'
+        + '<li><span class="k">Scope</span><span class="v">Uganda and East Africa, with 7H artists in the mix</span></li>'
+        + '<li><span class="k">Basis</span><span class="v">Editorial listening — not a streaming chart, no play counts ranked</span></li>'
+        + '<li><span class="k">Suggest</span><span class="v"><a href="mailto:7hmusicgroupug@gmail.com">7hmusicgroupug@gmail.com</a></span></li>'
+        + '</ul></div>'
+        + '<p>Every pick in this file is on both Spotify and Apple Music, and each card in the Songs of the Week section on the homepage carries a ▶ preview. Next week\'s list opens the same way: listen first, rank second.</p>'
+        + '<p class="note">Songs of the Week is an editorial selection by the 7H desk. No streaming figures are cited or ranked; release details and platform links are taken from the artists\' official pages. <em>Credit: Spotify; Apple Music; Party Wright Records; Himaya Music; 2Puff Records; Uncle Q Records.</em></p>' },
     {"id":48,"slug":"likdul-fp-red-bull-dance-your-style-visa-rejected","category":"News","title":"Likdul FP says visa rejection has blocked his Red Bull Dance Your Style trip to Zurich","excerpt":"After winning Red Bull Dance Your Style in Uganda, Likdul FP says he trained to represent the country in Zurich. Now, visibly distressed in a TikTok video, he says his visa was rejected and the opportunity may be slipping away.","date":"Oct 16, 2026","readTime":"5 min read","img":"images/articles/likdul-fp-red-bull-win.jpg","imgPos":"center 38%","imgCaption":"Likdul FP (second from right) holds the Red Bull Dance Your Style Uganda trophy with fellow dancers (photo supplied to 7H Music Blog; @likdulfortportal watermark).","author":{"name":"7H Music Blog","role":"Editorial","avatar":"images/brand/7h-avatar.svg"},"body":"<p class=\"lead\">Ugandan dancer <strong>Likdul FP</strong> won the national Red Bull Dance Your Style competition and earned the chance to carry Uganda’s flag to the international stage in Zurich. Now he says a rejected visa has put that journey in doubt.</p><p>In a TikTok video posted on his page, the dancer is visibly distressed and in tears as he describes what the trip meant to him. He says he had prepared and trained hard to represent Uganda in Switzerland, and saw the opportunity as a possible turning point for his own life and for his family. With the visa rejected, he says that work now feels as though it may have been for nothing.</p><p><a href=\"https://vt.tiktok.com/ZSgFu1GJc/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Watch Likdul FP’s statement on TikTok.</a></p><h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">What Red Bull Dance Your Style is</h3><p>Red Bull Dance Your Style is a one-on-one street-dance competition built around improvisation, musicality and personality. Dancers do not arrive with a fixed routine for a known song. The DJ can move between genres and eras, forcing each competitor to respond to the music in real time.</p><p>The format’s defining feature is that the crowd decides. Instead of a conventional judging panel scoring technique from a table, audience members vote after the battle, commonly using the competition’s red-and-blue choice system. That makes connection as important as control: a dancer has to hear the track, answer an opponent and win the room at once.</p><p>National competitions provide a route toward the international Dance Your Style stage. For a Ugandan winner, that route is more than another battle. It is access to a global dance community, international visibility and the possibility of future bookings, collaborations and sponsorship.</p><h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">A win that was supposed to travel</h3><p>Likdul FP’s national victory placed him in that pathway. The materials shared with 7H Music Blog document the Ugandan competition and his win: <a href=\"https://share.google/wL7JNptAuuNJNVbNA\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">view the first shared link</a> and <a href=\"https://share.google/0PuyrO962h6oa69NS\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">the second shared link</a>.</p><p>The painful part of his video is the gap between winning the opportunity and being able to use it. Training can prepare an athlete or artist for the stage; it cannot by itself clear the border. For independent African creatives, visas, documentation costs, appointment timelines and travel logistics can become an additional qualifying round after the public competition is already over.</p><blockquote>He says he trained hard to represent Uganda in Zurich and believed the opportunity could improve life for him and his family.<cite>Likdul FP’s TikTok statement, paraphrased</cite></blockquote><h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">What is confirmed — and what is not</h3><p>The visa rejection is being reported here from <strong>Likdul FP’s own account</strong>. The refusal letter, the visa category, the stated reason for refusal and any appeal or reapplication deadline have not been made public in the material supplied to this blog. There is also no official statement before us from the relevant Swiss visa authorities or from Red Bull Dance Your Style.</p><p>That distinction matters. A rejection should not be assigned a motive without the documents, and nobody outside the process can promise that public attention will reverse it. The urgent practical questions are whether the decision can be reviewed, whether a corrected application can be filed in time, and what travel-document support the competition organiser can provide to its national champion.</p><h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Uganda should not lose the moment quietly</h3><p>Likdul FP did the part the dancefloor asked of him: he competed, won the crowd and earned the chance to represent his country. His video is resonating because it shows how quickly a breakthrough can become heartbreak when the infrastructure around talent does not move with the talent itself.</p><p>The next update should come from the people who can act: the dancer and his representatives, the event organisers, and the authorities handling the application. Until then, support should remain factual and respectful — share his statement, avoid publishing private documents without consent, and do not turn a painful moment into abuse aimed at individuals.</p><p class=\"note\">Source note: this report is based on the links and account supplied to 7H Music Blog, including Likdul FP’s TikTok statement. The Google share links could not be independently opened in our publishing environment. We have therefore attributed the personal and visa claims to the dancer and have not reported an undisclosed refusal reason as fact. Header photograph was supplied to 7H Music Blog and identifies Likdul FP as second from right, holding the trophy.</p>"},
     {"id":46,"slug":"pare-pare-rave-uncle-waffles-kampala-2026","category":"Events","title":"Pare Pare Rave brings Uncle Waffles to Lugogo on December 18","excerpt":"Amapiano star Uncle Waffles headlines Pare Pare Rave at Millennium Grounds, Lugogo. The Die Hard tier is sold out; early-bird tickets are UGX 120,000 until October 31.","date":"Oct 16, 2026","readTime":"4 min read","img":"images/events/pare-pare-rave-uncle-waffles.jpg","imgPos":"center 35%","imgCaption":"Pare Pare Rave — Uncle Waffles, December 18, 2026 at Millennium Grounds, Lugogo (organiser artwork supplied to 7H Music Blog)","author":{"name":"7H Music Blog","role":"Editorial","avatar":"images/brand/7h-avatar.svg"},"listen":{"type":"artist","id":"53RGhjlRYRLe67b8i6Yh2","title":"Uncle Waffles — top tracks"},"apple":"https://music.apple.com/us/artist/uncle-waffles/1612263083","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Pare Pare Rave has set its Kampala date: <strong>Friday, December 18, 2026</strong> at <strong>Millennium Grounds, Lugogo</strong>, with Uncle Waffles at the top of the bill.</p><p>The supplied campaign artwork puts the Eswatini-born, South Africa-based DJ at the centre of the night. Uncle Waffles became one of amapiano’s most visible international selectors after her sets travelled far beyond the club through social clips, then converted that attention into releases and festival appearances. “Tanzania”, from the <em>Red Dragon</em> era, became an early signature and helped turn the DJ behind the decks into a recording artist with a global audience.</p><h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The Kampala date</h3><p>This is an event preview rather than a line-up announcement beyond the confirmed headliner. The poster names Uncle Waffles and gives the date, venue and ticket route; it does not show supporting performers, gate times or age restrictions. We will add those details only when the organisers publish them.</p><div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">Pare Pare Rave — ticket desk</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">When</span><span class=\"v\">Friday, December 18, 2026</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Where</span><span class=\"v\">Millennium Grounds, Lugogo, Kampala</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Headliner</span><span class=\"v\">Uncle Waffles</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Early bird</span><span class=\"v\">UGX 120,000 — advertised to expire October 31</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Die Hard</span><span class=\"v\">Sold out</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Tickets</span><span class=\"v\"><a href=\"https://rave.ug/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rave.ug</a></span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Inquiries</span><span class=\"v\">077 953 5225 / 070 853 5225</span></li></ul></div><figure><img src=\"images/events/pare-pare-rave-early-bird.jpg\" alt=\"Pare Pare Rave early-bird ticket poster showing UGX 120,000 and an October 31 expiry\"><figcaption>The early-bird artwork advertises UGX 120,000 until October 31.</figcaption></figure><h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">One tier has already moved</h3><p>The separate ticket update marks the Die Hard raver allocation as <strong>sold out</strong>. That makes the live early-bird offer the useful number for buyers now: UGX 120,000, with the poster saying the offer expires on October 31. Ticket prices and availability can change, so confirm the live checkout total at Rave Uganda before paying.</p><figure><img src=\"images/events/pare-pare-rave-sold-out.jpg\" alt=\"Pare Pare Rave poster marking Die Hard raver tickets sold out\"><figcaption>The organiser’s ticket update marks the Die Hard tier sold out.</figcaption></figure><p class=\"note\">Source: three Pare Pare Rave artworks supplied to 7H Music Blog on October 16, 2026. This article reports only the details visible on those materials. Ticket availability has not been independently checked at checkout.</p>"},
     {"id":47,"slug":"spinny-and-friends-festival-kololo-2026","category":"Events","title":"Spinny and Friends Festival announces DJ Spinny, Akio and Daliwonga for Kololo","excerpt":"The festival lands at Kololo Airstrip on December 19 with its first three headliners announced and six- and eight-seater table packages now advertised for Uganda and Rwanda buyers.","date":"Oct 16, 2026","readTime":"5 min read","img":"images/events/spinny-and-friends-2026.jpg","imgPos":"center 30%","imgCaption":"Spinny and Friends Festival — Phase 1 headliners, December 19, 2026 at Kololo Airstrip (organiser artwork supplied to 7H Music Blog)","author":{"name":"7H Music Blog","role":"Editorial","avatar":"images/brand/7h-avatar.svg"},"listen":{"type":"album","id":"3MrwtIbQYWx3GMUz3z1Wwg","title":"Buri Izooba — Mozaka, Amanya & DJ Spinny"},"apple":"https://music.apple.com/ug/album/buri-izooba-ep/1883480631","body":"<p class=\"lead\"><strong>Spinny and Friends Festival</strong> joins Kampala’s end-of-year calendar on <strong>Saturday, December 19, 2026</strong> at <strong>Kololo Airstrip</strong>. Its first headliner announcement names Akio, DJ Spinny and South African amapiano vocalist Daliwonga.</p><p>The “and Friends” framing fits the way DJ Spinny has been working across scenes. In March 2026, <em>Buri Izooba</em> brought Spinny together with Mozaka and Kampala singer-producer <a href=\"#article/amanya-mosquitoes-alte-rnb\">Amanya</a> for a four-track package that moved between the full club version, radio edit, instrumental and vocals. The player above is that collaboration — a useful route into the Ugandan side of the festival host’s sound before December.</p><h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Phase one of the bill</h3><p>The announcement calls Akio, DJ Spinny and Daliwonga the <strong>Phase 1 headliners</strong>, which means more names may follow. Akio and Daliwonga are marked with South African flags on the artwork. The poster does not yet give performance times, gate times, general-admission prices or age restrictions; those remain open until the organiser publishes them.</p><div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">Spinny and Friends Festival</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">When</span><span class=\"v\">Saturday, December 19, 2026</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Where</span><span class=\"v\">Kololo Airstrip, Kampala</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Phase 1</span><span class=\"v\">Akio · DJ Spinny · Daliwonga</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Tickets</span><span class=\"v\"><a href=\"https://www.spinnyandfriends.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">spinnyandfriends.com</a></span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Inquiries</span><span class=\"v\">+256 775 050041</span></li></ul></div><h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Uganda table packages</h3><p>The supplied Uganda ticket artwork advertises two table packages. The <strong>UGX 3,000,000 six-seater</strong> includes Don Julio Reposado, JW Gold Label, six event passes, three VIP parking slots, five mixers, a better view and exclusive merchandise. The <strong>UGX 5,000,000 eight-seater</strong> includes Don Julio 1942, JW Blue Label, eight event passes, ten mixers, five VIP parking slots, a premium view and exclusive merchandise.</p><figure><img src=\"images/events/spinny-and-friends-uganda-tables.jpg\" alt=\"Spinny and Friends Festival Uganda table packages: UGX 3 million for six seats and UGX 5 million for eight seats\"><figcaption>Uganda table packages as advertised by the organiser.</figcaption></figure><h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Rwanda booking prices</h3><p>A separate Rwanda sales artwork lists <strong>RWF 1,200,000</strong> for the six-seater and <strong>RWF 2,000,000</strong> for the eight-seater, with the same advertised package structure. Buyers should select the correct market and currency at checkout rather than converting between the two posters themselves.</p><figure><img src=\"images/events/spinny-and-friends-rwanda-tables.jpg\" alt=\"Spinny and Friends Festival Rwanda table packages: RWF 1.2 million for six seats and RWF 2 million for eight seats\"><figcaption>Rwanda table packages from the supplied campaign artwork.</figcaption></figure><p>General-admission pricing is not shown in the supplied images, so no figure is listed here. Check the official ticket site for individual passes, availability and full terms before paying.</p><p class=\"note\">Source: Spinny and Friends Festival Phase 1 and table-package artworks supplied to 7H Music Blog on October 16, 2026. Package contents and prices are transcribed from the posters; availability has not been independently checked at checkout.</p>"},
-    {"id":44,"slug":"king-avo-baddest-ep-2puff-records","category":"News","title":"King Avo steps forward with Baddest, his new EP under 2Puff Records","excerpt":"Four tracks in eleven minutes: King Avo’s Baddest brings Baranga, Selekta Ian and Unknon Djay into a compact new project under 2Puff Records.","date":"Oct 16, 2026","readTime":"3 min read","img":"https://i.scdn.co/image/ab67616d0000b2734ab9b7c01570b58f722921ba","imgCaption":"Baddest — official King Avo release artwork (Spotify)","author":{"name":"7H Music Blog","role":"Editorial","avatar":"images/brand/7h-avatar.svg"},"listen":{"type":"album","id":"5FEkY9Z7BqCquitU7RkfQX","title":"King Avo — Baddest"},"body":"<p class=\"lead\">Four tracks, just over eleven minutes, and a feature from Baranga: King Avo’s new EP, <em>Baddest</em>, puts a compact project at the centre of his next chapter under 2Puff Records.</p>\n<p>For listeners meeting him through a collaboration, this is a straightforward place to start. The release moves from a solo opener into two collaborative tracks before returning to King Avo alone for the close. Rather than stretching the introduction across an album, <em>Baddest</em> gives his name the headline in a short, complete sequence.</p>\n<h3>Inside the four-track EP</h3>\n<p><strong>“Pull up”</strong> opens the project at 2:02. Next is <strong>“Motion”</strong>, featuring <a href=\"#article/baranga-debut-album-chapters-i-never-wrote\">Baranga</a>, at 3:38 — the longest track on the EP. <strong>“Outside”</strong> brings in Selekta Ian and Unknon Djay for a 2:47 collaboration, before <strong>“Mariana”</strong> closes the sequence at 2:43. Those four tracks total 11 minutes and 10 seconds.</p>\n<p>The credits give the release two entry points: King Avo’s solo material on either side, and a collaborative middle section. If you arrived here through Baranga, “Motion” is the obvious connection; starting with “Pull up” and staying through “Mariana” gives you the full project in its intended order.</p>\n<h3>A connection to the wider circle</h3>\n<p>King Avo’s catalogue already includes “CHILLS,” “Gumide” featuring Mellan Kunta, and “Wake and Bake” featuring JST. His name also appears on Baranga’s <em>Chapters I Never Wrote</em>, on “JAGERMEISTER” and “LOST CAUSE.” Those listings make “Motion” part of an existing creative connection rather than a first meeting. <a href=\"https://music.apple.com/za/artist/king-avo/1738097686\">[1]</a></p>\n<h3>Start with the project</h3>\n<p>For 7H readers following the artists around Baranga and 2Puff Records, <em>Baddest</em> is another name to put on the listening list. This is a release spotlight, not a track-by-track verdict: the best next step is to hear the complete EP and decide which track stays with you.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/album/5FEkY9Z7BqCquitU7RkfQX\">Listen to King Avo’s <em>Baddest</em> on Spotify.</a></p>\n<p class=\"note\">Release details, track order, collaborators and running times: <a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/embed/album/5FEkY9Z7BqCquitU7RkfQX\">Spotify release listing</a>. The 2Puff Records affiliation was supplied by the label desk. Artwork: official Spotify release cover. No release date or production credits are asserted here.</p>"},
+    {"id":44,"slug":"king-avo-baddest-ep-2puff-records","category":"News","title":"King Avo steps forward with Baddest, his new EP under 2Puff Records","excerpt":"Four tracks in eleven minutes: King Avo’s Baddest brings Baranga, Selekta Ian and Unknon Djay into a compact new project under 2Puff Records.","date":"Oct 16, 2026","readTime":"3 min read","img":"https://i.scdn.co/image/ab67616d0000b2734ab9b7c01570b58f722921ba","imgCaption":"Baddest — official King Avo release artwork (Spotify)","author":{"name":"7H Music Blog","role":"Editorial","avatar":"images/brand/7h-avatar.svg"},"listen":{"type":"album","id":"5FEkY9Z7BqCquitU7RkfQX","title":"King Avo — Baddest"},"apple":"https://music.apple.com/ug/album/baddest-ep/6820025719","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Four tracks, just over eleven minutes, and a feature from Baranga: King Avo’s new EP, <em>Baddest</em>, puts a compact project at the centre of his next chapter under 2Puff Records.</p>\n<p>For listeners meeting him through a collaboration, this is a straightforward place to start. The release moves from a solo opener into two collaborative tracks before returning to King Avo alone for the close. Rather than stretching the introduction across an album, <em>Baddest</em> gives his name the headline in a short, complete sequence.</p>\n<h3>Inside the four-track EP</h3>\n<p><strong>“Pull up”</strong> opens the project at 2:02. Next is <strong>“Motion”</strong>, featuring <a href=\"#article/baranga-debut-album-chapters-i-never-wrote\">Baranga</a>, at 3:38 — the longest track on the EP. <strong>“Outside”</strong> brings in Selekta Ian and Unknon Djay for a 2:47 collaboration, before <strong>“Mariana”</strong> closes the sequence at 2:43. Those four tracks total 11 minutes and 10 seconds.</p>\n<p>The credits give the release two entry points: King Avo’s solo material on either side, and a collaborative middle section. If you arrived here through Baranga, “Motion” is the obvious connection; starting with “Pull up” and staying through “Mariana” gives you the full project in its intended order.</p>\n<h3>A connection to the wider circle</h3>\n<p>King Avo’s catalogue already includes “CHILLS,” “Gumide” featuring Mellan Kunta, and “Wake and Bake” featuring JST. His name also appears on Baranga’s <em>Chapters I Never Wrote</em>, on “JAGERMEISTER” and “LOST CAUSE.” Those listings make “Motion” part of an existing creative connection rather than a first meeting. <a href=\"https://music.apple.com/za/artist/king-avo/1738097686\">[1]</a></p>\n<h3>Start with the project</h3>\n<p>For 7H readers following the artists around Baranga and 2Puff Records, <em>Baddest</em> is another name to put on the listening list. This is a release spotlight, not a track-by-track verdict: the best next step is to hear the complete EP and decide which track stays with you.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/album/5FEkY9Z7BqCquitU7RkfQX\">Listen to King Avo’s <em>Baddest</em> on Spotify.</a></p>\n<p class=\"note\">Release details, track order, collaborators and running times: <a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/embed/album/5FEkY9Z7BqCquitU7RkfQX\">Spotify release listing</a>. The 2Puff Records affiliation was supplied by the label desk. Artwork: official Spotify release cover. No release date or production credits are asserted here.</p>"},
     {"id":45,"slug":"pyong-recordz-ntinda-label-spotlight","category":"Industry Insights","title":"Inside Pyong Recordz: the people, artists and business behind the Ntinda collective","excerpt":"An October 2026 profile of Pyong Recordz: founders Pryce Teeba and Dreadlock Dziz, its operating team, the RIA relationship, and the artists and releases shaping its wider ecosystem.","date":"Oct 16, 2026","readTime":"9 min read","img":"https://i.scdn.co/image/ab6761610000e5eb654ac7ec9a32b4cb36e98746","imgPos":"center 25%","imgCaption":"Pryce Teeba, founder of Pyong Recordz — official Spotify artist photo","author":{"name":"7H Music Blog","role":"Editorial","avatar":"images/brand/7h-avatar.svg"},"listen":{"type":"artist","id":"7LZeZZQTTiqOuxCN30dlGX","title":"Pryce Teeba — explore the catalogue"},"apple":"https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/pryce-teeba/1129257218","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Pyong Recordz is a Kampala-based Ugandan record label and artist collective with roots in Ntinda, a hip-hop foundation and a wider urban-music outlook. Its story includes the artists on the microphone, but also the people building the production, development and business structure behind them.</p><p>The organisational profile supplied to 7H Music Blog in October 2026 places creative independence, African identity and community at the centre of Pyong’s ambitions. It describes a home for recording, collaboration, content creation, promotion and artist growth — not simply a name beneath a release.</p><p class=\"note\">Source guide: leadership names, appointments and internal functions below come from the supplied organisational profile; they are not presented as independently verified public appointments. Linked release listings support public catalogue details. Explanations of how departments could work together are editorial interpretation, not approved job descriptions.</p>\n<h3>A label and a collective</h3><p>Pyong has two connected identities. As a label, its work concerns recorded music, releases, rights and artist development. As a collective, its creative community can include performers, producers, engineers and collaborators whose relationships extend beyond individual contracts. The label’s public site identifies its Ntinda roots and Pryce Teeba’s founding role. <a href=\"https://github.com/mikeo-ne/pyongrecordz\">Label website repository</a>.</p><p>Its catalogue reaches beyond rap into dancehall and other urban styles. Mike 1ne describes Pyong and Ria Entertainment as working hand in hand to distribute music and nurture talents including Chattarbwoy, Troy Mc, Omunubi and JOHN PAPA SMYL. Recurring shared release credits provide a public listening trail through that wider network.</p>\n<h3>Founders, partners and operating leadership</h3><p>The supplied structure names <strong>Pryce Teeba as Label President</strong> and <strong>Dreadlock Dziz as CEO</strong>, identifying both as founders. Teeba connects the artist-facing vision with the label’s hip-hop identity; the CEO role provides an organisational counterpart for coordination and execution. That division is a reading of the titles, not a claim that every duty has been formally allocated.</p><div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">Founders</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Pryce Teeba (Alfred Kasirye)</span><span class=\"v\">Label President</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Dreadlock Dziz (Mwesigye Allan)</span><span class=\"v\">CEO</span></li></ul></div><div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">Partners</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Baru (Amanya Frank)</span><span class=\"v\">Lead Sound Engineer · Partner</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Byamugisha Eddie (Billions)</span><span class=\"v\">CFO · Partner</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Twine Ronald (Sir Depp)</span><span class=\"v\">Partner</span></li></ul></div><div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">Marketing &amp; A&amp;R</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Jonathan Maiga</span><span class=\"v\">Head of Marketing</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Lochero Declan Mike (Mike1ne)</span><span class=\"v\">Head of A&amp;R</span></li></ul></div><div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">Operations</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Gang Andrew</span><span class=\"v\">COO</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Frank Atex</span><span class=\"v\">Corporate Affairs</span></li></ul></div><div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">Legal &amp; governance</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Ian Ariiho</span><span class=\"v\">Company Secretary and Legal</span></li></ul></div><div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">Label business</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Romeo Oyisse</span><span class=\"v\">Head of Label Business</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">RIA</span><span class=\"v\">Distribution and Publishing Administrator</span></li></ul></div><p>Baru brings an engineering focus, Billions a finance portfolio, and Sir Depp a partner-level role. No specific operational portfolio has been supplied for Sir Depp, so none is assigned here. The profile does not establish ownership percentages, investment contributions or voting rights.</p>\n<h3>From discovery to release: the A&amp;R connection</h3><p>Mike1ne’s Head of A&amp;R role places talent development within the operating structure. In practice, an A&amp;R workflow can connect scouting and assessment, repertoire selection, creative direction, producer and songwriter matching, and recording-readiness decisions. Marketing, engineering, legal, label business and distribution then help turn a promising recording into a prepared release.</p><p>Likewise, the CFO title points toward budgeting and financial oversight; the COO toward operational coordination; and company-secretarial and legal functions toward governance and clearances. These are functional interpretations, not a verified statement of each person’s day-to-day responsibilities.</p>\n<h3>An artist ecosystem, not a blanket claim of exclusive signings</h3><p>The supplied profile places Pryce Teeba, Chattarbwoy, Ambroy and Leghacity within Pyong’s core public-facing ecosystem, alongside Ali Bomaye, Son of Aboda, Jenesis Kimera, Omunubi, Troy Mc, JOHN PAPA SMYL and other collaborators. It also identifies BLXCK Charm / Blaxck Charm, Akubaba and Dreadlock Dziz within the creative network, and mentions Deriq Kissinger, Igxo and Moa Jay as wider affiliates. Current contractual status is not established by that list.</p><p>A production credit, guest verse, historical association or studio collaboration does not automatically establish an exclusive recording agreement. The records below show specific connections that listeners can follow.</p><h3>Chattarbwoy: a dancehall thread</h3>\n<p><strong>“All Da Way,” featuring Kudra Vendetta</strong>, was released on September 1, 2025. Its Apple Music listing carries the line <strong>“℗ 2025 Pyong Recordz, Ria entertainment”</strong> — a direct example of both names attached to a Chattarbwoy release. <a href=\"https://music.apple.com/us/album/all-da-way-feat-kudra-vendetta-single/1835180849\">[2]</a></p>\n<p>His five-track <em>Chattar Play</em> project extends that trail. The supplied profile dates it October 22, 2025, while Audiomack lists November 14, 2025; these dates differ, so neither is treated here as a single definitive release date. Its tracks include “Chattar a Play,” “Drop That Body,” “Maama,” “What Dem Call Me” and “Hero,” and its credit line names Pyong Recordz, Soaw Media and Ria Entertainment. <a href=\"https://audiomack.com/chattarbwoy/album/chattar-play\">[3]</a></p>\n<h3>Omunubi: a shared record and a solo release</h3>\n<p><strong>“Bampa,” featuring Chattarbwoy</strong>, links two of the artists in the same circle. Released on August 29, 2025, it credits Pyong Recordz, Soaw Media and Ria Entertainment. Omunubi’s <strong>“Terera”</strong>, dated December 12, 2025, also names Pyong and Ria; that listing spells the third partner “Saow Media.” <a href=\"https://music.apple.com/us/album/bampa-feat-chattarbwoy-single/1835190672\">[4]</a> <a href=\"https://music.apple.com/ug/album/terera-single/1857470058\">[5]</a></p>\n<p>These are useful starting points because they show both sides of the network: a collaboration between artists and a release under one artist’s own name, with the same partners recurring behind them.</p>\n<h3>Troy Mc: Cenozoic makes the connection explicit</h3>\n<p>Troy Mc’s <em>Cenozoic</em> EP arrived on <strong>June 14, 2026</strong>: six tracks, approximately twenty minutes, and the release line <strong>“℗ 2026 Pyong Recordz, Soaw Media, Ria entertainment.”</strong> The tracklist opens with “Let Him Rise,” followed by “Cenozoic” with JOHN PAPA SMYL, “Uganda Empya” with Tushi Nabakyala, “Zibuze” with Leghacity, “Couple Bae” with Joyie 7 Star and “Route 66” with Pryce Teeba. <a href=\"https://music.apple.com/ug/album/cenozoic-ep/6773058216\">Listen and view the EP credits on Apple Music.</a></p>\n<p>That is more than a passing feature credit for Troy: it is a complete project under his own name, with a collaborative cast that places him within the wider circle.</p>\n<h3>JOHN PAPA SMYL: follow the collaborations</h3>\n<p>The artist referred to as Papa John Smyl is listed on these releases as <strong>JOHN PAPA SMYL</strong>. Alongside the title track of <em>Cenozoic</em>, he appears with Troy Mc on <strong>“Boys from a Hood Near You (Part2)”</strong>, together with Pryce Teeba, St. Nelly-sade, Bemba Simba and Alpha DT. The parent album, <em>Marinate</em>, was released December 18, 2025, with Pyong Recordz, Soaw Media and Ria Entertainment credited. <a href=\"https://music.apple.com/ug/album/boys-from-a-hood-near-you-part2/1859422036?i=1859422046\">View the track and album listing.</a></p>\n<p>Those collaborations establish a public connection to the shared catalogue. They do not mean every SMYL release has the same backing: his single <em>INDEPENDENT</em>, for example, lists “℗ 2024 JOHN PAPA SMYL,” rather than Pyong and Ria. <a href=\"https://music.apple.com/au/album/independent-single/1833688878\">View that separate release credit.</a></p>\n<h3>The circle keeps widening</h3>\n<p>There are further listening routes beyond the four names above. Ambroy’s <strong>“Kibubura”</strong>, dated June 20, 2025, credits Pyong Recordz / Soaw Media / Ria Entertainment. Leghacity’s <strong>“Tushabade”</strong> with Vudú Child, dated June 4, 2026, also carries all three names. <a href=\"https://www.deezer.com/us/artist/142285812\">[6]</a> <a href=\"https://www.deezer.com/en/artist/118587382\">[7]</a></p>\n<h3>Selected discography: the wider release programme</h3><p>The supplied profile also identifies a concentrated 2025 release programme around Pryce Teeba: <em>Ebivuga</em> (July 11; seven tracks), <em>Kiwedde Mixtape</em> (August 14; fifteen tracks), <em>Kambajulire Mixtape</em> (August 14; nine tracks) and <em>Marinate</em> (December 18; fourteen tracks). Those project names and dates form part of the supplied catalogue account; <a href=\"https://www.deezer.com/en/artist/10620261\">Teeba’s public discography</a> provides a further listening reference.</p><p>Leghacity’s five-track EP is listed by Apple Music as <strong><em>Kirina Obaawo</em></strong>, dated July 15, 2025, with Pyong Recordz, Soaw Media and Ria Entertainment credited. The supplied profile also uses the spelling “Kiriba Obaawo”; this article follows the platform title for the release link. <a href=\"https://music.apple.com/in/album/kirina-obaawo-ep/1820998497\">[1]</a></p><p>The broader picture is a release programme spanning founder-led projects, artist-specific singles and collaborative work. It should not be reduced to Teeba’s catalogue alone, nor treated as an exhaustive discography.</p>\n<h3>Recognition belongs to the artists</h3><p>The official UG Hip Hop Awards winners list names <strong>Pryce Teeba as Male Rapper of the Year</strong> and <strong>Son of Aboda as Rookie of the Year for “Ayee”</strong>. These are individual artist honours, not awards won directly by Pyong Recordz. Within the ecosystem described in the supplied profile, they are signs of creative momentum rather than proof of any artist’s contractual status. <a href=\"https://www.ughha.com/\">[1]</a> Contemporary December 2025 reporting also records Teeba’s win. <a href=\"https://softpower.ug/recho-rey-pryce-teeba-win-big-at-2025-ug-hip-hop-awards/\">[2]</a></p>\n<h3>RIA, distribution and publishing administration</h3><p>The supplied organisational structure assigns RIA the role of <strong>Distribution and Publishing Administrator</strong>. Public listings repeatedly place Ria Entertainment alongside Pyong in release credits. Those two kinds of evidence should be kept distinct: the internal profile describes the mandate; shared ℗ lines establish a public credit, not the full terms of a distribution or publishing agreement.</p><p>Digital distribution concerns delivering recordings and release metadata to platforms. Publishing administration concerns compositions, songwriter information, registrations and eligible publishing income under an agreement. Neither automatically establishes ownership of the master recording. Neighbouring rights and royalty accounting are further areas requiring clear mandates and accurate records.</p><p>The actual agreements, not a platform credit alone, determine master ownership, royalty shares, recoupment, publishing scope and each party’s obligations. The supplied material does not disclose those terms or establish a corporate ownership relationship between Pyong and RIA.</p>\n<h3>Building a sustainable music company</h3><p>The structure suggests a potential operating chain: artists, A&amp;R and engineers develop recordings; legal and business teams support preparation and clearances; marketing and distribution connect the releases to audiences; finance tracks costs, income and contractual obligations. This is an organisational interpretation, not an audit of current operations.</p><p>Potential revenue routes include streaming and downloads, master licensing and synchronisation, publishing-related income where authorised, performances and bookings, brand partnerships, recording services and merchandise. These are opportunities for a company with this structure, not verified claims that Pyong currently earns from every channel.</p><p>Pyong’s stated ambition is African-rooted, contemporary and independent, with underground credibility and international reach. Its strongest profile is therefore neither a founder biography nor a static roster: it is a picture of the creative and business relationships needed to give Ugandan artists room to develop.</p><p class=\"note\">Updated October 2026 using the organisational profile supplied to 7H Music Blog, earlier label-desk information and linked public releases. Leadership and internal mandates remain attributed to the supplied profile; contractual status, ownership and private agreements are not independently established. Conflicting release dates and title spellings are noted rather than silently resolved. The supplied visual-identity section was incomplete and is not expanded here. Cover: Pryce Teeba’s official Spotify artist photo.</p>"},
-    { id: 41, slug: 'strictly-nyigos-in-the-jungle', category: 'Events', title: 'Strictly Nyigos in the Jungle: the 24-hour forest party that owns Independence Day', excerpt: 'From noon on October 9 to noon on October 10, DJ Alza\'s Strictly Nyigos turned Nyungu Yamawe Forest Park into a no-cameras, no-media dance marathon with art hung on the trees — three headliners deep, Club Pilsner behind the organising. Here is what the party is and where it came from.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: 'images/articles/strictly-nyigos-official-poster.jpg', imgPos: 'center 48%', imgCaption: 'Strictly Nyigos in the Jungle — Club Canvas event poster (supplied by Mike 1ne)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '2izHnGuv9KzbPbfX6KsoTG', title: 'Soundlykbb — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/soundlykbb/1690023792',
+    { id: 41, slug: 'strictly-nyigos-in-the-jungle', category: 'Events', title: 'Strictly Nyigos in the Jungle: the 24-hour forest party that owns Independence Day', excerpt: 'From noon on October 9 to noon on October 10, DJ Alza\'s Strictly Nyigos turned Nyungu Yamawe Forest Park into a no-cameras, no-media dance marathon with art hung on the trees — three headliners deep, Club Pilsner behind the organising. Here is what the party is and where it came from.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: 'images/articles/strictly-nyigos-official-poster.jpg', imgPos: 'center 48%', imgCaption: 'Strictly Nyigos in the Jungle — Club Canvas event poster (supplied to 7H Music Blog)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '2izHnGuv9KzbPbfX6KsoTG', title: 'Soundlykbb — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/soundlykbb/1690023792',
       body: '<p class="lead">On October 9, while the rest of the country watched parades and made speeches, Kampala\'s loudest room picked up its speakers and walked into a forest. The party was Strictly Nyigos in the Jungle — and it did not end until noon the next day.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>What it is</h3> <p>Strictly Nyigos is a dancehall-first party concept by <strong>DJ Alza</strong>, the Kampala selector who bills himself — accurately, on the available evidence — as "The Most Killa" and "Mr. Strictly Nyigos," and runs his nights under <em>The Most Killa Experience</em> banner. "Nyigos" is the whole point: Kampala slang for coupling up on the dancefloor — dancing close with the opposite sex, the way Koshen\'s "Couple Up" celebrates it. It is a two-person sport, and nobody stands around holding a drink and checking their phone. The party began as a city afternoon — bright prints, all-black crews, riddims from 2pm till late — and it grew fast: an August 2025 edition at the former Legends Sports Bar in Lugogo (USh30,000 single, USh75,000 for a squad of five) gave way to a residency at Silo 15, and then to this: an annual Independence-Day pilgrimage out of the city entirely. The 2026 jungle edition ran a full twenty-four hours — <strong>noon on Friday, October 9, to noon on Saturday, October 10</strong> — at Nyungu Yamawe Forest Park in Kitukutwe.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The venue is not a venue — it is a statement</h3> <p>Nyungu Yamawe Forest Park is part forest, part cultural hub, part open-air museum. Its cottages are named after African nationalists and freedom fighters — a deliberate choice, in the park\'s own telling, meant to nurture defiance in the face of cultural erosion — and the park itself salutes Nyungu ya Mawe, the 19th-century Nyamwezi leader who built the Ruga-ruga military force out of sheer personality. It has hosted festivals before; a 24-hour dancehall marathon is a different conversation. Hanging artwork on those trees for the party, then, is not decoration. It is a dialogue between the dancefloor and the history underneath it — paintings between the trunks, a gallery you can bassline through.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The house rules: no cameras, no under-18s</h3> <p>The flyer said it in two stamped badges: NO CAMERAS, NO MEDIA. Phones down, presence up — and even the press left their lenses at home. What happens between the trees stays between the trees, not as secrecy but as design: a room nobody is filming is a room where people dance like they mean it. The door was 18+, early-bird tickets went for USh50,000 with the gate at USh100,000, and the listing came with the one instruction every Kitukutwe event needs: plan your transport, because it is a long drive from central Kampala.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The club hand behind the trees</h3> <p>Here is what elevated the whole operation. The jungle edition was presented by <strong>Club Canvas</strong> with <strong>Club Pilsner</strong> backing the organising — the beer brand\'s muscle behind the bars, the branding and the production weight — and Strictly Nyigos itself is not a one-man-and-a-laptop affair: its residency home is <strong>Silo 15</strong>, the Bugolobi club on Bandali Rise whose calendar (Blast to the Past, Recess Wednesdays, the Sunrise Party, Silo Daycation and Strictly Nyigos among them) helped turn that street into a second name for Kampala\'s nightlife. When the club machine backs the forest edition, everything that usually goes wrong outdoors goes right: professional bar operations, tight staging and production, sound with actual low end, and security that is polite but firm. Past Silo 15 editions have stacked the booth with DJ Bugy, DJ Dash, Kashpro, Melvin Deejay, DJ Ssese, Saint Trevor, Orijinal Jenesis, Chattabwoy and Dreadlock Dziz — and even <a href="#article/joshua-baraka-recess-ii-braka-land">Joshua Baraka</a> turning up behind the decks. An outdoor party without that machine behind it is an adventure; with it, it is an institution. In the jungle, the selector marathon met live performance, with artists including <a href="#article/soundlykbb-spotlight">Soundlykbb</a> taking the forest stage.</p> <figure><img src="images/articles/strictly-nyigos-flyer.jpg" alt="Official flyer for Strictly Nyigos in the Jungle: Club Canvas presents, headlined by King Micheal, Soundlykbb and Beenie Gunter"><figcaption>The official flyer — Club Canvas presents, a Club Pilsner-backed bill three headliners deep (flyer: Club Canvas).</figcaption></figure> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The bill: three headliners deep</h3> <p>The flyer stacked the jungle edition three deep in headliners — <strong>King Micheal</strong>, <strong>Soundlykbb</strong> (in flyer style, "Sound Lyk BB") and <strong>Beenie Gunter</strong> — over an undercard that reads like a scan of the Kampala scene right now: Ziggy Dee, Ceaserous, <a href="#article/echoled-kush-after-hour">Echoled</a>, Eckostar, Kohen Jaycee, Ambroy, King Hanny, Tojo x Sean, Denesi x Cxnrvd, Biggie MC, <a href="#article/ali-boomaye-new-single">Ali Bomaye</a>, Chattar Bwoyi, CXJ Powell, Ajuuka, City Yankee (Spartan City), Vibes Liper, Gamucuwa and Mack Mick. For this blog the bill carried a home-team moment: Echoled is one of our own Fresh Finds, which meant the label desk spent half the night doing the proud head-nod in the dark.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Food, drinks, and cottages for the brave</h3> <p>A 24-hour party is a logistics problem wearing a party hat. Bars ran all night; the park\'s food cottages kept feeding the floor through the small hours; and the cottages offered proper on-site accommodation for anyone who planned ahead — or on-site collapse for anyone who didn\'t. A rolex at 3 a.m. under the trees, the organisers understand, is not food. It is infrastructure.</p> <div class="factbox"><p class="factbox-title">Strictly Nyigos, on record</p><ul><li><span class="k">Concept</span><span class="v">DJ Alza / The Most Killa Experience — "Mr. Strictly Nyigos"</span></li><li><span class="k">Residency</span><span class="v">Silo 15, Bandali Rise, Bugolobi</span></li><li><span class="k">Jungle edition</span><span class="v">Oct 9, 12:00 → Oct 10, 12:00 · Nyungu Yamawe Forest Park, Kitukutwe</span></li><li><span class="k">Presented by</span><span class="v">Club Canvas, backed by Club Pilsner</span></li><li><span class="k">Jungle bill</span><span class="v">King Micheal · Soundlykbb · Beenie Gunter + Echoled, Eckostar, Ziggy Dee, Ali Bomaye and a deep Kampala undercard</span></li><li><span class="k">House rules</span><span class="v">No cameras, no media · 18+ · early bird USh50,000, gate USh100,000</span></li><li><span class="k">Sound</span><span class="v">Dancehall first, nyigos till noon</span></li></ul></div> <p>One attendee — this blog\'s own label desk, in fact — went for the first time and came back with stories, a wrecked voice and strong opinions about rolex logistics. <a href="#article/strictly-nyigos-first-timer-diary">Read the diary here.</a></p> <p class="note">The jungle edition ran October 9-10, 2026; Silo 15 hosts Strictly Nyigos through the year — watch @deejay_alza and @Silo15_ for the next one. <em>Credit: Quicket Uganda; The RioTimes; All Events Kampala; PML Daily; Daily Monitor (Nyungu Yamawe Forest Park profile); DJ Alza and Silo 15 on X.</em></p>' },
-    { id: 42, slug: 'strictly-nyigos-first-timer-diary', category: 'Events', title: 'My first Strictly Nyigos: 24 hours in a forest where the cameras stay home', excerpt: 'I was told three things before going: wear shoes you can sacrifice, carry cash, and leave the camera at home. I obeyed two of three. Here is what Independence Day looks like when Kampala\'s dancefloor moves between the trees.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '7 min read', img: 'images/articles/strictly-nyigos-thank-you.jpg', imgPos: 'center 48%', imgCaption: 'Strictly Nyigos in the Jungle — Club Canvas thank-you artwork (supplied by Mike 1ne)', author: { name: 'Mike 1ne', role: '7H Music Group — label admin & producer', avatar: BASE + 'artist-mike-1ne.jpg' }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '2izHnGuv9KzbPbfX6KsoTG', title: 'Soundlykbb — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/soundlykbb/1690023792',
-      body: '<p class="lead">A first-person diary. On Independence Day I drove out to Nyungu Yamawe Forest Park in Kitukutwe for Strictly Nyigos in the Jungle — DJ Alza\'s 24-hour party, backed by the Silo 15 machine. What follows is what I saw, what I heard, what I ate at 3 a.m., and why I am already planning next year.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The first thing you see is not the stage</h3> <p>It\'s a painting. Then another. The jungle edition hangs artwork on the trees, so you walk in through what feels like an outdoor gallery that happens to be vibrating. Somewhere ahead, a sound system the size of a small building was doing warm-up stretches. The trees, for their part, looked thrilled to finally be holding something other than birds.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>I fought the no-cameras rule for about 40 minutes</h3> <p>My hand kept twitching toward my pocket like a phantom limb. Then the rule started doing its work: nobody was performing for a feed. The dancefloor got honest. You watched the set instead of watching yourself watching the set. By 4 p.m. I had forgotten the rule existed; by 4 a.m. I understood it was the entire point. The flyer stamped two badges — NO CAMERAS, NO MEDIA — so even the press left their lenses at home; the only coverage this party will ever get is memory, and diaries like this one written by people who were inside it. The only evidence I brought home was a wristband, a voice I found two days later, and this article — which, being text, I am choosing to believe counts as no cameras.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The sound is massive — and the forest is its cabinet</h3> <p>Bass in an open field just leaves. Bass between trees gets held, folded and returned. Every drop came back off the canopy like the forest was hyping the DJ up, and I am convinced the birds relocated out of professional respect. Somewhere behind me, a cottage named after an African nationalist was presumably rattling its picture frames in solidarity.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The crowd — and yes, the style was unreal</h3> <p>The Strictly Nyigos dress culture is real: bright prints by day, all-black crews by night, sneakers and boots for the long haul. And the name is an instruction, not a vibe: nyigos means coupling up on the dancefloor — dancing close with the opposite sex, Koshen\'s "Couple Up" style — and this floor took the definition seriously, politely but thoroughly. And the women — beautifully turned out, dancing like the forest owed them money and they had come to collect. I have covered a lot of Kampala parties for this blog. This crowd dressed like people who knew they would never be photographed and decided to look magnificent anyway, which might be the most Kampala thing I can imagine.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The moment: Soundlykbb</h3> <p>Allow me one paragraph of professional bias: Soundlykbb is my favourite Ugandan artist — we have said so on this very site — and watching him perform between the trees felt like the forest had read our coverage and decided to agree. The flyer had stacked him between King Micheal and Beenie Gunter, three headliners deep, over an undercard that included <a href="#article/echoled-kush-after-hour">Echoled</a> — one of our own Fresh Finds — which meant I spent half the night doing the proud label-admin head-nod in the dark, equal parts fan and supervisor. When he came on, the whole clearing sang back at him, Entebbe accent and all. As the label\'s admin, I spend my days building rooms for artists like this. As a fan, I got to stand in one that grew its own walls.</p> <figure><img src="images/artists/soundlykbb.jpg" alt="Soundlykbb"><figcaption>Soundlykbb — my favourite Ugandan artist, and the reason the forest lost its mind (photo: 7H Music Blog).</figcaption></figure> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Food, drinks, and my one logistical mistake</h3> <p>The bars never closed and the food cottages fed people straight through the night. I had paid the USh50,000 early-bird price and carried exactly the smugness you would expect from a man who watched the gate charge USh100,000 — latecomers turning into instant economists at the door is a Kampala tradition of its own. A rolex at 3 a.m. under the trees is not food — it is infrastructure, and I want it on record that I respected it deeply. The cottages offered proper beds. I did not book one. At around 5 a.m., watching people walk calmly to their rooms while I entered negotiations with my own legs, I learned the lesson the only way anyone ever learns it: <em>book a cottage, or make a friend who did.</em> I did the second one, eventually. He is reading this. Thank you, brother.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>What the club\'s hand changed</h3> <p>The flyer said Club Canvas presents, with Club Pilsner backing the organising, and you could feel that muscle everywhere: bars that never ran dry, branding that survived the mist, production that stayed tight for twenty-four hours. Underneath it sat the party\'s real home — Strictly Nyigos lives at Silo 15 on Bandali Rise the rest of the year — and its fingerprints were just as clear, down to security that was polite but absolutely firm. That backing is the difference between "somebody\'s idea" and a party that can run from one Independence Day noon to the next without losing the plot. As someone who organises things for a living, I was taking notes between songs.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Dawn, October 10</h3> <p>Mist through the canopy. Birds attempting a comeback and losing to a bassline. The artworks still hanging there like they had been part of the forest all along. Uganda turned 64, and honestly, the freest room in the country had trees for walls. The party was scheduled to close at noon; my personal closing time was somewhat earlier and is nobody\'s business.</p> <p>First Strictly Nyigos: survived, outdanced, spiritually rearranged. What happens between the trees stays between the trees — but if you want the full picture of what this party actually is, <a href="#article/strictly-nyigos-in-the-jungle">read the explainer</a>, and press play on Soundlykbb above to hear what the forest sounded like. Somebody wake me when it is October 2027.</p> <p class="note">A first-person diary by Mike 1ne, label administrator at 7H Music Group. Event details verified against the Quicket Uganda listing. <em>Credit: Quicket Uganda; The RioTimes; PML Daily.</em></p>' },
