     { id: 35, slug: 'mr-fallback-jennifer', category: 'Press Release', title: 'Mr Fallback drops new single "Jennifer"', excerpt: 'Two weeks after "Kiwatto", 7H\'s Afro-pop hitmaker is back with "Jennifer" — a love song built for the dancefloor, out now on all platforms.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '3 min read', img: 'images/covers/jennifer.jpg', imgCaption: 'The official "Jennifer" cover art — 2Puff Records', author: { name: '7H Music Group', role: 'Press Office', avatar: BASE + 'artist-mr-fallback.jpg' }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '4Vb1RrTyRhPfnJfiwNX9Rh', title: 'Jennifer — Mr Fallback' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/us/album/jennifer-single/6822713688',
       body: '<p class="lead">Mr Fallback has a new name on his lips — and it\'s <em>Jennifer</em>.</p><figure><img src=\"images/covers/jennifer.jpg\" alt=\"Jennifer — Mr Fallback official cover art\"><figcaption>The official "Jennifer" cover art — 2Puff Records</figcaption></figure><p>7H Music Group\'s Afro-pop and Afrobeats hitmaker returns with <em>Jennifer</em>, his new single, out now on Spotify, Apple Music and all platforms. It lands barely two weeks after <a href="#article/mr-fallback-kiwatto"><em>Kiwatto</em></a>, keeping up the release pace that has defined his 2026 — <em>Abeyoo</em> with Sopreno C, then <em>Kiwatto</em>, and now this.</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>A love song for the dancefloor</h3><p>Where <em>Kiwatto</em> was all bounce, <em>Jennifer</em> leans into melody: a dedication record that still moves. It is the sweet spot Mr Fallback has been working toward since <em>Ndi Solidi Mu</em> — warm, hook-led, dance-ready, and unmistakably East African.</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>The 2Puff Records partnership</h3><p><em>Jennifer</em> is also a story about how the record reached the world. The single carries the imprint of <strong>2Puff Records</strong>, the label Mr Fallback partnered with to bring the song to life — its branding sits at the very top of the official cover art, above the title itself. The collaboration pairs 2Puff\'s release push with the in-house 7H sound built by <a href="#article/meet-mike-1ne">Mike 1ne</a>, putting two Kampala imprints behind one record instead of one. For a label family that has self-released every Mr Fallback single to date, from <em>Ndi Solidi Mu</em> to <a href="#article/mr-fallback-kiwatto"><em>Kiwatto</em></a>, it is a new chapter in how his music travels: the same in-house writing and production, now backed by a partner whose name is stamped on the artwork.</p><blockquote>"Every song I put out this year has been a step. Jennifer is the one I\'ve been waiting to share."<cite>Mr Fallback</cite></blockquote><p>Stream <em>Jennifer</em> now using the buttons above, and follow <a href="#artists">Mr Fallback on the 7H roster</a> for what comes next. <em>Credit: 7H Music Group press office.</em></p>' },
     { id: 36, slug: 'quency-jus-sayin-freestyle-spotlight', category: 'Interview', title: 'Fresh Finds: Quency, the Kampala rapper who freestyled his way onto our radar', excerpt: 'His "Jus Sayin\'" freestyle pulled in Pryce Teeba and put a new name in the UG hip-hop conversation. Here is why Quency belongs in your rotation.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '4 min read', img: 'images/artists/quency.jpg', imgPos: 'center 25%', imgCaption: 'Quency (photo: Instagram @quency_4sho)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '2fNmYzBomojUZJYuwrPHfC', title: "Jus Sayin' (Freestyle) — Quency feat. Pryce Teeba" }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/us/album/jus-sayin-freestyle-feat-pryce-teeba-single/6768497351',
@@ -206,26 +374,25 @@
       body: '<p class="lead">ECEE raps like someone who has had to build his own road — because, from Kitgum, he has.</p><p>Lubangakene Emmy — born November 8, 1997 in Kitgum, northern Uganda — writes, sings and produces as ECEE: a rapper blending hip-hop and trap with Afro-influenced melody, independent on his own Ecee UG imprint. He first caught attention with "my name", and has kept stacking since: the <em>Reborn</em> EP (2022), <em>Broke Boyz</em> (2023), <em>More</em> (2024), the <em>Concysson</em> EP (2025), and a 2026 run that takes in <em>Game Over</em>, <em>Ug Funk</em> and <em>Rather Be</em>.</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>The sound</h3><p>Spotify files him under hip-hop/rap, but the catalogue refuses a single lane. "Kitgum" names the hometown and carries its weight; "Whassup" and "Win" work the club side; <em>Rather Be</em> (June 12, 2026) leans into melody without losing the bars. His own description of the method is plain — raw, honest lyrics drawn from personal experience and from the struggles and triumphs of the people around him, carried by a flow that switches between rap and singing mid-thought.</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>Focus: two Fresh Finds, one record</h3><p>The most telling release of his 2026 is <em>Focus</em> (September 4, 2026) — a collaboration with <a href="#article/yuko-neo-soul-spotlight">YUKO</a>, the neo-soul voice in this same Fresh Finds round. Put the two careers side by side and the record reads like a scene map: northern Uganda rap meeting Ugandan-born, Bruges-raised soul, both independent, both writing their own credits. Before that, his feature run had already widened the frame — Timcence\'s "Iloko Kato" (2025) among a stack of guest verses across the Kampala underground.</p><blockquote>"His lyrics are raw, honest, and powerful — messages of hope, resilience, and strength."<cite>ECEE, artist biography</cite></blockquote><p>Start with "Kitgum", then <em>Rather Be</em>, then <em>Focus</em> — and watch the north-south axis of this scene tighten. Stream with the buttons above, and follow his releases in the grid below. <em>Credit: Spotify; Apple Music; SoundCloud; YouTube.</em></p>' },
     { id: 39, slug: 'yuko-neo-soul-spotlight', category: 'Interview', title: 'Fresh Finds: YUKO, the neo-soul voice carrying Uganda to Bruges and back', excerpt: '"Pourin", "Ba Da Da", "MURDA" and "Kingdom" with Mike Mungu — the Ugandan-born, Bruges-raised singer who built her own genre and her own label, YUKOLAND.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '4 min read', img: PHOTO('9d95200373e871a5651e8ca2'), imgPos: 'center 25%', imgCaption: 'YUKO (photo: Spotify artist profile)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '20AESzvXafH4xLWjicmKLV', title: 'YUKO — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/us/artist/yuko/1772291652',
       body: '<p class="lead">YUKO does not want to be put in a box — and the catalogue proves she never has been.</p><p>Born in Uganda and raised in Bruges, Belgium from the age of seven, YUKO writes between reggae, neo-soul, Afrobeats and R&amp;B, releasing through her own YUKOLAND imprint. Her own words frame it best: music was her only refuge at the point when everything else let her down — and she writes in the hope of being that for someone else.</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>The run</h3><p>It starts with <em>I NEED YO LOVE</em> (September 2024, filed under reggae), then <em>Eden</em> and the three-track <em>Sweet Poison Kisses</em> in early 2025, then <em>Pourin</em> (June 20, 2025) — her first self-directed video — and <em>Ba Da Da</em> (September 2025), her most-streamed record to date. 2026 sharpens the picture: <em>MURDA</em> with Timo Kaliisa (March 13), about wanting love without ever begging for it, and <em>Kingdom</em> with <a href="#article/mike-mungu-new-project">Mike Mungu</a> (June 9, on PALAGROOVE / RAIN. LABS) — a duet that puts her beside one of Kampala\'s most-listened-to R&amp;B voices.</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>The features and the homecoming</h3><p>Her voice travels further than her own discography: Tojo\'s "We Yu De" with CxJ Powell (December 2025) has become one of the scene\'s biggest crossover records, and Spotify\'s own recommendation engine files her next to <a href="#article/joka-sky-blue-to-no-condition">Joka</a> and <a href="#article/amanya-mosquitoes-alte-rnb">Amanya</a>. In March 2026 she brought it home with a Kampala show at Khulted — the first of what should be many returns between Bruges and the city she writes about from both sides.</p><blockquote>"Music was my only refuge at one point — the only thing I could hold onto that wouldn\'t let me down. I write music in the hopes of being that for someone else."<cite>YUKO</cite></blockquote><p>Start with <em>Pourin</em>, then <em>Ba Da Da</em>, then <em>Kingdom</em> — and note her side of <em>Focus</em> with <a href="#article/ecee-kitgum-focus-spotlight">ECEE</a> in this same round. Stream with the buttons above. <em>Credit: Spotify; Apple Music; VI.BE.</em></p>' },
-    {"id":40,"slug":"aumex-artist-accelerator-conference","category":"Industry Insights","title":"AUMEX: the expo building Uganda's music business from the inside","excerpt":"A 12-week Artist Accelerator, an ARMC Johannesburg delegation, a Nasty C-headlined showcase behind it and a conference in Kampala on November 14 — how Amplify Ugandan Music Expo is turning talent into infrastructure.","date":"Oct 16, 2026","readTime":"5 min read","img":"images/articles/aumex-2026-brand-poster.jpg","imgCaption":"AUMEX — Amplify Uganda Music Expo, 2026 artwork (supplied by Mike 1ne)","author":{"name":"7H Music Blog","role":"Contributor","avatar":"images/brand/7h-avatar.svg"},"listen":{"type":"artist","id":"2gzWmhOZhDN6gXL49JW9qj","title":"Nasty C — top tracks"},"apple":"https://music.apple.com/us/artist/nasty-c/1041347781","body":"<p class=\"lead\">\"The talent has never been the problem. The infrastructure has.\" AUMEX has built its entire programme around that sentence.</p><figure><img src=\"images/articles/aumex-2026-brand-poster.jpg\" alt=\"Purple and yellow AUMEX 2026 artwork reading Amplify Uganda Music Expo\"><figcaption>AUMEX 2026 brand artwork, supplied by Mike 1ne.</figcaption></figure><h3>Women in Music Retreat 2026</h3><p>The supplied AUMEX Women in Music Retreat poster announced September 25–27, 2026 in Kampala, Uganda, under the themes <strong>creation, mentorship and connection</strong>. It described three days of collaboration, learning and creative exchange for women in music from across Africa, with support from Institut français. These are the details advertised on the poster, not a report of attendance or outcomes.</p><figure><img src=\"images/articles/aumex-women-in-music-retreat-2026.jpg\" alt=\"AUMEX Women in Music Retreat 2026 poster: September 25–27 in Kampala, Uganda; creation, mentorship and connection\"><figcaption>Women in Music Retreat 2026 — September 25–27, Kampala. Poster supplied by Mike 1ne.</figcaption></figure><p>Amplify Ugandan Music Expo — AUMEX — is the Kampala organisation founded by <strong>Abaasa Rwemereza</strong>, a Berklee College of Music graduate who studies, builds and teaches the music economy in East Africa. Its pitch is structural rather than promotional: a <strong>12-week Artist Accelerator Programme</strong> that teaches emerging artists the business side of the craft — contracts, publishing, catalogues, rights — and then puts them in the rooms where that knowledge gets used.</p><h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The showcase that set the bar: Nasty C, 2025</h3><p>The expo's second edition, on November 7, 2025 at MOTIV Kampala, put the whole idea in one room: a free conference day under the theme \"Building a Scalable Future for Ugandan Music\", with panels on artist development, royalties and metadata, and a global touring strategy session — the last of them featuring Nasty C's own manager, Ayanda Ngcobo — before the showcase turned the hall over to the scene's emerging names: Son of Aboda, Vini Mercy, Tungi, Echoled, Expect Xhavi and DJ City Girl.</p><p>Then the headliner. South African rap star <strong>Nasty C</strong> closed the night as part of his <strong>Ivyson Tour</strong> — the run behind his 2025 album <em>FREE</em>, which went to number one in Uganda on its way to charting in 44 countries, and made him the first South African artist to top charts in ten territories. Billed as the largest hip-hop tour an African rapper has staged, at 11 countries and 18 stops, it had exactly one East African date — Kampala, unlocked by fans through the ticket-pledge campaign that routed the whole run. He took the stage at 11pm for \"Strings and Bling\", \"Soft\" and \"SMA\" and stayed behind afterwards to meet the crowd, and the night became the reference point for everything the expo has done since.</p><h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The accelerator and the ARMC delegation</h3><p>In May 2026 that work went continental. At the Africa Rising Music Conference (ARMC) in Johannesburg — sixth edition, May 22-23 at Constitution Hill, with Uganda named a focus country alongside Australia — the Ugandan delegation was led by AUMEX and built from the accelerator's second cohort: <strong>Mbeewo, Vini Mercy, <a href=\"#article/amanya-mosquitoes-alte-rnb\">Amanya</a>, Matt Travers and <a href=\"#article/nana-nyadia-afro-fusion-spotlight\">Nana Nyadia</a></strong>, with Vini Mercy performing at the official showcase. The delegation also sat down with Downtown Music Publishing Africa for one-on-one sessions on managing catalogues and intellectual property — precisely the gap AUMEX was founded to close.</p><h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">What comes next: Kampala, November 14</h3><p>The follow-up lands at home. The <strong>AUMEX Conference</strong> is slated for <strong>November 14, 2026 in Kampala</strong> — it sits in our <a href=\"#article/kampala-live-season-chameleone-levixone-mesach\">live-season calendar</a> — carrying the same conversations about market access, mobility and export onto Ugandan soil. The partnership circle keeps widening: the Alliance Française de Kampala has hosted the programme's sessions, and the expo's orbit now draws from across the scene's label map rather than one camp — <a href=\"#article/amanya-mosquitoes-alte-rnb\">Amanya</a> and the independent cohort around Mbeewo and Matt Travers on one side, the Party Wright Records / Himaya Music axis behind <a href=\"#article/ayma-gone-ass-titude\">AYMA</a> on the other. <a href=\"#article/nana-nyadia-afro-fusion-spotlight\">Nana Nyadia</a>, another 2026 delegate, has since left Party Wright Records to release independently.</p><div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">AUMEX, on record</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Founder</span><span class=\"v\">Abaasa Rwemereza — Berklee College of Music, Kampala</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Programme</span><span class=\"v\">12-week Artist Accelerator Programme (contracts, publishing, rights)</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">ARMC 2026</span><span class=\"v\">Johannesburg, May 22-23 — Uganda focus country; second-cohort delegation</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">2025 edition</span><span class=\"v\">November 7, 2025 — MOTIV Kampala; <a href=\"#article/aumex-nasty-c-showcase-2025\">Nasty C headlined</a> the showcase (Ivyson Tour)</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Conference</span><span class=\"v\">November 14, 2026 — Kampala</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Partners</span><span class=\"v\">Alliance Française de Kampala; Downtown Music Publishing Africa (sessions)</span></li></ul></div><p>For a scene that exports talent more easily than it exports ownership, that distinction is the whole game. The 2025 showcase proved the expo could put a continental headliner on a Kampala stage; the 2026 conference has to prove the same rooms can produce the next one. <em>Credit: New Vision (June 18, 2026); Nile Post; Pulse Uganda; Sqoop; MBU; PML Daily; Sanyu FM; Billboard Africa; Music In Africa; Beats of Africa; AUMEX; Talent Africa Group.</em></p>","imgPos":"center 38%"},
-    {"id":43,"slug":"aumex-nasty-c-showcase-2025","category":"News","title":"The night Nasty C headlined AUMEX: FREE, the Ivyson Tour and the only East African stop","excerpt":"Before the November 14 conference, a look back at the expo's second edition: MOTIV Kampala, November 7, 2025, an emerging-talent showcase — and a headline set by Nasty C, the only East African stop on his Ivyson Tour for the album FREE.","date":"Oct 16, 2026","readTime":"5 min read","img":"images/articles/aumex-nasty-c-ivyson-poster-2025.jpg","imgCaption":"Nasty C — AUMEX Showcase / Ivyson Tour, November 7, 2025 promotional poster (supplied by Mike 1ne)","author":{"name":"7H Music Blog","role":"Contributor","avatar":"images/brand/7h-avatar.svg"},"listen":{"type":"artist","id":"2gzWmhOZhDN6gXL49JW9qj","title":"Nasty C — top tracks"},"apple":"https://music.apple.com/us/artist/nasty-c/1041347781","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Amplify Ugandan Music Expo is not only a conference — and the clearest proof came at the end of its second edition, when a South African rap star walked on stage at MOTIV Kampala at 11pm.</p><figure><img src=\"images/articles/aumex-nasty-c-ivyson-poster-2025.jpg\" alt=\"Red Nasty C Ivyson Tour poster for the AUMEX Showcase in Kampala, November 7, 2025 at MOTIV, Plot 7 Old Port Bell Road\"><figcaption>The promotional poster bills Nasty C for the AUMEX Showcase at MOTIV on November 7, 2025, presented by Talent Africa Group and AUMEX. Supplied by Mike 1ne.</figcaption></figure> <p>On November 7, 2025, AUMEX took over the Bugolobi venue under the theme \"Building a Scalable Future for Ugandan Music\": a free, day-long conference covering artist development, royalties and metadata — Blessing Manafa (OneRPM), Motolani Alake (Virgin Music Group), Geoffrey Ekongot (Uganda Musicians Association) and David Tayebwa (Josplay) on the money side — plus a global touring strategy session with Jenny Tan (TOORLY), Ali Alibhai (Talent Africa Group) and Ayanda Ngcobo, who manages Nasty C. The day opened with a UgaTunes demo led by CEO Shadrack Kisame and ran a Ssese Nation workshop for aspiring DJs. The expo was staged with Talent Africa Group and backed by Guinness Smooth and its \"Make It Yours\" campaign.</p> <p>At 7pm the programme gave way to the <strong>AUMEX Showcase</strong>, the part of the night built for names still on the way up: <a href=\"#article/tungi-bebe-to-life-of-sound\">Tungi</a>, <a href=\"#article/echoled-kush-after-hour\">Echoled</a>, Vini Mercy, Son of Aboda, Expect Xhavi and DJ City Girl. It is the same idea the expo's Artist Accelerator is built on — put emerging talent in a room that includes the people who can move their careers — and this audience stayed for what came next.</p> <h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The headline: Nasty C and the Ivyson Tour</h3> <p>Nasty C closed the show as part of his <strong>Ivyson Tour</strong>, the run behind <em>FREE</em>. Released on September 30, 2025, the album went to number one in Uganda on its way to charting in 44 countries, and made him the first South African artist to top charts in ten territories. The tour around it was billed as the largest hip-hop run an African rapper has staged — 11 countries, 18 stops — and it routed itself through fan pledges: cities campaigned to unlock their own date. Kampala made it through to the final round and became the tour's only East African stop.</p> <p>He took the stage around 11pm and worked through \"Strings and Bling\", \"Soft\" and \"SMA\", the crowd carrying the hooks back at him; afterwards he stayed to meet fans, a detail the local press picked up. For a city that gets plenty of continental stars on a fly-in basis, this one landed differently — the fans had done the work to bring it, and the artist treated the date like more than a stop on the map.</p> <h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Why it still matters</h3> <p>The showcase matters beyond the night itself because AUMEX's whole pitch is that Uganda exports talent far more easily than it exports ownership: the gap is infrastructure — contracts, publishing, catalogues, market access — and here was one stage holding both halves at once, a continental headliner on one side and six Ugandan names on the other, in a room full of people who broker those deals. <a href=\"#article/aumex-artist-accelerator-conference\">This year's programme</a>, from the 12-week Artist Accelerator to the ARMC Johannesburg delegation, is the follow-up.</p> <p>The <strong>AUMEX Conference</strong> returns to Kampala on <strong>November 14, 2026</strong>, and sits in our <a href=\"#article/kampala-live-season-chameleone-levixone-mesach\">live-season calendar</a>. Whoever headlines the next showcase has a hard act to follow — and, on the evidence of 2025, a good reason to take the call.</p> <div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">The AUMEX 2025 showcase, on record</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Conference</span><span class=\"v\">November 7, 2025 — MOTIV Kampala, Bugolobi (free entry)</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Theme</span><span class=\"v\">\"Building a Scalable Future for Ugandan Music\"</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Showcase</span><span class=\"v\">Son of Aboda · Vini Mercy · Tungi · Echoled · Expect Xhavi · DJ City Girl</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Headliner</span><span class=\"v\">Nasty C — Ivyson Tour, on stage at 11pm</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Partners</span><span class=\"v\">Talent Africa Group; Guinness Smooth (\"Make It Yours\")</span></li></ul></div> <p class=\"note\">Next: the AUMEX Conference, November 14 in Kampala. <em>Credit: Nile Post; Pulse Uganda; Sqoop; MBU; PML Daily; Voice of Uganda; Sanyu FM; MyJoyOnline; AUMEX; Talent Africa Group; Apple Music; Spotify.</em></p>","imgPos":"center 40%"},
-    {"id":27,"slug":"nyege-nyege-2026-wakaliwood-edition-preview","category":"News","title":"Nyege Nyege 2026: a river, seven stages and a live film set","excerpt":"Uganda's flagship festival returns to Kalagala Falls on November 19-22 with 200-plus artists, a Wakaliwood collaboration, the Reggae and Boiler Room stages back, and a conference week leading in.","date":"Oct 16, 2026","readTime":"5 min read","img":"images/articles/nyege-nyege-wakaliwood-poster-2026.jpg","imgCaption":"Nyege Nyege XI — Wakaliwood edition poster, November 19–22, 2026 (supplied by Mike 1ne)","author":{"name":"7H Music Blog","role":"Editorial","avatar":"images/brand/7h-avatar.svg"},"listen":{"type":"artist","id":"3JQTIErs7TXtmo3HIv3yJa","title":"King Saha — top tracks"},"apple":"https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/king-saha/805432891","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Nyege Nyege is going back to the river — and this year it is taking a film crew with it.</p><figure><img src=\"images/articles/nyege-nyege-wakaliwood-poster-2026.jpg\" alt=\"Wakaliwood presents Nyege Nyege XI action-packed festival poster: November 19–22, 2026 at Nyege Nyege Festival Grounds, Kalagala Falls, Uganda\"><figcaption>The supplied Nyege Nyege XI poster advertises November 19–22, 2026 at Kalagala Falls, Uganda, with the theme “The festival becomes the movie set.” Artwork supplied by Mike 1ne.</figcaption></figure> <p>Uganda's flagship festival returns to Adrift Overland Camp at Kalagala Falls, near Jinja, for its 11th edition on <strong>November 19-22</strong>: more than 200 artists across seven stages, four days and three nights on the Nile. Tickets went live in September at a launch held at the Talent Africa rooftop in Kampala, where organisers confirmed the riverside site as the festival's permanent home and set out a wider programme around the music — film, fashion, visual art, theatre, comedy and food.</p> <h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The Wakaliwood Edition</h3> <p>This year's theme is a collaboration with <strong>Wakaliwood</strong>, the DIY action-film studio from Wakaliga that has spent two decades turning Kampala suburbs into action epics. Parts of the festival site will become a live film set, with scenes shot across the four days — a first dedicated film component, and a natural pairing for two Ugandan projects that built international audiences from fiercely independent roots.</p> <h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">What is back, and what is new</h3> <p>The Reggae Stage and the Boiler Room both return, alongside dedicated Kenyan and Rwandan takeover showcases and the SO UG Stage powered by Uganda Waragi, which keeps its focus on contemporary Ugandan popular culture. Names announced for the main stage so far include <strong>King Saha</strong>, <strong>Jackie Chandiru</strong>, <strong>Linus</strong>, <strong>Abeeka Band</strong>, <strong>Etania</strong>, <strong>DJ Akio</strong> of Strictly Soul and the Zonke Africa DJ Collective. Uganda Waragi's maker, Uganda Breweries Limited, renewed its partnership on a three-year agreement, and a Ministry of Tourism tie-in under the Explore Uganda brand links the festival to regional travel packages and circuit destinations.</p> <p>Before the gates open, the second <strong>African Electronic Music Conference</strong> runs from November 17 to 19 in Jinja, turning festival week into a full gathering for the continent's electronic scene. In the weeks before that, warm-up parties travel to Nairobi and Kigali, hosted by Sheila Gashumba and Kenya's Azeezah Hashim.</p> <div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">Nyege Nyege 2026</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">When</span><span class=\"v\">November 19-22, 2026</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Where</span><span class=\"v\">Adrift Overland Camp, Kalagala Falls, Jinja</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Scale</span><span class=\"v\">200+ artists · 7 stages · riverside camping for ~1,000</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Early bird</span><span class=\"v\">UGX 100,000 \"Die Hard\" pass, ended September 30</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Tickets</span><span class=\"v\">festival.nyegenyege.com — official sellers MTN MoMo and Tag Ticketing</span></li></ul></div> <p>It is worth remembering what the festival is worth where it happens: a government report released in 2025 put the 2024 edition's contribution to Jinja's economy at about <strong>USD 1.84 million (UGX 6.62 billion)</strong>. With camping expanded and tourism packages attached, the 2026 edition is being built to push that further.</p> <p class=\"note\">Check the official site for current ticket tiers and the full line-up — names are still being announced. <em>Credit: Nyege Nyege Festival; SoftPower News; Daily Monitor; Travel PR News; RioTimes.</em></p>","imgPos":"center 43%"},
