n Music Expo — AUMEX — is the Kampala organisation founded by <strong>Abaasa Rwemereza</strong>, a Berklee College of Music graduate who studies, builds and teaches the music economy in East Africa. Its pitch is structural rather than promotional: a <strong>12-week Artist Accelerator Programme</strong> that teaches emerging artists the business side of the craft — contracts, publishing, catalogues, rights — and then puts them in the rooms where that knowledge gets used.</p><h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The showcase that set the bar: Nasty C, 2025</h3><p>The expo's second edition, on November 7, 2025 at MOTIV Kampala, put the whole idea in one room: a free conference day under the theme \"Building a Scalable Future for Ugandan Music\", with panels on artist development, royalties and metadata, and a global touring strategy session — the last of them featuring Nasty C's own manager, Ayanda Ngcobo — before the showcase turned the hall over to the scene's emerging names: Son of Aboda, Vini Mercy, Tungi, Echoled, Expect Xhavi and DJ City Girl.</p><p>Then the headliner. South African rap star <strong>Nasty C</strong> closed the night as part of his <strong>Ivyson Tour</strong> — the run behind his 2025 album <em>FREE</em>, which went to number one in Uganda on its way to charting in 44 countries, and made him the first South African artist to top charts in ten territories. Billed as the largest hip-hop tour an African rapper has staged, at 11 countries and 18 stops, it had exactly one East African date — Kampala, unlocked by fans through the ticket-pledge campaign that routed the whole run. He took the stage at 11pm for \"Strings and Bling\", \"Soft\" and \"SMA\" and stayed behind afterwards to meet the crowd, and the night became the reference point for everything the expo has done since.</p><h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The accelerator and the ARMC delegation</h3><p>In May 2026 that work went continental. At the Africa Rising Music Conference (ARMC) in Johannesburg — sixth edition, May 22-23 at Constitution Hill, with Uganda named a focus country alongside Australia — the Ugandan delegation was led by AUMEX and built from the accelerator's second cohort: <strong>Mbeewo, Vini Mercy, <a href=\"#article/amanya-mosquitoes-alte-rnb\">Amanya</a>, Matt Travers and <a href=\"#article/nana-nyadia-afro-fusion-spotlight\">Nana Nyadia</a></strong>, with Vini Mercy performing at the official showcase. The delegation also sat down with Downtown Music Publishing Africa for one-on-one sessions on managing catalogues and intellectual property — precisely the gap AUMEX was founded to close.</p><h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">What comes next: Kampala, November 14</h3><p>The follow-up lands at home. The <strong>AUMEX Conference</strong> is slated for <strong>November 14, 2026 in Kampala</strong> — it sits in our <a href=\"#article/kampala-live-season-chameleone-levixone-mesach\">live-season calendar</a> — carrying the same conversations about market access, mobility and export onto Ugandan soil. The partnership circle keeps widening: the Alliance Française de Kampala has hosted the programme's sessions, and the expo's orbit now draws from across the scene's label map rather than one camp — <a href=\"#article/amanya-mosquitoes-alte-rnb\">Amanya</a> and the independent cohort around Mbeewo and Matt Travers on one side, the Party Wright Records / Himaya Music axis behind <a href=\"#article/ayma-gone-ass-titude\">AYMA</a> on the other. <a href=\"#article/nana-nyadia-afro-fusion-spotlight\">Nana Nyadia</a>, another 2026 delegate, has since left Party Wright Records to release independently.</p><div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">AUMEX, on record</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Founder</span><span class=\"v\">Abaasa Rwemereza — Berklee College of Music, Kampala</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Programme</span><span class=\"v\">12-week Artist Ac