+    {"id":43,"slug":"aumex-nasty-c-showcase-2025","category":"News","title":"The night Nasty C headlined AUMEX: FREE, the Ivyson Tour and the only East African stop","excerpt":"Before the November 14 conference, a look back at the expo's second edition: MOTIV Kampala, November 7, 2025, an emerging-talent showcase — and a headline set by Nasty C, the only East African stop on his Ivyson Tour for the album FREE.","date":"Oct 16, 2026","readTime":"5 min read","img":"images/articles/aumex-nasty-c-ivyson-poster-2025.jpg","imgCaption":"Nasty C — AUMEX Showcase / Ivyson Tour, November 7, 2025 promotional poster (supplied to 7H Music Blog)","author":{"name":"7H Music Blog","role":"Contributor","avatar":"images/brand/7h-avatar.svg"},"listen":{"type":"artist","id":"2gzWmhOZhDN6gXL49JW9qj","title":"Nasty C — top tracks"},"apple":"https://music.apple.com/us/artist/nasty-c/1041347781","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Amplify Ugandan Music Expo is not only a conference — and the clearest proof came at the end of its second edition, when a South African rap star walked on stage at MOTIV Kampala at 11pm.</p><figure><img src=\"images/articles/aumex-nasty-c-ivyson-poster-2025.jpg\" alt=\"Red Nasty C Ivyson Tour poster for the AUMEX Showcase in Kampala, November 7, 2025 at MOTIV, Plot 7 Old Port Bell Road\"><figcaption>The promotional poster bills Nasty C for the AUMEX Showcase at MOTIV on November 7, 2025, presented by Talent Africa Group and AUMEX. Supplied to 7H Music Blog.</figcaption></figure> <p>On November 7, 2025, AUMEX took over the Bugolobi venue under the theme \"Building a Scalable Future for Ugandan Music\": a free, day-long conference covering artist development, royalties and metadata — Blessing Manafa (OneRPM), Motolani Alake (Virgin Music Group), Geoffrey Ekongot (Uganda Musicians Association) and David Tayebwa (Josplay) on the money side — plus a global touring strategy session with Jenny Tan (TOORLY), Ali Alibhai (Talent Africa Group) and Ayanda Ngcobo, who manages Nasty C. The day opened with a UgaTunes demo led by CEO Shadrack Kisame and ran a Ssese Nation workshop for aspiring DJs. The expo was staged with Talent Africa Group and backed by Guinness Smooth and its \"Make It Yours\" campaign.</p> <p>At 7pm the programme gave way to the <strong>AUMEX Showcase</strong>, the part of the night built for names still on the way up: <a href=\"#article/tungi-bebe-to-life-of-sound\">Tungi</a>, <a href=\"#article/echoled-kush-after-hour\">Echoled</a>, Vini Mercy, Son of Aboda, Expect Xhavi and DJ City Girl. It is the same idea the expo's Artist Accelerator is built on — put emerging talent in a room that includes the people who can move their careers — and this audience stayed for what came next.</p> <h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The headline: Nasty C and the Ivyson Tour</h3> <p>Nasty C closed the show as part of his <strong>Ivyson Tour</strong>, the run behind <em>FREE</em>. Released on September 30, 2025, the album went to number one in Uganda on its way to charting in 44 countries, and made him the first South African artist to top charts in ten territories. The tour around it was billed as the largest hip-hop run an African rapper has staged — 11 countries, 18 stops — and it routed itself through fan pledges: cities campaigned to unlock their own date. Kampala made it through to the final round and became the tour's only East African stop.</p> <p>He took the stage around 11pm and worked through \"Strings and Bling\", \"Soft\" and \"SMA\", the crowd carrying the hooks back at him; afterwards he stayed to meet fans, a detail the local press picked up. For a city that gets plenty of continental stars on a fly-in basis, this one landed differently — the fans had done the work to bring it, and the artist treated the date like more than a stop on the map.</p> <h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Why it still matters</h3> <p>The showcase matters beyond the night itself because AUMEX's whole pitch is that Uganda exports talent far more easily than it exports ownership: the gap is infrastructure — contracts, publishing, catalogues, market access — and here was one stage holding both halves at once, a continental headliner on one side and six Ugandan names on the other, in a room full of people who broker those deals. <a href=\"#article/aumex-artist-accelerator-conference\">This year's programme</a>, from the 12-week Artist Accelerator to the ARMC Johannesburg delegation, is the follow-up.</p> <p>The <strong>AUMEX Conference</strong> returns to Kampala on <strong>November 14, 2026</strong>, and sits in our <a href=\"#article/kampala-live-season-chameleone-levixone-mesach\">live-season calendar</a>. Whoever headlines the next showcase has a hard act to follow — and, on the evidence of 2025, a good reason to take the call.</p> <div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">The AUMEX 2025 showcase, on record</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Conference</span><span class=\"v\">November 7, 2025 — MOTIV Kampala, Bugolobi (free entry)</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Theme</span><span class=\"v\">\"Building a Scalable Future for Ugandan Music\"</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Showcase</span><span class=\"v\">Son of Aboda · Vini Mercy · Tungi · Echoled · Expect Xhavi · DJ City Girl</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Headliner</span><span class=\"v\">Nasty C — Ivyson Tour, on stage at 11pm</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Partners</span><span class=\"v\">Talent Africa Group; Guinness Smooth (\"Make It Yours\")</span></li></ul></div> <p class=\"note\">Next: the AUMEX Conference, November 14 in Kampala. <em>Credit: Nile Post; Pulse Uganda; Sqoop; MBU; PML Daily; Voice of Uganda; Sanyu FM; MyJoyOnline; AUMEX; Talent Africa Group; Apple Music; Spotify.</em></p>","imgPos":"center 40%"},
+    {"id":27,"slug":"nyege-nyege-2026-wakaliwood-edition-preview","category":"News","title":"Nyege Nyege 2026: a river, seven stages and a live film set","excerpt":"Uganda's flagship festival returns to Kalagala Falls on November 19-22 with 200-plus artists, a Wakaliwood collaboration, the Reggae and Boiler Room stages back, and a conference week leading in.","date":"Oct 16, 2026","readTime":"5 min read","img":"images/articles/nyege-nyege-wakaliwood-poster-2026.jpg","imgCaption":"Nyege Nyege XI — Wakaliwood edition poster, November 19–22, 2026 (supplied to 7H Music Blog)","author":{"name":"7H Music Blog","role":"Editorial","avatar":"images/brand/7h-avatar.svg"},"listen":{"type":"artist","id":"3JQTIErs7TXtmo3HIv3yJa","title":"King Saha — top tracks"},"apple":"https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/king-saha/805432891","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Nyege Nyege is going back to the river — and this year it is taking a film crew with it.</p><figure><img src=\"images/articles/nyege-nyege-wakaliwood-poster-2026.jpg\" alt=\"Wakaliwood presents Nyege Nyege XI action-packed festival poster: November 19–22, 2026 at Nyege Nyege Festival Grounds, Kalagala Falls, Uganda\"><figcaption>The supplied Nyege Nyege XI poster advertises November 19–22, 2026 at Kalagala Falls, Uganda, with the theme “The festival becomes the movie set.” Artwork supplied to 7H Music Blog.</figcaption></figure> <p>Uganda's flagship festival returns to Adrift Overland Camp at Kalagala Falls, near Jinja, for its 11th edition on <strong>November 19-22</strong>: more than 200 artists across seven stages, four days and three nights on the Nile. Tickets went live in September at a launch held at the Talent Africa rooftop in Kampala, where organisers confirmed the riverside site as the festival's permanent home and set out a wider programme around the music — film, fashion, visual art, theatre, comedy and food.</p> <h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The Wakaliwood Edition</h3> <p>This year's theme is a collaboration with <strong>Wakaliwood</strong>, the DIY action-film studio from Wakaliga that has spent two decades turning Kampala suburbs into action epics. Parts of the festival site will become a live film set, with scenes shot across the four days — a first dedicated film component, and a natural pairing for two Ugandan projects that built international audiences from fiercely independent roots.</p> <h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">What is back, and what is new</h3> <p>The Reggae Stage and the Boiler Room both return, alongside dedicated Kenyan and Rwandan takeover showcases and the SO UG Stage powered by Uganda Waragi, which keeps its focus on contemporary Ugandan popular culture. Names announced for the main stage so far include <strong>King Saha</strong>, <strong>Jackie Chandiru</strong>, <strong>Linus</strong>, <strong>Abeeka Band</strong>, <strong>Etania</strong>, <strong>DJ Akio</strong> of Strictly Soul and the Zonke Africa DJ Collective. Uganda Waragi's maker, Uganda Breweries Limited, renewed its partnership on a three-year agreement, and a Ministry of Tourism tie-in under the Explore Uganda brand links the festival to regional travel packages and circuit destinations.</p> <p>In the weeks before that, warm-up parties travel to Nairobi and Kigali, hosted by Sheila Gashumba and Kenya's Azeezah Hashim.</p> <div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">Nyege Nyege 2026</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">When</span><span class=\"v\">November 19-22, 2026</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Where</span><span class=\"v\">Adrift Overland Camp, Kalagala Falls, Jinja</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Scale</span><span class=\"v\">200+ artists · 7 stages · riverside camping for ~1,000</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Early bird</span><span class=\"v\">UGX 100,000 \"Die Hard\" pass, ended September 30</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Tickets</span><span class=\"v\">festival.nyegenyege.com — official sellers MTN MoMo and Tag Ticketing</span></li></ul></div> <p>It is worth remembering what the festival is worth where it happens: a government report released in 2025 put the 2024 edition's contribution to Jinja's economy at about <strong>USD 1.84 million (UGX 6.62 billion)</strong>. With camping expanded and tourism packages attached, the 2026 edition is being built to push that further.</p> <p class=\"note\">Check the official site for current ticket tiers and the full line-up — names are still being announced. <em>Credit: Nyege Nyege Festival; SoftPower News; Daily Monitor; Travel PR News; RioTimes.</em></p>","imgPos":"center 43%"},
     { id: 28, slug: 'joshua-baraka-tiwa-savage-commando', category: 'News', title: 'Joshua Baraka pulls "Commando" forward — the Tiwa Savage link-up lands this month', excerpt: 'The Recess II track he teased with Tiwa Savage is coming in October instead of with the EP, after a reception he calls "crazy" — and it is the clearest sign yet of Uganda-to-Lagos traffic.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '4 min read', img: COVER('7905cb89733adb4f0b18236f'), imgCaption: 'The official "Recess II" cover art — "Commando" sits at number two on the EP', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '3sjmAVaeka80SCvK69bedW', title: 'Joshua Baraka — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/joshua-baraka/1526660844',
       body: '<p class="lead">The biggest Ugandan record of the season may have just moved up a month.</p> <p>Joshua Baraka has been teasing "Commando", his collaboration with Nigerian star <strong>Tiwa Savage</strong>, and on October 11 he told fans it is arriving sooner than planned. "The reception on Commando is crazy. I\'m going to release it in October instead of November with the project," he posted on X. "Thank you Natives for always giving me the confidence when I walk into a room anywhere in the world." The teaser itself — captioned "ARE YOU READY?🔥..COMMANDO 🇺🇬X🇳🇬 B\'raka X Tiwa" — has since travelled across Ugandan and Nigerian music pages, and the song sits at number two on <em>Recess II</em>, the EP whose Spotify pre-save countdown still points to <strong>November 12</strong>.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Why it matters</h3> <p>Tiwa Savage is one of the defining voices of Afrobeats\' global decade, with a catalogue and a following far beyond Lagos. For Baraka the link-up extends a run that already includes the JAE5-produced "Wrong Places" (past 29 million Spotify plays on our last check) and a verse on South African amapiano producer Al Xapo\'s "SNOKONOKO VI" earlier this year. Uganda-to-Lagos traffic of this kind is still rarer than it should be, which makes the timing louder than the song.</p> <p>Two of the EP\'s other tracks, "YoYo" and "what do i know", are already out, and the homecoming <strong>B\'RAKA LAND</strong> concert at Lugogo Hockey Grounds falls sixteen days after the EP\'s November 12 date (see <a href="#article/joshua-baraka-recess-ii-braka-land">the Recess II story</a>).</p> <p class="note">No release date for the single has been confirmed beyond Baraka\'s own post — watch his channels. <em>Credit: X/@itsJoshuaBaraka; Sqoop; RN0nline; Patro Uganda; Spotify.</em></p>' },
     { id: 29, slug: 'kampala-live-season-chameleone-levixone-mesach', category: 'Events', title: 'Kampala\'s live season: Chameleone, Levixone, Mesach Semakula and a calendar that runs to December', excerpt: 'Anniversary shows, gospel nights and comeback concerts — plus jazz and comedy — as Uganda\'s promoters stack the last quarter of 2026.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '5 min read', img: 'images/articles/kampala-live-season.jpg', imgCaption: 'A packed Kampala concert night (illustration: 7H Music Blog)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '6nTXL0DkmSqjvcKjn6hCUz', title: 'Jose Chameleone — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/jose-chameleone/318588116',
-      body: '<p class="lead">The last quarter of 2026 is the busiest Uganda\'s live circuit has looked in years.</p> <p>Start with October: the <strong>Kampala Jazz & Soul Benefit</strong> takes over the UMA Multi-Purpose Hall at the Show Grounds on <strong>October 24</strong>, doors at 5pm through midnight — one of the city\'s few dedicated jazz-and-soul nights this season. Around it, the month has already delivered Edwizzy Fest, AFROBEEZA\'s Afrobeats and Afro-house night at Acacia Mall, and a silent-disco series in Kimbeja, while ObaFest turned Gulu into a two-day entertainment hub up north.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>November: gospel, kadongo kamu, and the Nile</h3> <p><strong>Levixone</strong> brings A One Tym Experience to the Kampala Serena Hotel on <strong>November 8</strong>; <strong>Mesach Semakula</strong> celebrates 50 Years of Greatness at Kingdom Kampala on <strong>November 13</strong>; and <strong>Rachael Magoola</strong>\'s 60th-anniversary concert lands on <strong>November 27</strong>. And on <strong>November 14</strong> the industry itself convenes: the AUMEX Conference brings the business side of the music to Kampala (<a href="#article/aumex-artist-accelerator-conference">covered here</a>). Between those dates sits the one every calendar is built around — Nyege Nyege at Kalagala Falls, November 19-22 (<a href="#article/nyege-nyege-2026-wakaliwood-edition-preview">previewed here</a>) — with the African Electronic Music Conference running in Jinja from the 17th.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>December: the comeback shows</h3> <p><strong>Jose Chameleone</strong> returns to the Cricket Oval at Lugogo on <strong>December 4</strong> for "Unlimited" — and he has said publicly that he invited Presidents Samia Suluhu Hassan of Tanzania and Évariste Ndayishimiye of Burundi to be in the house. A week later <strong>Nina Roz</strong> headlines the Lugogo Hockey Grounds (December 11). Then the dance calendar takes over: <a href="#article/pare-pare-rave-uncle-waffles-kampala-2026">Pare Pare Rave</a> brings Uncle Waffles to Millennium Grounds on December 18; <a href="#article/spinny-and-friends-festival-kololo-2026">Spinny and Friends Festival</a> lands at Kololo Airstrip on December 19; and Afro Netion\'s Wave Edition runs at Motiv UG that same day.</p> <div class="factbox"><p class="factbox-title">On the calendar</p><ul><li><span class="k">Oct 24</span><span class="v">Kampala Jazz & Soul Benefit — UMA Show Grounds</span></li><li><span class="k">Nov 8</span><span class="v">Levixone — Kampala Serena Hotel</span></li><li><span class="k">Nov 13</span><span class="v">Mesach Semakula: 50 Years of Greatness — Kingdom Kampala</span></li><li><span class="k">Nov 14</span><span class="v">AUMEX Conference — Kampala</span></li><li><span class="k">Nov 19-22</span><span class="v">Nyege Nyege Festival — Kalagala Falls, Jinja</span></li><li><span class="k">Dec 4</span><span class="v">Chameleone Unlimited — Cricket Oval, Lugogo</span></li><li><span class="k">Dec 11</span><span class="v">Nina Roz — Lugogo Hockey Grounds</span></li><li><span class="k">Dec 18</span><span class="v">Pare Pare Rave: Uncle Waffles — Millennium Grounds, Lugogo</span></li><li><span class="k">Dec 19</span><span class="v">Spinny and Friends Festival — Kololo Airstrip</span></li><li><span class="k">Dec 19</span><span class="v">Afro Netion: Wave Edition — Motiv UG</span></li></ul></div> <p>There is a second, less comfortable story inside this calendar. Promoter and TV host Douglas Lwanga said flatly this month that "Uganda\'s entertainment industry is no longer profitable"; dancehall artist Kapeke apologised after walking off the Roast & Rhyme stage citing sound problems; Tracy Melon cut a Mbarara set short after a fan\'s behaviour. More shows do not automatically mean better-paid artists — a tension the industry\'s own numbers back up.</p> <p class="note">Dates and venues are taken from organiser listings (evento.ug, UGEvent, AllEvents); always confirm with the promoter before travelling. <em>Credit: evento.ug; UGEvent; AllEvents; RioTimes; Sqoop; Patro Uganda.</em></p>' },
-    {"id":30,"slug":"blankets-and-wine-2026-season","category":"Events","title":"Blankets & Wine Kampala: December 13 at Lugogo","excerpt":"The Kampala save-the-date is here: Blankets & Wine returns to Millennium Grounds, Lugogo on December 13, 2026, with early-bird tickets advertised at 100K.","date":"Oct 16, 2026","readTime":"2 min read","img":"images/articles/blankets-and-wine-kampala-poster.jpg","imgCaption":"Blankets & Wine Kampala save-the-date poster — supplied by Mike 1ne","author":{"name":"7H Music Blog","role":"Editorial","avatar":"images/brand/7h-avatar.svg"},"body":"<p class=\"lead\">Blankets &amp; Wine has a December date for Kampala. The supplied save-the-date poster announces <strong>13 December 2026</strong> at <strong>Millennium Grounds, Lugogo</strong>, with early-bird tickets advertised at <strong>100K</strong>.</p><p>For anyone planning their end-of-year live-music calendar, those are the details to put in the diary. The artwork identifies the event as <strong>a Chama Nation production</strong> and directs ticket buyers to <a href=\"https://www.blanketsandwine.com/\">www.blanketsandwine.com</a>.</p><h3>The Kampala details</h3><div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">Blankets &amp; Wine — save the date</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Date</span><span class=\"v\">13 December 2026</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Venue</span><span class=\"v\">Millennium Grounds, Lugogo, Kampala</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Early bird</span><span class=\"v\">100K, as advertised on the poster</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Production</span><span class=\"v\">Chama Nation</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Tickets</span><span class=\"v\"><a href=\"https://www.blanketsandwine.com/\">blanketsandwine.com</a></span></li></ul></div><h3>What is still to come</h3><p>This is a save-the-date announcement, not a line-up reveal. The poster does not name performers or give gate-opening times, a full ticket breakdown or age restrictions. We will leave those details open rather than attach a bill from another edition. Terms and conditions apply, as stated on the artwork; check the ticket listing before buying.</p><figure><img src=\"images/articles/blankets-and-wine-kampala-poster.jpg\" alt=\"Blankets and Wine poster announcing 13 December 2026, early bird 100K, Millennium Grounds Lugogo, and tickets at www.blanketsandwine.com\"><figcaption>The supplied Kampala poster: December 13 at Lugogo, a Chama Nation production.</figcaption></figure><h3>Kampala, not Nairobi</h3><p>The date above belongs to the <strong>Kampala edition</strong>. It should not be confused with the separate Nairobi event listed for December 20 in our regional calendar. Keep the city and venue in view when planning travel or choosing tickets.</p><p class=\"note\">Source: the Blankets &amp; Wine save-the-date poster supplied to 7H Music Blog by Mike 1ne. This update replaces the previous Nairobi-focused article while preserving its original link. Event details reflect the poster; ticket availability has not been independently checked.</p>","imgPos":"center 40%"},
