a fan\'s behaviour. More shows do not automatically mean better-paid artists — a tension the industry\'s own numbers back up.</p> <p class="note">Dates and venues are taken from organiser listings (evento.ug, UGEvent, AllEvents); always confirm with the promoter before travelling. <em>Credit: evento.ug; UGEvent; AllEvents; RioTimes; Sqoop; Patro Uganda.</em></p>' },
     { id: 31, slug: 'east-africa-streaming-2026-gen-z-amapiano', category: 'Industry Insights', title: 'East Africa\'s streaming wave: Gen Z, amapiano and the end of one national sound', excerpt: 'Spotify\'s Nairobi numbers put 18-24s behind 53.7% of streams while Bongo Flava, gengetone, gospel and amapiano all grow double digits — and the region\'s borders keep dissolving.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '5 min read', img: 'images/articles/east-africa-streaming-wave.jpg', imgCaption: 'A DJ set in East Africa (illustration: 7H Music Blog)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '1bNjWBFWsAAzZSR59lRdpR', title: 'Kabza De Small — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/kabza-de-small/1021730955',
-      body: '<p class="lead">The most interesting thing about East African listening in 2026 is that no single genre owns it any more.</p> <p>Spotify\'s internal data for Nairobi, published in June, put listeners aged 18-24 behind <strong>53.7%</strong> of all streams in the Kenyan capital — and then showed what those young listeners do with the app: Bongo Flava grew 75%, gengetone 48%, gospel 37%, amapiano 34%, R&B 28% and Afrobeats 25%. Not one national sound, but a shuffle between them.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Amapiano stopped being South African</h3> <p>The clearest regional story is what East Africa did with amapiano after importing it. Tanzanian producers slowed the log drums under Swahili hooks; Kenyan artists pushed Sheng punchlines into the pocket; Ugandan Afro-pop acts used the rhythm as a bridge back to big choruses. South Africa\'s Scorpion Kings headlined Blankets & Wine\'s December 2025 edition in Nairobi, Tanzanian star Marioo shared the same stage, and Ugandan promoters booked Boohle for a Kampala party — the traffic runs both ways. Producers are crossing borders too: Al Xapo\'s "SNOKONOKO VI" with <a href="#article/joshua-baraka-recess-ii-braka-land">Joshua Baraka</a> took a South African amapiano blueprint and gave it an East African vocal.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The gatekeepers changed</h3> <p>Radio still matters — the Atlas data puts radio listeners at more than double the live-event attendance of non-listeners (<a href="#article/uganda-music-economy-atlas-report-2026">the numbers are here</a>) — but the first filter for a new song is now a playlist, a TikTok clip or a DJ set. Spotify has leaned into that with Culture Club, its editor-led podcast on African music, recorded live at the company\'s Greasy Tunes sessions in Nairobi in July with Kenyan producer Eric Musyoka; its episodes cover Nigerian hip-hop, South Africa\'s Lekompo movement, East Africa\'s new wave and the role of women in amapiano.</p> <p>For artists the upside is reach without permission: a Kampala act can build a Nairobi audience without a Kenyan label or a radio plug. The catch is monetisation, and it is severe — Uganda\'s streaming penetration sits near 1.1% against a regional average of five to ten per cent, so the audiences arriving on the phone are still worth a fraction of what they are elsewhere.</p> <p class="note">Growth figures are Spotify\'s June 2026 Nairobi data as reported in August 2026. <em>Credit: Spotify (Culture Club / Greasy Tunes coverage); OkayAfrica; Vumbi Ventures; Music In Africa.</em></p>' },
+      body: '<p class="lead">The most interesting thing about East African listening in 2026 is that no single genre owns it any more.</p> <p>Spotify\'s internal data for Nairobi, published in June, put listeners aged 18-24 behind <strong>53.7%</strong> of all streams in the Kenyan capital — and then showed what those young listeners do with the app: Bongo Flava grew 75%, gengetone 48%, gospel 37%, amapiano 34%, R&B 28% and Afrobeats 25%. Not one national sound, but a shuffle between them.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Amapiano stopped being South African</h3> <p>The clearest regional story is what East Africa did with amapiano after importing it. Tanzanian producers slowed the log drums under Swahili hooks; Kenyan artists pushed Sheng punchlines into the pocket; Ugandan Afro-pop acts used the rhythm as a bridge back to big choruses. Amapiano acts now tour East Africa as a matter of course, and Ugandan promoters booked Boohle for a Kampala party — the traffic runs both ways. Producers are crossing borders too: Al Xapo\'s "SNOKONOKO VI" with <a href="#article/joshua-baraka-recess-ii-braka-land">Joshua Baraka</a> took a South African amapiano blueprint and gave it an East African vocal.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The gatekeepers changed</h3> <p>Radio still matters — the Atlas data puts radio listeners at more than double the live-event attendance of non-listeners (<a href="#article/uganda-music-economy-atlas-report-2026">the numbers are here</a>) — but the first filter for a new song is now a playlist, a TikTok clip or a DJ set. Spotify has leaned into that with Culture Club, its editor-led podcast on African music, recorded live at the company\'s Greasy Tunes sessions in Nairobi in July with Kenyan producer Eric Musyoka; its episodes cover Nigerian hip-hop, South Africa\'s Lekompo movement, East Africa\'s new wave and the role of women in amapiano.</p> <p>For artists the upside is reach without permission: a Kampala act can build a Nairobi audience without a Kenyan label or a radio plug. The catch is monetisation, and it is severe — Uganda\'s streaming penetration sits near 1.1% against a regional average of five to ten per cent, so the audiences arriving on the phone are still worth a fraction of what they are elsewhere.</p> <p class="note">Growth figures are Spotify\'s June 2026 Nairobi data as reported in August 2026. <em>Credit: Spotify (Culture Club / Greasy Tunes coverage); OkayAfrica; Vumbi Ventures; Music In Africa.</em></p>' },
     { id: 32, slug: 'uganda-music-economy-atlas-report-2026', category: 'Industry Insights', title: 'The numbers behind Uganda\'s music economy — and why independence is the default', excerpt: 'A national report puts 89.7% of Ugandan artists outside any label, 27% earning nothing, and streaming penetration at 1.1%. The picture is tough — and it explains almost everything about how the scene behaves.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: 'images/articles/uganda-music-economy.jpg', imgCaption: 'The business end of Ugandan music (illustration: 7H Music Blog)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '3sjmAVaeka80SCvK69bedW', title: 'Joshua Baraka — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/joshua-baraka/1526660844',
-      body: '<p class="lead">A single report explains almost everything about how Uganda\'s music scene behaves.</p> <p>The Atlas report, published in January 2026, is best known for naming Joshua Baraka\'s "Wrong Places" the country\'s top song of 2025 — Fik Fameica and Liam Voice\'s "Biggwa" followed at 11, Vinka, Nwagi and Ava Peace\'s "Batuleke" at 12, Elijah Kitaka\'s "Ekyange" at 13, with Azawi, Bebe Cool, Rema Namakula and Winnie Nwagi all inside the top 30. But the numbers around the chart are the more important part.</p> <div class="factbox"><p class="factbox-title">The industry in numbers</p><ul><li><span class="k">89.7%</span><span class="v">of Ugandan artists work independently — no label, no capital, no infrastructure</span></li><li><span class="k">27%</span><span class="v">earn nothing at all from their music</span></li><li><span class="k">1.1%</span><span class="v">streaming penetration in Uganda, against a regional average of 5-10%</span></li><li><span class="k">2.1×</span><span class="v">more live-show opportunities for artists who promote on radio</span></li><li><span class="k">66% vs 31%</span><span class="v">regular radio listeners who attend six or more events a year, versus non-listeners</span></li><li><span class="k">3:1</span><span class="v">women outperform men on brand deals — the only income path where they do</span></li></ul></div> <p>Read together, those figures describe a fast-moving, highly entrepreneurial scene with almost no infrastructure under it. Nine in ten artists are their own label, so every release is self-funded; radio, not streaming, is still the switch that turns a song into paid live work; and brand partnerships — where women out-earn men three to one — are the most reliable non-show income in the business.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The reform argument</h3> <p>The report landed in a year of public argument about the industry\'s foundations. In April, Uganda Communications Commission bans on certain songs reignited a debate about how — and whether — music should be regulated, with NTV following the fallout. Musician Elijah Kitaka called the scene "1500 years behind" and asked for copyright training rather than sympathy; Karole Kasita argued Uganda has failed to develop its own identifiable sound; Sheebah Karungi\'s copyright case with her former Team No Sleep camp went to court; and the Uganda National Musicians Federation\'s funding arguments spilled into public. Meanwhile Spice Diana, David Lutalo and others have spoken openly about what the economics actually feel like.</p> <p>For independents the report is less a verdict than a map: keep release costs low, treat live and brand work as the revenue, and own everything you can. It is the version of the industry most Ugandan artists are already living in.</p> <p class="note">Figures as reported by Pulse Uganda in January 2026 and NTV in April 2026. <em>Credit: Atlas report via Pulse Uganda; NTV Uganda; UgandaOnline; Howwe.</em></p>' },
+      body: '<p class="lead">A single report explains almost everything about how Uganda\'s music scene behaves.</p> <p>The Atlas report, published in January 2026, is best known for naming Joshua Baraka\'s "Wrong Places" the country\'s top song of 2025 — Fik Fameica and Liam Voice\'s "Biggwa" followed at 11, Vinka, Nwagi and Ava Peace\'s "Batuleke" at 12, Elijah Kitaka\'s "Ekyange" at 13, with Azawi, Bebe Cool, Rema Namakula and Winnie Nwagi all inside the top 30. But the numbers around the chart are the more important part.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"images/articles/kampala-jinja-road.jpg\" alt=\"Jinja Road in Kampala with boda boda riders, pedestrians and traffic\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>Jinja Road, Kampala. The Atlas report found that 89.7% of Ugandan artists work with no label behind them. <em>Photo: Thelifeofdaniel1994 / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0 (cropped)</em></figcaption></figure> <div class="factbox"><p class="factbox-title">The industry in numbers</p><ul><li><span class="k">89.7%</span><span class="v">of Ugandan artists work independently — no label, no capital, no infrastructure</span></li><li><span class="k">27%</span><span class="v">earn nothing at all from their music</span></li><li><span class="k">1.1%</span><span class="v">streaming penetration in Uganda, against a regional average of 5-10%</span></li><li><span class="k">2.1×</span><span class="v">more live-show opportunities for artists who promote on radio</span></li><li><span class="k">66% vs 31%</span><span class="v">regular radio listeners who attend six or more events a year, versus non-listeners</span></li><li><span class="k">3:1</span><span class="v">women outperform men on brand deals — the only income path where they do</span></li></ul></div> <p>Read together, those figures describe a fast-moving, highly entrepreneurial scene with almost no infrastructure under it. Nine in ten artists are their own label, so every release is self-funded; radio, not streaming, is still the switch that turns a song into paid live work; and brand partnerships — where women out-earn men three to one — are the most reliable non-show income in the business.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The reform argument</h3> <p>The report landed in a year of public argument about the industry\'s foundations. In April, Uganda Communications Commission bans on certain songs reignited a debate about how — and whether — music should be regulated, with NTV following the fallout. Musician Elijah Kitaka called the scene "1500 years behind" and asked for copyright training rather than sympathy; Karole Kasita argued Uganda has failed to develop its own identifiable sound; Sheebah Karungi\'s copyright case with her former Team No Sleep camp went to court; and the Uganda National Musicians Federation\'s funding arguments spilled into public. Meanwhile Spice Diana, David Lutalo and others have spoken openly about what the economics actually feel like.</p> <p>For independents the report is less a verdict than a map: keep release costs low, treat live and brand work as the revenue, and own everything you can. It is the version of the industry most Ugandan artists are already living in.</p> <p class="note">Figures as reported by Pulse Uganda in January 2026 and NTV in April 2026. <em>Credit: Atlas report via Pulse Uganda; NTV Uganda; UgandaOnline; Howwe.</em></p>' },
     { id: 33, slug: 'african-music-global-october-2026', category: 'News', title: 'From the Grammys to October: African music\'s year on the world stage', excerpt: 'Tyla made category history in February, Fela Kuti received a posthumous Lifetime Achievement Award, and this month the world\'s release calendar carries South African jazz and a Ugandan singer trading hooks with Lagos.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '5 min read', img: 'images/articles/african-music-global-2026.jpg', imgCaption: 'Africa on the world stage (illustration: 7H Music Blog)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '3SozjO3Lat463tQICI9LcE', title: 'Tyla — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/us/artist/tyla/1552925732',
       body: '<p class="lead">African music\'s year on the world stage started with a historic Grammy night and has not slowed down since.</p> <p>At the <strong>68th Grammy Awards</strong> on February 1, South Africa\'s Tyla won Best African Music Performance for "Push 2 Start" — her second win in the category after "Water" in 2024, making her the first artist to take it twice, and the third straight year a woman has won it, following Tems\' "Love Me JeJe" in 2025. She beat a shortlist that included Burna Boy\'s "Love", Davido and Omah Lay\'s "With You", Ayra Starr and Wizkid\'s "Gimme Dat" — and Uganda\'s Eddy Kenzo, nominated with Mehran Matin for "Hope & Love". The night before, Fela Kuti was honoured with a posthumous Lifetime Achievement Award; Trevor Noah hosted the ceremony for the sixth and final time.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>What October looks like</h3> <p>The current month carries the same signature in a quieter key. Blue Note released <em>L I N D A</em>, the second label album from Durban saxophonist <strong>Linda Sikhakhane</strong> and his fifth overall, produced by Nduduzo Makhathini — spiritual jazz with township bones, and a reminder that Africa\'s global exports are not only pop. Pop-wise, the traffic keeps moving between Lagos, Johannesburg and East Africa, with our own <a href="#article/joshua-baraka-tiwa-savage-commando">Uganda-Nigeria collaboration of the season</a> still ahead.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The other half of the story</h3> <p>International recognition and domestic economics are not the same story. The same season that produced a Grammy win came with Ugandan data showing 27% of local artists earning nothing from music and streaming penetration stuck at 1.1% (<a href="#article/uganda-music-economy-atlas-report-2026">the numbers are here</a>). A nomination travels further than a royalty cheque. Both facts are true in 2026 — which is precisely why the next five years of African music will be fought over infrastructure, not awards.</p> <p class="note">Grammy details as reported in February 2026; <em>L I N D A</em> release dates per Blue Note / Universal listings. <em>Credit: OkayAfrica; Music In Africa; Guardian Nigeria; AllMusic; Premium Times.</em></p>' },
     { id: 34, slug: 'joka-sky-blue-to-no-condition', category: 'Interview', title: 'Spotlight: Joka, the Afro-pop shapeshifter behind "SU CASA"', excerpt: 'Six releases deep and still reworking his own sound, Joka has moved from the rap-adjacent records of 2022 to the Afro-pop of "SKY BLUE" and this year\'s "NO CONDITION" — always with his own name on the writing credit.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: 'images/artists/joka.jpg', imgPos: 'center 30%', imgCaption: 'Joka performing live in Kampala (photo: Instagram @joka_ug)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '7umpHJB8guUwgjaP2ZryKw', title: 'Joka — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/joka/1620053754',
       body: '<p class="lead">Joka has never stayed in one lane long enough for a listener to catch up.</p> <p>The Kampala Afro-pop singer and songwriter — <strong>Ntale Joram Emmanuel</strong> in the credits, and his name is on every one of them — has spent five years releasing small, deliberate projects at roughly one a year, each one a little further from the last. The lane keeps moving; the handwriting stays his. On Apple Music he is filed under Afro-Pop, but the catalogue runs from soft rap over drum programming to full Afro-beat production, and his best records tend to be the ones that sit between the two.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The run</h3> <p>It starts with <em>Louder Actions</em>, a five-track EP in May 2022, and a dense first year of singles: "Beamer" and "Outlander" with the producer and label owner <strong>GEOXwill</strong>, "Monster", "Lovesick" with Mxlan A, "Stay", "FEELS LIKE SUMMER" with Drillz the Rapper and, that October, "Andele" with Heimech. The following year he stretched out: "S K I N D E E P" in February, "Cigar" with Ledra in April, "Bandit" with Thy\'Medi and Chicor in September, "Differently" with Soundlykbb in November and "December" with GEOX the Producer and ItzDanny at the end of the year — all of it orbiting the ten-track <em>O U T S I D E</em> album he released in August 2023.</p> <p>Then he sharpened. <em>TO LOVE IS TO HATE</em> landed in March 2024 — seven tracks, with "NIGHTS IN RIYADH", "FADE" and "RUSH" among them — followed by <em>New Wave</em> in November and the six-track <em>SKY BLUE</em> at the end of that December, which gave him his three most-streamed songs: <strong>"GUZINAZONTO"</strong>, <strong>"MASAALI"</strong> and <strong>"TELL EM NO"</strong>. "I Miss You", his duet with <a href="#article/amanda-moon-afro-pop-singles">Amanda Moon</a>, came from the same sessions.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>This year: "NO CONDITION"</h3> <p>His newest release is a change of gear. <strong>"NO CONDITION"</strong>, out on April 24, 2026 through PALAGROOVE and RAIN.LABS, sounds like a reset: the drums sit further back, the voice is doing more of the work, and there is nothing on the record that a producer could not have built in a bedroom. It has been holding steady in the top five of his Apple Music listings since. Along the way he has also become a dependable guest — "SU CASA" with Ogezi and GEOXwill, "Deliver" with X-tmore te and Jam Vybz, "RED PEPPER" with DNK, and features on <a href="#article/soundlykbb-spotlight">Soundlykbb</a>\'s and GEOXwill\'s own projects. <strong>Update — Oct 16, 2026:</strong> the era has already moved again — his single <a href="#article/joka-ready-new-era"><em>READY</em></a> arrived on October 2 through Villaggio Media, Zonke Africa and Ria Entertainment, and opens the next chapter.</p> <div class="factbox"><p class="factbox-title">Joka, on record</p><ul><li><span class="k">Name</span><span class="v">Ntale Joram Emmanuel — Kampala, Uganda</span></li><li><span class="k">Projects</span><span class="v"><em>Louder Actions</em> 2022 · <em>O U T S I D E</em> 2023 · <em>TO LOVE IS TO HATE</em> · <em>New Wave</em> · <em>SKY BLUE</em> 2024</span></li><li><span class="k">Latest</span><span class="v">"READY" — October 2, 2026 (Villaggio Media / Zonke Africa / Ria Entertainment)</span></li><li><span class="k">Start with</span><span class="v">"GUZINAZONTO", "MASAALI", "TELL EM NO", "SU CASA"</span></li><li><span class="k">Frequent collaborators</span><span class="v">GEOXwill / GEOX the Producer, Ogezi, Thy\'Medi, DNK, Soundlykbb, Amanda Moon</span></li></ul></div> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The "Munakampala" line</h3> <p>The one piece of self-description he has given a platform is a sentence in his Apple Music biography about "Munakampala", the song he calls the record he always wanted to write — "from a place of confusion, anxiety and of course love." That is a fair reading of everything else too: songs about the city, written by somebody who is honest about the fact that the city is complicated.</p> <p>The catalogue is split across platforms. Spotify carries his collaborations and part of the run — "I Miss You" with Amanda Moon, "KWATA" with Thy\'Medi and GEOXwill, "Racks" with Baru, "Differently" with Soundlykbb, "ZERO" with Tungi and the Senté remix of "NO CONDITION" — while the 2022-24 albums are still most complete on Apple Music and Deezer. Two other artists share his name on Spotify (a Polish rapper and a Dutch hardstyle project); the Ugandan Joka is the one with Amanda Moon and GEOXwill in the credits.</p> <p class="note">Fresh Find: he is on both Spotify and Apple Music as Joka — the player above runs his top tracks, and his newest single is in the Releases grid. <em>Credit: Apple Music; Deezer; MusicBrainz; Boomplay; Audiomack.</em></p>' },
     { id: 23, slug: 'nana-nyadia-afro-fusion-spotlight', category: 'Interview', title: 'Spotlight: Nana Nyadia, Afro-fusion from Kampala to Johannesburg', excerpt: 'A Nyege Nyege debut, an Artist Accelerator nod at AUMEX and a new independent chapter — the Afro-fusion and soul voice whose catalogue began with a 2022 heartbreak.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: PHOTO('ab7a444fa9b2900f9c54fea6'), imgPos: 'center 25%', imgCaption: 'Nana Nyadia (photo: Spotify artist profile)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '2SzY2nJnBs8mzh2jgqOmdL', title: 'Nana Nyadia — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/nana-nyadia/1766006884',
       body: '<p class="lead">Nana Nyadia writes about the space between two homes — and sings it like she is still standing in the middle of it.</p> <p>Born in Uganda with South Sudanese roots and raised between Kenya and Uganda, the singer and songwriter now based in Kampala works in Afro-fusion and soul: Afrobeats rhythm underneath a voice that carries more than the beat lets on. Her own description is the plainest one going — "Afro-Fusion &amp; Soul artist ... crafting culture-bridging stories" — and it is the most accurate summary anyone has written for her.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>It started with "Sad Song"</h3> <p>She has traced her way into music to a song called "Sad Song", written out of a 2022 heartbreak and the mental-health struggles that came with it. A 2024 Daily Monitor profile followed that thread: an artist who found the studio through the hardest year she had had, then a 23-year-old student of Graphics and Design at Isbat University. Her writing name is Nyadia Fiona, and the credits carry it — her own name on every release, alongside producer TK DICOCO on "Habibi".</p> <p>The records keep the mood. "STAND FOR" and its acoustic version arrived in September and October 2024, followed by "Habibi" that December. Then came "Mpenzi" in August 2025 and "Feeling" that November — both built with producer Melanin Boy — and "Not Promised" in May 2026 with Single Dee, a duet whose Shazam credits pair Nyadia Fiona with Deng James Aleu, and which South Sudanese "best of the year" playlists picked up within weeks.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Nyege Nyege, and the label years</h3> <p>She made her Nyege Nyege debut in 2024 — the festival that has become the scene\'s annual proving ground — and in December 2025 she was the guest on The Ugandan Boy Talk Show, where the conversation ran from her Ugandan, South Sudanese and Kenyan background to the debut EP she has been building, <em>Sour Valentine</em>. Nothing has been announced since, which leaves it filed firmly under "one to watch".</p> <p>Her singles have all landed through Party Wright Records — the Kampala label whose circle also includes <a href=\"#article/ayma-gone-ass-titude\">AYMA</a> and which reaches South Sudan through Himaya Music. That chapter has closed: she has left the label and now releases independently.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Johannesburg, June 2026</h3> <p>In June, New Vision reported that Nana Nyadia was part of the AUMEX Artist Accelerator delegation — the programme\'s second cohort — at the Africa Rising Music Conference in Johannesburg, alongside Mbeewo, Vini Mercy, <a href="#article/amanya-mosquitoes-alte-rnb">Amanya</a> and Matt Travers — a continental nod for a catalogue that is still only a handful of singles deep — at an expo whose 2025 showcase was headlined by <a href="#article/aumex-nasty-c-showcase-2025">Nasty C</a>.</p> <p>That is the story so far: a writer who kept choosing the softer, harder take, and a run of songs that sound like travel. Start with "Not Promised", then let "Feeling" run. <em>Credit: Daily Monitor; New Vision; The Ugandan Boy Talk Show; Audiomack; Shazam; Spotify; Apple Music.</em></p>' },
-    { id: 24, slug: 'tai-dai-whet-new-wave-spotlight', category: 'Interview', title: 'Spotlight: Tai Dai and the new-wave rap of "WHET"', excerpt: 'Rapper, singer, songwriter — Aseru Thandiwe Dralega has been on everyone else\'s record for three years. The five-track "WHET" EP is the moment the catalogue turned and pointed at her.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: PHOTO('933f78e29342953dc434151a'), imgPos: 'center 25%', imgCaption: 'Tai Dai (photo: Spotify artist profile)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '77G1P2NHQOnx0leyvpN3ym', title: 'Tai Dai — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/tai-dai/1426402603',
-      body: '<p class="lead">Uganda\'s new wave has a lot of names. Tai Dai has spent the last few years making sure hers is one you learn properly.</p> <p>Born Aseru Thandiwe Dralega, she works as a rapper, singer and songwriter — her given names turn up in the writing credits — and describes her sound as the place where hip-hop, alté, Afrobeats and UK garage meet. That is a broad claim until you hear the catalogue, which moves from Kampala rap to something closer to pop melody without ever changing voice.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The long apprenticeship</h3> <p>Her earliest credit is a guest spot on Lagum the Rapper\'s "UNA" (2020). "D.O.n (Pull up)" with Prnz and Ni\'dario followed in 2022, and then the collaborations stacked up: "Thursday" with Kaboo and <a href="#article/soundlykbb-spotlight">Soundlykbb</a> in January 2024 on Home Crowd Recordings, "Boy Scout" with mwami and Lagum the Rapper that June, and the five-track "EA OPEN" EP with Agent Mgumbe and mwami in September 2024. Along the way she has appeared on Axon and Soundlykbb\'s "Ni**as In Fashion" from <em>CARTEL BIZNESS</em>, Maya Amolo\'s "Last Time", Kavali King\'s "Pristine", Ninja C\'s "On The Road" and, in July 2026, Mxestro\'s "Okugwa mu Deep End".</p> <p>Then came "Outside Shoes" in May 2025 — the record she unpacked at length on The Ugandan Boy Talk Show\'s Boxing Day episode, in a conversation that ran from that song to Nyege Nyege and to the women holding down Ugandan hip-hop.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>WHET</h3> <p><em>WHET</em>, her five-track EP, arrived on July 23, 2026 under her own Tai Dai Official imprint, and it is the clearest statement yet: "GRANTED" opens it, "Uh Huh" — already out as a single in April — supplies the hook, "CHEST (Interlude)" breaks it in half, and "Where U Go" and "UNFORGETTABLE" close it out. Short records, sharp edges, no filler.</p> <p>What it adds up to is an artist the scene already trusts — the one who is on everybody\'s song before she is on her own cover. <em>WHET</em> is where that finally flips. <em>Credit: Apple Music; Spotify; Deezer; Genius; The Ugandan Boy Talk Show.</em></p>' },
+    { id: 24, slug: 'tai-dai-whet-new-wave-spotlight', category: 'Interview', title: 'Spotlight: Tai Dai and the new-wave rap of \"WHET\"', excerpt: 'Rapper, singer, songwriter — Aseru Thandiwe Dralega has been on everyone else\'s record for three years. The five-track \"WHET\" EP is the moment the catalogue turned and pointed at her. Here is the cut that could only exist on this EP, and the one already in circulation.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '7 min read', img: 'https://i.scdn.co/image/ab6761610000e5eb933f78e29342953dc434151a', imgPos: 'center 25%', imgCaption: 'Tai Dai (photo: Spotify artist profile)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: 'images/brand/7h-avatar.svg' }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '77G1P2NHQOnx0leyvpN3ym', title: 'Tai Dai — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/tai-dai/1426402603',
+      body: '<p class=\"lead\">Uganda\'s new wave has a lot of names. Tai Dai has spent the last few years making sure hers is one you learn properly.</p> <p>Born Aseru Thandiwe Dralega, she works as a rapper, singer and songwriter — her given names turn up in the writing credits — and describes her sound as the place where hip-hop, alté, Afrobeats and UK garage meet. That is a broad claim until you hear the catalogue, which moves from Kampala rap to something closer to pop melody without ever changing voice.</p> <h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The long apprenticeship</h3> <p>Her earliest credit is a guest spot on Lagum the Rapper\'s \"UNA\" (2020). \"D.O.n (Pull up)\" with Prnz and Ni\'dario followed in 2022, and then the collaborations stacked up: \"Thursday\" with Kaboo and <a href=\"#article/soundlykbb-spotlight\">Soundlykbb</a> in January 2024 on Home Crowd Recordings, \"Boy Scout\" with mwami and Lagum the Rapper that June, and the five-track \"EA OPEN\" EP with Agent Mgumbe and mwami in September 2024. Along the way she has appeared on Axon and Soundlykbb\'s \"Ni**as In Fashion\" from <em>CARTEL BIZNESS</em>, Maya Amolo\'s \"Last Time\", Kavali King\'s \"Pristine\", Ninja C\'s \"On The Road\" and, in July 2026, Mxestro\'s \"Okugwa mu Deep End\".</p> <p>Then came \"Outside Shoes\" in May 2025 — the record she unpacked at length on The Ugandan Boy Talk Show\'s Boxing Day episode, in a conversation that ran from that song to Nyege Nyege and to the women holding down Ugandan hip-hop.</p> <h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">WHET</h3> <p><em>WHET</em>, her five-track EP, arrived on July 23, 2026 under her own Tai Dai Official imprint, and it is the clearest statement yet: \"GRANTED\" opens it, \"Uh Huh\" — already out as a single in April — supplies the hook, \"CHEST (Interlude)\" breaks it in half, and \"Where U Go\" and \"UNFORGETTABLE\" close it out. Short records, sharp edges, no filler.</p> <h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The two songs to start with</h3>\n<p>Five tracks, 11 minutes. The EP does not have a filler problem, but it does have two songs doing different work.</p>\n<ul class=\"winners\"><li><span class=\"k\">The outlier<br>\"Where U Go\"</span><span class=\"v\">At 3:32 it is the longest cut on the EP by a full minute, the only track on the back half with a featured voice — MAUIMØON, the Ugandan singer-producer who was Apple Music\'s first Up Next East Africa artist in 2023 — and one of the three tracks here that never had a single\'s rollout. It is the deepest breath on a record built on short ones.</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Doing the rounds<br>\"Uh Huh\"</span><span class=\"v\">The one that arrived first: a single on 23 April, two months before the EP, and still the shortest full song here at 2:13. \"UNFORGETTABLE\" followed as a single in June, but \"Uh Huh\" has the longest runway — the record most likely to have reached you before the project did.</span></li></ul>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The next room</h3><p>On 13 December she joins the Blankets &amp; Wine bill at Millennium Grounds, Lugogo — the first festival slot since <em>WHET</em> where the crowd is there for the whole afternoon rather than one song. It is also the clearest signal yet that the EP did what it was meant to do: put her name on the poster, not just in the credits. <a href=\"#article/blankets-and-wine-2026-season\">Full line-up here</a>.</p><p>What it adds up to is an artist the scene already trusts — the one who is on everybody\'s song before she is on her own cover. <em>WHET</em> is where that finally flips. <em>Credit: Apple Music; Spotify; Deezer; Genius; The Ugandan Boy Talk Show.</em></p>' },
     { id: 25, slug: 'ayma-gone-ass-titude', category: 'News', title: 'AYMA links with Soundlykbb on "GONE" — then drops "ASS TITUDE"', excerpt: 'The Party Wright Records singer has had a full 2026: "GONE" with Soundlykbb, a run of features across Kampala and Juba, and a brand-new solo single out today.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '4 min read', img: APPLE_ART('Music221/v4/da/b4/e9/dab4e9df-e203-92da-84e9-85daf7383703/712132199933_cover.jpg'), imgCaption: 'The official "ASS TITUDE" cover art — Party Wright Records / Himaya Music', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '45p8xCFhR0gkXgzyWW0Wos', title: 'AYMA — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/ayma/1818880233',
       body: '<p class="lead">AYMA keeps good company — and this year the company has kept getting bigger.</p> <p>In September 2026 the Kampala Afro-beat singer joined <a href="#article/soundlykbb-spotlight">Soundlykbb</a> on "GONE", a short, buoyant record that pairs Soundlykbb\'s Entebbe-rooted hook-making with AYMA\'s lighter, higher register. It has since settled at the top of his Spotify top-tracks list.</p> <p>Today he adds to it. "ASS TITUDE" is a solo single released through Party Wright Records under exclusive licence to Himaya Music — the Kampala-to-Juba axis that also carried <a href=\"#article/nana-nyadia-afro-fusion-spotlight\">Nana Nyadia</a> before she left Party Wright to go independent — and it follows June\'s "MAPENZI" in a run that has quietly become one of the steadier ones in that label family.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The run behind it</h3> <p>AYMA arrived in 2025 with "FOUND", an R&amp;B-leaning debut on his own AYMA MUSIC imprint, then "Go Down" that August and "Only" that October. The features widened the frame: Tojo\'s "Fall In Love" and Party Wright\'s "Intentional Love" in 2026, plus VTM Records\' "Over" and "MELODIES" and E-FROST\'s "Nyege Nyege" with ZEUS BNM in October.</p> <p>He is still early — a few thousand monthly listeners, no album, no press run — which is exactly the point of a Fresh Find: get in before the roll-out. <em>Credit: Spotify; Apple Music; Party Wright Records; Himaya Music.</em></p>' },
     { id: 26, slug: 'amanda-moon-afro-pop-singles', category: 'Interview', title: 'Spotlight: Amanda Moon, the Afro-pop name letting the singles speak', excerpt: '"Mandem", "Ready for You", "I Miss You" with Joka, "No More Tears" with Ric Keys — a small, self-released Afro-pop catalogue with no press kit and hooks that keep travelling.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '4 min read', img: PHOTO('82dfa55279af253d03303c21'), imgPos: 'center 25%', imgCaption: 'Amanda Moon (photo: Spotify artist profile)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '5lqRfQ6FpBufPWHpXy8HeO', title: 'Amanda Moon — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/amanda-moon/1746973428',
