       body: '<p class="lead">It\'s official: Joshua Baraka\'s new EP, <em>Recess II</em>, arrives on <strong>November 12, 2026</strong>. Spotify has opened pre-saves with a live countdown. Sixteen days later, the Kawempe-born star plays the biggest homecoming show of his career.</p> <p>Spotify\'s tracklist preview shows seven songs on the sequel to 2024\'s <em>Recess</em>. Both of his 2026 solo singles are there, with "YoYo" at track 3 and "what do i know" at track 5. The other titles are still hidden, but Baraka has revealed the biggest one himself. On October 11 he teased "Commando", a collaboration with Nigerian Afrobeats star <strong>Tiwa Savage</strong>. Pulse Uganda and Sqoop report it as the EP\'s second track. There is no standalone release date for the song yet.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The road to Recess II</h3> <p>"what do i know", his first solo single of 2026, landed on July 1. "YoYo" followed on August 26, a song about two people stuck between friendship and something more. He sings it in English, Luganda, French and Lingala. Baraka wrote and produced it himself, with extra production from Hendrix Sam, and it has already passed 1.1 million Spotify plays. Both singles are released under his own name, under exclusive licence to Moves Recordings.</p> <p>Before that came a run of collaborations. In February he released a "Morocco" remix with Jamaican dancehall star Shenseea and Axon. In April came "AYAYAAH" with Rwanda\'s Element EleéeH and Kenya\'s Bien, and in June "YOU" with Khid Ceejay. His back catalogue keeps growing too. The JAE5-produced "Wrong Places" has passed 29.5 million plays on Spotify, and "No Bad Vibes", his collaboration with Jazzy, Gyakie and Kilimanjaro, has passed 235 million. He now draws about 1.6 million monthly listeners.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>"It\'s East Africa"</h3> <p>Baraka has always thought regionally. "From the start, I didn\'t want to just stay in Uganda," he told Joey Akan on OkayAfrica\'s <em>Afrobeats Intelligence</em> in May. He remembered taking buses from Kampala to Kenya and Rwanda to push his music. "When I talk of East Africa, I talk about it in a block, because to me, it\'s not just one place. It\'s not just Uganda… it\'s East Africa." He was blunt about the comfort trap at home, too: "If you blow up in Uganda, they don\'t really try to go out there and get together with other artists or take their music out there. You\'ll still make money, you still have deals, you\'ll still be good."</p> <p>That approach has taken him to the Afro Nation stage and sold-out shows in Europe and Australia. It also earned him a nomination for Best African Music Act at the 2026 MOBO Awards.</p> <figure><img src="images/articles/joshua-baraka-mbale.jpg" alt="Joshua Baraka performing at Bandali Lounge in Mbale" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>On the Brakaland EA Tour: Baraka at Bandali Lounge, Mbale. Photo: Nile Post.</figcaption></figure> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Brakaland on the road</h3> <p>Before the EP, he took the show on the road. The Brakaland EA Tour is his first nationwide roadshow. It is backed by Johnnie Walker, which recently named him its "Icon Walker". The tour visited Gulu, Arua, Mbale and Mbarara. At Bandali Lounge in Mbale he opened with "What Do I Know" and closed with "YoYo". "I think if they are looking for his number one fan across the world, I would come in first," one reveller told New Vision. At Nkore Place in Mbarara he came on at about 1am and played "Nana", "YoYo", "Dive In", "This Time", "Wrong Places", "Dreams", "Morocco" and "Dalilah". "There is no song of his that I don\'t know or stream," one fan told Sqoop.</p> <p>"Thank you, Mbarara, for showing up and showing me so much love," Baraka said that night. "We\'ve taken Brakaland across Uganda, and now it\'s time to bring it home. Kampala, we\'re coming for you. I\'ll see you at the Hockey Grounds on November 28 for the grand finale."</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The finale: Lugogo, November 28</h3> <div class="factbox"><p class="factbox-title">B\'RAKA LAND East Africa</p><ul><li><span class="k">When</span><span class="v">Saturday, November 28, 2026. Gates open at 12pm</span></li><li><span class="k">Where</span><span class="v">Lugogo Hockey Grounds, Kampala</span></li><li><span class="k">Tickets</span><span class="v">UGX 75,000 general admission · UGX 120,000 VIP · UGX 2,000,000 per table, at <a href="https://brakaland.ug/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">brakaland.ug</a></span></li></ul></div> <p>The official site promises "one full day in the heart of Kampala". DJs, food and friends start at noon, and the main show begins at sundown. It\'s a big step up from a year ago. On November 29, 2025, Baraka played his first-ever concert, <em>Into The Braka Land</em>, at the Ndere Cultural Centre, presented by Talent Africa Group. This year the name returns on a festival-sized ground, after a full tour and with a new EP to sing.</p> <blockquote>"This year has tested me in more ways than one. There have been a lot of ups and downs, but I\'m grateful for it all. You have showed me more love than I deserve through everything, and all I can do is give you music and shows. I love you all so much. Uganda, get ready. B\'raka is coming home."<cite>Joshua Baraka</cite></blockquote> <p>Pre-save <em>Recess II</em> on <a href="https://open.spotify.com/prerelease/1LxUYn2jTWDyBlDmyKPcvs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spotify</a>, and learn the words before the 28th. <em>Credit: Spotify; brakaland.ug; Pulse Uganda; Sqoop; New Vision; Nile Post; PML Daily; MBU; OkayAfrica (Afrobeats Intelligence); Album Talks; Talent Africa Group; Apple Music.</em></p>' },
     { id: 20, slug: 'ug-hip-hop-awards-2025-winners-2026', category: 'News', title: 'UG Hip Hop Awards: every 2025 winner, and how to enter #UGHIPHOPAWARDS26', excerpt: 'Pryce Teeba and Recho Rey took the top rapper prizes, the Soundlykbb-featuring "Ni**as in Fashion" won Song of the Year and The Mith won Album of the Year. Here are all the winners, the stories behind them, and how to enter the next edition.', date: 'Oct 14, 2026', readTime: '7 min read', img: 'images/articles/ughha-2025-launch.jpg', imgCaption: 'the UG Hip Hop Awards \'25 press launch (photo: Nile Post)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '7LZeZZQTTiqOuxCN30dlGX', title: 'Pryce Teeba — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/pryce-teeba/1129257218',
       body: '<p class="lead">Ugandan rap has its own night of the year. The UG Hip Hop Awards (UGHHA) set out to give "a fair platform for all Ugandan Hip-hop acts", celebrating "the past, present and the future of the genre in Uganda." The official site has already switched its banner to <strong>#UGHIPHOPAWARDS26</strong>.</p> <p>The last ceremony was held on Friday, December 5, 2025, at the International University of East Africa (IUEA) auditorium in Kampala. Old-school pioneers and new-wave rappers filled the room. Bond 7 whisky was lead sponsor, with Pepsi and NRG Radio as partners, and every adult with an ordinary ticket got a free 200ml bottle at the door. "The Uganda Hip Hop Awards speaks directly to the nation\'s creative energy," said Hillary Baguma, UBL\'s brand manager for mainstream spirits. Hip-hop veterans Babaluku, Navio and Lyrical G attended the launch, where project manager Nathan Mubembe said the awards keep the genre\'s "continued flow into the mainstream."</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Every 2025 winner</h3> <p class="note">The official site lists these as the 9th-edition winners. Some press coverage of the night called it the 8th.</p> <ul class="winners"><li><span class="k">Male Rapper of the Year</span><span class="v"><a href="#article/pryce-teeba-hip-hop-afrobeats">Pryce Teeba</a></span></li><li><span class="k">Female Rapper of the Year</span><span class="v">Recho Rey</span></li><li><span class="k">Song of the Year</span><span class="v">Axon &amp; Denim Cartel, "Ni**as in Fashion" ft. <a href="#article/soundlykbb-spotlight">Soundlykbb</a> &amp; Tai Dai</span></li><li><span class="k">Album of the Year</span><span class="v">The Mith, <em>The Ugandan 2</em></span></li><li><span class="k">Mixtape of the Year</span><span class="v">Timcence, <em>No Skips Allowed</em></span></li><li><span class="k">Rap Fusion of the Year</span><span class="v">Timcence, "Can Ayeba"</span></li><li><span class="k">Collaboration of the Year</span><span class="v">Eno Hip Hop Cypher (Eno Hip Hop / Konkrete Music, ft. Ruyonga, Navio, Keko, Kvan, Lagum, J-Wats, Da Agent, Vkaycee, Afraw, The Homie &amp; AJ Stylz)</span></li><li><span class="k">Sweet 16 (Verse of the Year)</span><span class="v">Navio, <em>Eno Hip Hop Cypher</em></span></li><li><span class="k">Lyricist of the Year</span><span class="v">Tucker HD, "Just Left Ntinda"</span></li><li><span class="k">Producer of the Year</span><span class="v">Rukidi Beatz, "Power"</span></li><li><span class="k">Rookie of the Year</span><span class="v">Son Of Aboda, "Ayee"</span></li><li><span class="k">Video of the Year</span><span class="v">Kavali ft. UNITY, "YA SALAWO"</span></li><li><span class="k">Inspirational Song of the Year</span><span class="v">Barna ft. Karungi, "Victory"</span></li><li><span class="k">Central Rapper of the Year</span><span class="v">Vudu Child, "Wanjaulo" ft. Drop Pin, Lany X &amp; OG Stash</span></li><li><span class="k">Eastern Rapper of the Year</span><span class="v">TIZ MC OMUBEDE, "Fallen Souls Mu Silimu Freestyle"</span></li><li><span class="k">Northern Rapper of the Year</span><span class="v">Stayput, "Jo Kare Ni" ft. Toobi Smolz</span></li><li><span class="k">Western Rapper of the Year</span><span class="v">Truth256, "Party"</span></li><li><span class="k">Diaspora Act of the Year</span><span class="v">J Baller UG, "Don Corleone (Remix)"</span></li><li><span class="k">Deejay of the Year</span><span class="v">DJ Ata</span></li><li><span class="k">Media Personality of the Year</span><span class="v">NUVEYLIVE</span></li><li><span class="k">Lifetime Achievement Award</span><span class="v">Jeff Ekongot</span></li></ul> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The storylines</h3> <p><strong>Pryce Teeba\'s year.</strong> The <a href="#article/pryce-teeba-hip-hop-afrobeats">Pyong Recordz founder</a> won Male Rapper of the Year, the title Navio held in 2024. His 14-track album <em>Marinate</em> followed the same month. <strong>Recho Rey, full circle.</strong> Rachael Mirembe first broke through at the 2017 UG Hip Hop Awards as the only woman in the MTN cypher competition. She went on to win Female Rapper and Rookie of the Year in the MTN era, and in 2025 she was named Female Rapper of the Year again.</p> <p><strong>Doubles.</strong> Timcence took both Mixtape and Rap Fusion of the Year. Konkrete Music\'s Eno Hip Hop Cypher won Collaboration of the Year, and Navio\'s verse on it won the Sweet 16. <strong>The Mith keeps winning.</strong> After Lyricist and Mixtape of the Year in 2024, he took Album of the Year for <em>The Ugandan 2</em>. NUVEYLIVE kept Media Personality of the Year. The night\'s emotional high point was a standing ovation for Jeff (Geoffrey) Ekongot as he received the Lifetime Achievement Award.</p> <p><strong>The 7H connection.</strong> Song of the Year went to "Ni**as in Fashion", Axon and Denim Cartel\'s record featuring Fresh Find <a href="#article/soundlykbb-spotlight">Soundlykbb</a> and Tai Dai. Tai Dai also guests on "Temptation" by <a href="#article/sente-afro-fusion-storyteller">Senté</a>, whose debut single "DND" earned him two UGHHA nominations, for Rap Fusion and Rookie of the Year.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Rewind: 2024</h3> <p>The previous edition named Navio Male Rapper and Ninja C Female Rapper of the Year. Ruyonga\'s "Parte Yani" won both Song and Rap Fusion of the Year, and Rickman Manrick\'s <em>NdiMuto</em> took Album of the Year. Trappex Booming was Producer of the Year, Nsokwa was Rookie of the Year, and MC Yallah received the Lifetime Achievement Award.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>How to enter in 2026</h3> <p>No date or nominee list for the 2026 edition (the 10th, by the official site\'s count) has been published yet. The process is public, though. The organisers announce the nomination window on their social media, and it stays open for one month. Artists, labels and managers submit through <a href="https://www.ughha.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ughha.com</a> or a shared link, and the work must have been released within the eligibility period they publish. Nominees are announced afterwards, and the ceremony is held in Kampala and broadcast live on social media and TV.</p> <p>Follow <a href="https://www.instagram.com/officialughha/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@officialughha</a> so you don\'t miss the call for entries. Questions go to <a href="mailto:officialughha@gmail.com">officialughha@gmail.com</a>. <em>Credit: ughha.com; Pulse Uganda; The Independent; SoftPower News; allAfrica; Nile Post; MBU; HipHopAfrican; MTN Uganda.</em></p>' },
-    { id: 19, slug: 'echoled-kush-after-hour', category: 'Interview', title: 'Echoled on "Kush After Hour": a late-night EP that shows his range', excerpt: 'The singer behind "Mangadalena" wrote all six songs on his second EP, moving between dancehall, Afro-R&B and Afro-pop. He has also been named a festival headliner and added Joshua Baraka\'s former manager to his team.', date: 'Oct 14, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: COVER('3c4907db885382316b378d39'), imgCaption: 'The official "Kush After Hour" cover art', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '4Mg8jCywOiQ4qDE7Xz419U', title: 'Kush After Hour — Echoled' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/album/kush-after-hour-ep/6800323697',
-      body: '<p class="lead">In just over a month, Echoled released a new EP, was named a festival headliner and got a management upgrade. Ibunbaazi Ssentongo has made himself one of the busiest names in Ugandan music.</p> <figure><img src="images/artists/echoled.jpg" alt="Echoled in a dark denim outfit, seated against a light studio background"><figcaption>Echoled — artist portrait supplied to 7H Music Blog.</figcaption></figure> <p>His music draws on Afrobeat, dancehall, R&amp;B and Afro drill. Nile Post describes the blend as Afro-R&amp;B and dancehall with a touch of urban <em>kadongo kamu</em>, the storytelling tradition of Ugandan guitar music. The audience is growing fast. He now has more than 40,000 monthly listeners on Spotify, where "Mangadalena" leads his catalogue with over 150,000 plays, ahead of "New Love", "Embilizi" and "Home Alone". "Mangadalena", "Broken Hearts" (released on Valentine\'s Day 2026) and "Embilizi" are all getting heavy airplay.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Inside Kush After Hour</h3> <p>Released on August 28, <em>Kush After Hour</em> follows his 2025 EP <em>It Speaks</em>. Echoled told Nile Post that the idea came straight from conversations with his listeners. He noticed that many fans connected most with his music during their "Kush moments", so he set out to make a body of work for that time. He describes the result as sensational, soothing and energetic, and a move beyond conventional dancehall.</p> <p>He wrote all six songs himself. Aiz Fix My Mix and Jacob Beats produced the EP, building the beats from scratch. The variety is deliberate. The title track blends R&amp;B and dancehall, "Sitimila" goes Afro-R&amp;B and "Embilizi" leans Afro-pop, with "Dirty Veronica", "Kinte" and "Angelica" completing the six. People have tended to tie him to the Afro-pop feel of "Mangadalena", he said, and this project is his chance to show other sides of his artistry while staying an East African artist at heart.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Headliner, with a new team</h3> <p>The Afro Migration Festival picked him to headline its 2026 edition, three days of African music, art and culture at Nile Nest Resort in Jinja over the Independence weekend (October 9–11). "Echoled represents the new wave of Ugandan music — authentic, energetic and deeply connected to the culture," the festival team said when they announced him.</p> <p>Behind the scenes, Lobstar has joined his management team. He is the former manager of <a href="#article/joshua-baraka-recess-ii-braka-land">Joshua Baraka</a> and widely credited with a hand in the "YoYo" singer\'s success. Lobstar will work alongside Echoled\'s parent management company, MVP Management. Echoled released a steady stream of singles before the EP: "One Night Stand" (December 2025), then "Broken Hearts", "Kintu" and "Home Alone" in 2026. The momentum is real.</p> <p>Start with "Mangadalena", then let <em>Kush After Hour</em> play through, preferably late. <em>Credit: Nile Post; allAfrica; MBU; Spotify; Apple Music.</em></p>' },
+    { id: 19, slug: 'echoled-kush-after-hour', category: 'Interview', title: 'Echoled on \"Kush After Hour\": a late-night EP that shows his range', excerpt: 'The singer behind \"Mangadalena\" wrote all six songs on his second EP, moving between dancehall, Afro-R&B and Afro-pop. Here is the track that could only be his, and the one already doing the rounds.', date: 'Oct 14, 2026', readTime: '7 min read', img: 'https://i.scdn.co/image/ab67616d0000b2733c4907db885382316b378d39', imgCaption: 'The official \"Kush After Hour\" cover art', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: 'images/brand/7h-avatar.svg' }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '4Mg8jCywOiQ4qDE7Xz419U', title: 'Kush After Hour — Echoled' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/album/kush-after-hour-ep/6800323697',
+      body: '<p class=\"lead\">In just over a month, Echoled released a new EP, was named a festival headliner and got a management upgrade. Ibunbaazi Ssentongo has made himself one of the busiest names in Ugandan music.</p> <figure><img src=\"images/artists/echoled.jpg\" alt=\"Echoled in a dark denim outfit, seated against a light studio background\"><figcaption>Echoled — artist portrait supplied to 7H Music Blog.</figcaption></figure> <p>His music draws on Afrobeat, dancehall, R&amp;B and Afro drill. Nile Post describes the blend as Afro-R&amp;B and dancehall with a touch of urban <em>kadongo kamu</em>, the storytelling tradition of Ugandan guitar music. The audience is growing fast. He now has more than 40,000 monthly listeners on Spotify, where \"Mangadalena\" leads his catalogue with over 150,000 plays, ahead of \"New Love\", \"Embilizi\" and \"Home Alone\". \"Mangadalena\", \"Broken Hearts\" (released on Valentine\'s Day 2026) and \"Embilizi\" are all getting heavy airplay.</p> <h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Inside Kush After Hour</h3> <p>Released on August 28, <em>Kush After Hour</em> follows his 2025 EP <em>It Speaks</em>. Echoled told Nile Post that the idea came straight from conversations with his listeners. He noticed that many fans connected most with his music during their \"Kush moments\", so he set out to make a body of work for that time. He describes the result as sensational, soothing and energetic, and a move beyond conventional dancehall.</p> <p>He wrote all six songs himself. Aiz Fix My Mix and Jacob Beats produced the EP, building the beats from scratch. The variety is deliberate. The title track blends R&amp;B and dancehall, \"Sitimila\" goes Afro-R&amp;B and \"Embilizi\" leans Afro-pop, with \"Dirty Veronica\", \"Kinte\" and \"Angelica\" completing the six. People have tended to tie him to the Afro-pop feel of \"Mangadalena\", he said, and this project is his chance to show other sides of his artistry while staying an East African artist at heart.</p> <div class=\"factbox\"><p class=\"factbox-title\">Kush After Hour at a glance</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Released</span><span class=\"v\">28 August 2026 · 6 tracks, 16 minutes</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Written by</span><span class=\"v\">Ssentongo Ibunbaazi (Echoled)</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Produced by</span><span class=\"v\">Aiz Fix My Mix · Jacob Beats</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Label</span><span class=\"v\">℗ 2026 Echoled, on Apple Music</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Management</span><span class=\"v\">MVP Management, with Lobstar</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Best first listen</span><span class=\"v\">\"Dirty Veronica\" for range · \"Embilizi\" for the record already in circulation</span></li></ul></div>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">The two songs to start with</h3>\n<p>Six tracks in 16 minutes does not leave room for a skipped song, but it does leave room for two different jobs. One track here could only have come from Echoled; another has already been doing the rounds.</p>\n<ul class=\"winners\"><li><span class=\"k\">The outlier<br>\"Dirty Veronica\"</span><span class=\"v\">At 3:26 it is the longest song on an EP where nothing else passes 2:42, and it sits at track four, between the Afro-R&amp;B of \"Sitimila\" and the Afro-pop of \"Embilizi\" — the hinge of the record. If the EP\'s claim is range, this is the song that has to prove it.</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Doing the rounds<br>\"Embilizi\"</span><span class=\"v\">The one with the video — Apple lists a music video for it — and the one MBU singled out, alongside \"Mangadalena\" and \"Broken Hearts\", for heavy airplay. At 2:35 it is one of the shortest tracks here, which helps explain why it is the one you are most likely to meet first outside the record.</span></li></ul>\n<h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">Headliner, with a new team</h3> <p>The Afro Migration Festival picked him to headline its 2026 edition, three days of African music, art and culture at Nile Nest Resort in Jinja over the Independence weekend (October 9–11). \"Echoled represents the new wave of Ugandan music — authentic, energetic and deeply connected to the culture,\" the festival team said when they announced him.</p> <p>Behind the scenes, Lobstar has joined his management team. He is the former manager of <a href=\"#article/joshua-baraka-recess-ii-braka-land\">Joshua Baraka</a> and widely credited with a hand in the \"YoYo\" singer\'s success. Lobstar will work alongside Echoled\'s parent management company, MVP Management. Echoled released a steady stream of singles before the EP: \"One Night Stand\" (December 2025), then \"Broken Hearts\", \"Kintu\" and \"Home Alone\" in 2026. The momentum is real.</p> <p>Start with \"Mangadalena\", then let <em>Kush After Hour</em> play through, preferably late. <em>Credit: Nile Post; allAfrica; MBU; Spotify; Apple Music.</em></p>' },
     { id: 18, slug: 'sente-afro-fusion-storyteller', category: 'Interview', title: 'Spotlight: Senté, Kampala\'s Afro-fusion storyteller', excerpt: 'A Spotify Fresh Finds Africa pick, a two-time UG Hip Hop Awards nominee and, in Deeds Magazine\'s words, "at the fore" of East Africa\'s alt-R&B rise. Meet the voice behind "Let It Play" and "Teasin\'".', date: 'Oct 13, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: PHOTO('dd7fc0b970e14650b8db45f4'), imgPos: 'center 30%', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '28f5KCRxUeYsOEegevfKrj', title: 'Teasin\' — Senté' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/album/teasin-ep/1823792749',
       body: '<p class="lead">"With Alt R&amp;B fashioned as the \'it\' genre in East Africa currently, Senté is at the fore of its ascent." Deeds Magazine wrote that when it named the Kampala artist one of ten East African creative innovators to know.</p> <p>Senté calls himself an Afro-fusion artist. His own bio describes music that blends "hip-hop lyricism, RnB-inspired melodies and afro-centric rhythms". Deeds praises his "velvety melodies" and "cinematic narrative-writing" at the meeting point of R&amp;B, hip-hop and Afro-pop. Spotify files him under Alté and Afro-soul, and his own playlists sign off as "Man Like Senté".</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>A debut that got noticed</h3> <p>His first single, "DND" with Denesi and Chxf Barry (December 2023), earned two UG Hip Hop Awards nominations, for Rap Fusion and Rookie of the Year. Two months later came "Let It Play", a cross-border duet with Kenya\'s Maya Amolo produced by Yannique and Shress. With more than 135,000 Spotify plays, it is still his most-played song. "Kampala Woman" and his debut EP <em>You Wasted Kampala Love</em> followed in March and April 2024. In May that year, Spotify selected him for Fresh Finds Africa. BBC 1Xtra, Deeds Magazine, Tangaza Magazine and COLORSxSTUDIOS have all featured his music.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Teasin\' and the 2025 run</h3> <p>He built toward his second EP with a single every couple of months: "Twista" in March 2025, "Temptation" with Tai Dai in April and "Typo" with Azooora in June. <em>Teasin\'</em>, a five-track EP released with Platoon, followed on July 23, 2025.</p> <p>He\'s also one of the new wave\'s most dependable guests. You\'ll find him on <a href="#article/soundlykbb-spotlight">Soundlykbb</a>\'s "CASINO", Irxnzi\'s "Sun Don\'t Shine" and Geinixx\'s "COCO". He also appears on "DINNER" with La Soülchyld, kalibwani and Joka, and on "Somebody" from <a href="#article/mike-mungu-new-project">Mike Mungu</a>\'s <em>LOVE ISNT ENOUGH</em>. Spotify\'s "fans also like" list puts him next to Chxf Barry, Baru and <a href="#article/tungi-bebe-to-life-of-sound">Tungi</a>, the heart of Kampala\'s new wave.</p> <p>Start with "Let It Play", then go straight into <em>Teasin\'</em>. He\'s on Instagram at <a href="https://www.instagram.com/sente4ever/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@sente4ever</a>. <em>Credit: Spotify; Deeds Magazine; Apple Music; Shazam.</em></p>' },
     { id: 17, slug: 'amanya-mosquitoes-alte-rnb', category: 'Interview', title: 'Spotlight: Amanya, the Kampala bedroom producer behind "MOSQUITOES"', excerpt: 'Her voice moves through Alté, R&B, pop and Afro-house. With the "Buri Izooba" EP alongside Mozaka and DJ Spinny, plus "MOSQUITOES" and its new Tipcey remix, Amanya is one of Kampala\'s quiet new finds.', date: 'Oct 13, 2026', readTime: '5 min read', img: PHOTO('d16877d11a3a292e6f40a7cd'), imgPos: 'center 30%', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '7CudWV8gnBMZx9v3wfDjnS', title: 'MOSQUITOES — Amanya' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/album/mosquitoes-single/1885660665',
@@ -259,7 +426,7 @@
     { id: 8, slug: 'beyond-7h-ugandas-new-wave', category: 'Interview', title: 'Beyond 7H: Uganda\'s new wave of independent voices', excerpt: 'A round-up of the artists, producers and songwriters shaping Kampala\'s sound outside the major-label system.', date: 'Sep 14, 2026', readTime: '7 min read', img: 'images/articles/new-wave-collage.jpg', imgCaption: 'Pryce Teeba, Kyle Simbwa, Mike Mungu and Soundlykbb', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '77G1P2NHQOnx0leyvpN3ym', title: 'Tai Dai — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/tai-dai/1426402603',
       body: '<p class="lead">Kampala\'s music scene has never been louder — or more independent.</p><p>From Afro-pop and hip-hop to R&B and amapiano, a new generation of artists is building audiences on their own terms, often with nothing more than a home studio and a SoundCloud link. This blog exists to cover all of them — 7H artists and the wider scene alike.</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>Fresh Finds</h3><p>Alongside 7H\'s Baranga, Mr Fallback, Mike 1ne and Smokie Cee, keep an eye on Fresh Finds like <a href="#article/sente-afro-fusion-storyteller">Senté</a>, <a href="#article/amanya-mosquitoes-alte-rnb">Amanya</a>, <a href="#article/echoled-kush-after-hour">Echoled</a>, <a href="#article/pryce-teeba-hip-hop-afrobeats">Pryce Teeba</a>, <a href="#article/kyle-simbwa-apple-music-up-next">Kyle Simbwa</a>, <a href="#article/mike-mungu-new-project">Mike Mungu</a>, <a href="#article/soundlykbb-spotlight">Soundlykbb</a>, <a href="#article/ali-boomaye-new-single">Ali Bomaye</a> and <a href="#article/remz-down-new-wave-spotlight">Remz</a> — the emerging voices rising out of Ntinda, Entebbe and beyond. Got a story or a release we should cover? Reach out at <a href="mailto:7hmusicgroupug@gmail.com">7hmusicgroupug@gmail.com</a>. <em>Credit: 7H Music Blog editorial.</em></p>' },
     { id: 9, slug: 'kyle-simbwa-apple-music-up-next', category: 'Interview', title: 'Spotlight: Kyle Simbwa — Apple Music\'s Up Next artist for East Africa', excerpt: 'The Ugandan vocalist, producer and multi-instrumentalist is celebrated for blending 90s R&B, 80s synth-pop and Afrobeats into a distinctive Alté sound.', date: 'Oct 11, 2026', readTime: '7 min read', img: 'images/articles/kyle-simbwa-up-next.jpg', imgPos: 'center 30%', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '5YA97S9AzbC0pbTaeX3X8W', title: 'East African Summer — Kyle Simbwa' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/album/east-african-summer/6797513622',
-      body: '<p class="lead">Kyle Simbwa has been named Apple Music\'s Up Next artist for East Africa — a major milestone for Ugandan and East African music on the global stage.</p><p>The 27-year-old from Kasangati, a town near Kampala, is celebrated for blending \'90s R&B, \'80s synth-pop, A.O.R. and Disney TV influences with Afrobeats and Hip-Hop, creating a distinctive Afrobeat/Alté sound. "Being selected for Up Next East Africa by Apple Music means a lot because I\'m part of a generation of African artists building something uniquely ours," Simbwa said. "I want people to hear my music, feel where I\'m from, and see how far it can travel."</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>Hands-On Craft</h3><p>Deeply involved in every stage of his music, Simbwa works across songwriting, production, recording, mixing and engineering, giving him the freedom to experiment. His growing live presence includes headline performances at Xpressions and appearances at Afro Urban alongside Mike Kayihura and Kohen Jaycee. His 2025 debut project <em>Person</em> and singles like "Mwana Gwe" and "East African Girl" established him as a rising force, and his seven-track EP <em>East African Summer</em> (2026) explores love, nightlife and youth culture. In January he lent his voice to <a href="#article/remz-down-new-wave-spotlight">Remz</a> on "Do You Wrong" — one of the year\'s quietest and best link-ups.</p><p>"2026 is about taking everything I\'ve been building and pushing it further," he said. <em>Credit: Nyakundi Report; KBC; Global Publishers; Shazam. Apple Music Up Next East Africa.</em></p>' },
+      body: '<p class="lead">Kyle Simbwa has been named Apple Music\'s Up Next artist for East Africa — a major milestone for Ugandan and East African music on the global stage.</p><p>The 27-year-old from Kasangati, a town near Kampala, is celebrated for blending \'90s R&B, \'80s synth-pop, A.O.R. and Disney TV influences with Afrobeats and Hip-Hop, creating a distinctive Afrobeat/Alté sound. "Being selected for Up Next East Africa by Apple Music means a lot because I\'m part of a generation of African artists building something uniquely ours," Simbwa said. "I want people to hear my music, feel where I\'m from, and see how far it can travel."</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>Hands-On Craft</h3><p>Deeply involved in every stage of his music, Simbwa works across songwriting, production, recording, mixing and engineering, giving him the freedom to experiment. His growing live presence includes headline performances at Xpressions and appearances at Afro Urban alongside Mike Kayihura and Kohen Jaycee. His 2025 debut project <em>Person</em> and singles like "Mwana Gwe" and "East African Girl" established him as a rising force, and his seven-track EP <em>East African Summer</em> (2026) explores love, nightlife and youth culture. In January he lent his voice to <a href="#article/remz-down-new-wave-spotlight">Remz</a> on "Do You Wrong" — one of the year\'s quietest and best link-ups.</p><h3 class=\"font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold\">From Up Next to the festival bill</h3><p>On 13 December Simbwa joins the Blankets &amp; Wine bill at Millennium Grounds, Lugogo, alongside BNXN, King Saha, Tai Dai, Aziz Azion, Tojo and Sean Offixial. It is the first major Kampala festival booking since the Up Next nod, and the logical next step for an artist whose catalogue is built on hooks rather than features. <a href=\"#article/blankets-and-wine-2026-season\">Full line-up here</a>.</p><p>"2026 is about taking everything I\'ve been building and pushing it further," he said. <em>Credit: Nyakundi Report; KBC; Global Publishers; Shazam. Apple Music Up Next East Africa.</em></p>' },
     { id: 10, slug: 'tungi-bebe-to-life-of-sound', category: 'Interview', title: 'Tungi: from "Bebe" to "Life of Sound" — a Kampala Afrobeat story', excerpt: 'Mutungi Keith, the Ugandan singer-songwriter blending Afrobeat, hip-hop and R&B, and his journey from a 2020 debut single to a growing catalogue.', date: 'Oct 6, 2026', readTime: '7 min read', img: PHOTO('a2aa524a55ea76758747e1c0'), imgPos: 'center 30%', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '4ppzhpslWW0PcByjJBLaKt', title: 'Life of Sound — Tungi' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/album/life-of-sound/1778800460',
       body: '<p class="lead">Tungi — real name Mutungi Keith — is a Ugandan singer and songwriter born and raised in Uganda, emerging as a prominent figure in the Afrobeat, hip-hop and R&B scenes since the early 2020s.</p><p>His entry into music was shaped by childhood in Uganda, where music played a central role; early memories of singing for peers ignited his passion. His debut single "Bebe" (August 28, 2020) marked his first foray into Afrobeat and urban sounds, followed by the <em>Pearl of Africa</em> EP series (2022–2023) and <em>Life of Sound</em> (2024). Influenced by Wizkid, J Hus and others, Tungi blends Afrobeat rhythms with hip-hop and R&B, exploring love, self-discovery and Ugandan urban life.</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>Collaborations & Reach</h3><p>He has collaborated with Joshua Karuhanga on "Bandale" and with Mike 1ne on <a href="#article/tungi-mike-1ne-siteesa">"Siteesa"</a> (2026), and appears on Kyle Simbwa\'s <em>East African Summer</em> ("Sugar Honey"). His catalogue spans EPs like <em>Rush</em>, <em>Free Time</em> and singles such as "Wonder", "G Energy" and "Mademoiselle". "My dream is for my music to travel worldwide," he says. <em>Credit: The Culturing; Grokipedia; Apple Music; Deezer.</em></p>' },
     { id: 11, slug: 'tungi-mike-1ne-siteesa', category: 'Press Release', title: 'Tungi & Mike 1ne link up on "Siteesa"', excerpt: 'The new collaboration blends Afro-pop bounce with Mike 1ne\'s in-house production touch.', date: 'Oct 6, 2026', readTime: '3 min read', img: BASE + 'cover-siteesa.jpg', imgCaption: 'The official "Siteesa" cover art', author: { name: '7H Music Group', role: 'Press Office', avatar: BASE + 'artist-mike-1ne.jpg' }, listen: { type: 'track', id: '4GDB9tMylfRQavVoI6UOkq', title: 'Siteesa — Tungi & Mike 1ne' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/us/album/siteesa/1888289462?i=1888289464',
